window.TEACHER={"built":"25 October 2026","machines":{"fish":{"id":"fish","title":"The fishing machine","href":"m/fish.html","desc":"A rock in the Hauraki Gulf: speed from a cast, the rod as a lever (moments), why a float floats (density), why the fish isn't where it looks (refraction), and mean vs median on the day's catch.","blurb":"Casts, levers, floating, refraction and mean vs median off a rock in the Hauraki Gulf.","tags":["Year 9","Year 10","Science","Maths"],"built":true,"eyebrow":"Science and maths · Forces, density, light and statistics","crumb":"Year 9 and 10 Science (forces, density, light) and Maths (rates, statistics)","badge":{"label":"Forces, density, light and data · Years 9–11"},"papers":[{"label":"NZQA: Physics, Earth and Space Science","url":"https://www2.nzqa.govt.nz/ncea/subjects/past-exams-and-exemplars/physics-earth-and-space-science/"},{"label":"NZQA: Mathematics and Statistics","url":"https://www2.nzqa.govt.nz/ncea/subjects/past-exams-and-exemplars/"},{"label":"Fisheries NZ: fishing rules","url":"https://www.mpi.govt.nz/fishing-aquaculture/recreational-fishing/fishing-rules/"}],"truth":"The cast ignores air resistance and the height of the rod tip, and heights are squashed to fit the screen. The rod is held level and treated as weightless. Densities are typical values; sea water is taken as 1.025 g/cm³ and its refractive index as 1.33. Snapper lengths are random samples, not real catch data. Minimum sizes and daily limits vary by area and change: check the current rules with Fisheries New Zealand before you fish.","footer":"Covers Year 9–10 forces and motion (speed, gravity, moments), matter (density, floating), light (refraction) and statistics (mean, median, range, skew). Leads into NCEA Level 1 science and maths.","mainLabel":"A person fishing from a rock: a cast over the water, a rod as a lever, a float and sinker, a fish under the surface and a measuring board","kupu":[{"mi":"tere","en":"speed","ta":7953,"note":"","chip":["avg"],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"cast"},{"mi":"tōpana","en":"force","ta":40375,"note":"","chip":["effort"],"evidence":"Te Reo Pūtaiao (MoE 2009) cited in entry","stepsNote":"lever"},{"mi":"taumaha","en":"weight","ta":7718,"note":"","chip":["weight"],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"lever"},{"mi":"papatipu","en":"mass","ta":5179,"note":"","chip":["mass"],"evidence":"Te Reo Pūtaiao (MoE 2009) cited in entry","stepsNote":"float"},{"mi":"rōrahi","en":"volume","ta":40040,"note":"","chip":["vol"],"evidence":"Te Reo Pūtaiao (MoE 2009) cited in entry","stepsNote":"float"},{"mi":"kiato","en":"density","ta":45567,"note":"","chip":["rho"],"evidence":"Te Reo Pūtaiao (MoE 2009) cited in entry","stepsNote":"float"},{"mi":"mānu","en":"to float","ta":3595,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"float"},{"mi":"totohu","en":"to sink","ta":8537,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"float"}],"steps":[{"k":"cast","lab":"The cast","cap":"Cast the sinker out over the water. Change the angle and how fast it leaves the rod tip, then watch where it lands and how long it takes.","exam":"The sinker travelled 31 m across the water in 2.1 s, so its average horizontal speed was 31 ÷ 2.1 ≈ 15 m/s. Gravity pulls it down the whole time, which is why its path curves. Ignoring air, the longest cast is at 45°.","trap":"Using the launch speed as the average speed. The sinker slows going up and speeds up coming down, so divide the total distance by the total time.","big":"The big idea: average speed = distance ÷ time. Without air, a cast goes furthest at 45°: steeper wastes speed going up, flatter lands too soon.","kupu":["tere"]},{"k":"lever","lab":"The rod is a lever","cap":"Hold a fish on the end of the rod. Your back hand is the pivot, your front hand pushes up, and the fish pulls the tip down. Change the rod, your grip and the fish.","exam":"The fish's weight is W = mg = 2 × 9.8 = 19.6 N acting 2.4 m from the pivot, a moment of 47 N m. My front hand is 0.5 m from the pivot, so it must push with 47 ÷ 0.5 = 94 N to balance it. The rod trades force for distance: the tip moves 4.8 times further than my hand.","trap":"Using the mass (kg) instead of the weight (N). Moments use forces, so multiply the mass by 9.8 first.","big":"The big idea: a moment is a turning effect: force × distance from the pivot. When it balances, clockwise moments = anticlockwise moments.","kupu":["tōpana","taumaha"]},{"k":"float","lab":"Float or sink","cap":"A foam float holds up a lead sinker. Add sinker weight and watch the float ride lower, until the whole rig is denser than sea water and sinks.","exam":"Density = mass ÷ volume. Lead is 11.3 g/cm³, much denser than sea water (1.025 g/cm³), so a sinker alone sinks. The foam float is only 0.05 g/cm³. Together, the float and sinker have an average density below 1.025 g/cm³, so they float. Add too much lead and the average density goes above the water's, and the rig sinks.","trap":"Thinking heavy things always sink. A ship is very heavy but floats, because its average density (including the air inside) is less than water.","big":"The big idea: density = mass ÷ volume. An object floats when its average density is less than the water's. Upthrust equals the weight of the water pushed aside.","kupu":["kiato","papatipu","rōrahi","mānu","totohu"]},{"k":"light","lab":"Where's the fish?","cap":"Look into the water and the fish seems closer to the surface than it really is. Move your eye and change the depth to see why.","exam":"Light from the fish travels from water into air. It speeds up and bends away from the normal at the surface. My brain traces the light back in a straight line, so the fish appears higher than it really is. Looking straight down, a fish 2.0 m deep looks about 2.0 ÷ 1.33 = 1.5 m deep.","trap":"Saying light bends towards the normal as it leaves the water. Into a faster material (air) it bends away; into a slower one (water, glass) it bends towards.","big":"The big idea: refraction. Light changes speed when it crosses from water into air, so it changes direction. Your brain assumes light travels in straight lines.","kupu":[]},{"k":"stats","lab":"The day's catch","cap":"Twenty snapper on the measuring board. The pink line is 30 cm, the minimum size in much of the upper North Island. Catch more samples and compare the mean and the median.","exam":"To find the median of 20 lengths, put them in order and take the mean of the 10th and 11th values. To find the mean, add all 20 lengths and divide by 20. If a few large fish pull the mean above the median, the data is skewed to the right, so the median is the better 'typical' length.","trap":"Finding the middle of the list without ordering it first. Always sort the values, smallest to largest.","big":"The big idea: the mean adds up and shares out; the median is the middle value. A few very big or very small fish pull the mean but barely move the median.","kupu":[]},{"k":"test","lab":"Test yourself","cap":"Four challenges. Use the controls, then press Check. The picture follows the last control you used.","exam":"","trap":"","big":"Exam tip: write the formula, put the numbers in with units, then say what it means: \"so the rig floats\".","kupu":[]}],"challenges":[{"q":"Land a cast exactly 40 m out (within 1 m).","check":"()=>Math.abs(range()-40)<1","ok":"()=>`Yes: ${fmt(range(),1)} m at ${s.ang}° and ${fmt(s.v,1)} m/s, in ${fmt(flight(),2)} s.`","no":"()=>`Not yet: ${fmt(range(),1)} m. Try 45° first, then change the speed.`","okIsFn":true,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"Find the heaviest whole-gram sinker that still lets the float float.","check":"()=>floats()&&s.sink===Math.floor((FLOAT_V*RHO_SEA-FLOAT_M)/(1-RHO_SEA/RHO_LEAD))","ok":"()=>`Yes: ${s.sink} g. The rig's average density is ${fmt(avgRho(),3)} g/cm³, just under sea water's 1.025.`","no":"()=>floats()?`It floats with ${s.sink} g, but could you add more?`:`At ${s.sink} g the average density is ${fmt(avgRho(),3)} g/cm³, more than 1.025, so it sinks.`","okIsFn":true,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"Looking straight down, set the real depth so the fish looks 1.5 m deep.","check":"()=>Math.abs(appStraight()-1.5)<0.03","ok":"()=>`Yes: ${fmt(s.depth,2)} ÷ 1.33 = ${fmt(appStraight(),2)} m.`","no":"()=>`Not yet: ${fmt(s.depth,2)} ÷ 1.33 = ${fmt(appStraight(),2)} m. Real depth = apparent depth × 1.33.`","okIsFn":true,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"Catch samples until you get one where the median is 30 cm or more but the mean is under 30 cm.","check":"()=>{const S=stats();return S.med>=30&&S.mean<30}","ok":"()=>{const S=stats();return `Yes: median ${fmt(S.med,1)}, mean ${fmt(S.mean,1)}. A few small fish drag the mean down but not the median.`}","no":"()=>{const S=stats();return `This sample: median ${fmt(S.med,1)}, mean ${fmt(S.mean,1)}. Catch another and look for small fish pulling the mean down.`}","okIsFn":true,"noIsFn":true}],"controls":[{"type":"play","id":"play","label":""},{"type":"range","id":"ang","label":"Cast angle"},{"type":"range","id":"v","label":"Launch speed"},{"type":"range","id":"L","label":"Rod length"},{"type":"range","id":"d","label":"Hands apart"},{"type":"range","id":"m","label":"Fish"},{"type":"range","id":"sink","label":"Sinker"},{"type":"range","id":"depth","label":"Real depth"},{"type":"range","id":"eye","label":"Your eye, sideways from the fish"},{"type":"button","id":"catch","label":"🎣 Catch another 20"}],"shell":"kit"},"read":{"id":"read","title":"The reading machine","href":"m/read.html","desc":"Adaptive silent reading for Years 7–13: pick your worlds, read with a guided line-by-line window, a free timer or one-sentence focus, then answer explained questions with \"Prove it\" evidence while speed and level adapt to you.","blurb":"Adaptive silent reading with “Prove it” evidence questions that level up with you.","tags":["Year 7","Year 8","Year 9","Year 10","NCEA L1–L3","English","Reading"],"built":true,"shell":"own","kupu":[{"mi":"pānui","en":"to read","ta":5085,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"He Pātaka Kupu (Te Taura Whiri 2008) cited in entry","stepsNote":"(machine list only)"},{"mi":"tuhinga","en":"text, writing","ta":8648,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"(machine list only)"},{"mi":"kupu","en":"word","ta":3275,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"(machine list only)"},{"mi":"kupu matua","en":"keyword","ta":11923,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"(machine list only)"},{"mi":"whakarāpopoto","en":"to summarise; summary","ta":9800,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"(machine list only)"},{"mi":"kaituhi","en":"writer, author","ta":2031,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"(machine list only)"}],"steps":[],"challenges":[],"papers":[]},"words":{"id":"words","title":"The language features machine","href":"m/words.html","desc":"A word mixing desk: hear alliteration, assonance and onomatopoeia, overlap the two sides of a comparison, turn up intensity with faders, recolour a scene by tone, then build the exam sentence on a slot machine.","blurb":"A mixing desk for alliteration, assonance, comparison and tone.","tags":["Year 9","Year 10","English"],"built":true,"eyebrow":"English · Language features","crumb":"Year 9 and 10 English: reading unfamiliar texts and poems","badge":{"label":"English · Year 9","examText":"Tue 3 Nov","exam":"2026-11-03T09:00:00+13:00"},"papers":[{"label":"NZQA: English past exams","url":"https://www2.nzqa.govt.nz/ncea/subjects/past-exams-and-exemplars/"}],"truth":"Every line, passage and poem here is original, written for this machine, and simplified so one feature stands out at a time. Real texts mix features together, and a line can use more than one. The sounds are simple synthesised effects. Check your school's exam guidance for exactly what your teacher expects.","footer":"Covers Year 9–10 English: identifying and explaining language features (simile, metaphor, personification, alliteration, assonance, onomatopoeia, hyperbole, repetition, rhetorical questions, sensory imagery, emotive language and tone) in unfamiliar texts and poems. Leads into NCEA Level 1 English.","mainLabel":"A word mixing desk: words as pads, comparisons as overlapping circles, intensity faders, a scene that changes with tone, and an exam-sentence slot machine","kupu":[{"mi":"kupu","en":"word","ta":3275,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"tone"},{"mi":"kupu whakarite","en":"metaphor, simile, figurative language","ta":3286,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"compare"},{"mi":"orokati tārua","en":"alliteration","ta":46400,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"sound"},{"mi":"kupu ororite","en":"onomatopoeia","ta":46388,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"sound"},{"mi":"huarite","en":"rhyme","ta":46329,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"sound"},{"mi":"tukurua","en":"repetition","ta":8681,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"amp"},{"mi":"kīanga","en":"phrase","ta":2607,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"He Pātaka Kupu (Te Taura Whiri 2008) cited in entry","stepsNote":"explain"},{"mi":"rerenga kōrero","en":"sentence","ta":6727,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"explain"}],"steps":[{"k":"sound","lab":"Hear the words","cap":"Each word is a pad on the desk. Pick a channel, press ▶ Play the line and listen for the pattern. Tap any pad to hear one word. Sound only plays when you tap.","exam":"The writer uses alliteration ('Big berms bounced my bike back') to copy the bumpy, thudding rhythm of the wheels slamming into each berm, which makes the reader feel every jolt through the handlebars.","trap":"Calling any repeated letter alliteration. It's about the sound, not the spelling: 'kind cats' is alliteration, but 'cheap cars' isn't. Assonance is vowel sounds, wherever they are in the word.","big":"The big idea: sound features make you hear the writing. Alliteration repeats first consonant sounds, assonance repeats vowel sounds, and onomatopoeia sounds like the noise it names. Always ask: what does the sound copy, or what mood does it make?","kupu":["orokati tārua","kupu ororite","huarite"]},{"k":"compare","lab":"Comparisons","cap":"A comparison puts two things side by side. The overlap is what they share, and that's where the effect comes from. Switch between simile, metaphor and personification.","exam":"The writer uses a simile ('like a stone down a well') to show how steep the drop is and how little control the rider has, which makes the reader feel the stomach-lurching fall and sense there is no turning back.","trap":"Just saying 'it compares the track to a stone'. That only names the feature. Say what they share (straight down, fast, no way back) and why that matters.","big":"The big idea: a simile says one thing is like another; a metaphor says it is another; personification gives something non-human a human action. To explain the effect, name what the two things share.","kupu":["kupu whakarite"]},{"k":"amp","lab":"Turn it up","cap":"Start with a plain sentence. Slide up a channel to add hyperbole, repetition, a rhetorical question or emotive language, and watch the intensity climb.","exam":"The writer uses hyperbole ('practically upside down') to exaggerate the steepness and show how terrifying it looks from the top, which makes the reader understand how huge the drop feels to the rider.","trap":"Calling every question a rhetorical question. If the writer answers it straight away, or expects an answer, it's just a question. A rhetorical question is asked for effect.","big":"The big idea: these features turn up the feeling. Hyperbole exaggerates, repetition hammers an idea home, a rhetorical question pulls the reader in, and emotive language makes them feel. Explain what feeling is being turned up, and why.","kupu":["tukurua"]},{"k":"tone","lab":"Picture and tone","cap":"The same place, three tones. Change the tone and watch the scene and the word choices change. Sensory imagery is underlined by sense; tone words are in bold.","exam":"The writer creates an excited tone through word choices like 'flew' and 'whooped'. Sensory imagery such as 'cold air whipping my face' (touch) helps the reader share the rush and energy of the moment.","trap":"Saying 'the tone is happy' or 'sad' with no proof. Pick a precise tone word (thrilled, uneasy, peaceful) and quote the words that create it.","big":"The big idea: imagery paints the picture; tone colours it. Tone is the writer's attitude, and you prove it with word choices. Name the tone with a precise word (tense, not just 'bad'), then quote the words that create it.","kupu":["kupu"]},{"k":"explain","lab":"Build the sentence","cap":"The exam sentence has four parts. Tap a reel to change it, and push the strength meter as high as it goes. Then try Next line.","exam":"","trap":"Ending with 'which makes the reader want to keep reading'. It could be said about any text, so it shows nothing. Say what this line makes the reader feel, picture or understand.","big":"The big idea: The writer uses [feature] ('quote') to [effect], which makes the reader [feel or understand something]. The feature name gets you started; the effect and the reader's response are where the marks are.","kupu":["rerenga kōrero","kīanga"]},{"k":"test","lab":"Test yourself","cap":"An original poem from your world. Answer each challenge, then press Check. Solved lines light up on the poem.","exam":"","trap":"","big":"Exam tip: in an unfamiliar text, find two or three features, then write one strong sentence for each: feature, short quote, effect, reader. Three precise sentences beat a page of feature-spotting.","kupu":[]}],"challenges":[{"q":"Line 1: “At the top, the redwoods hold their breath.” Which feature stands out? Choose…hyperbolemetaphorsimilepersonification","check":"()=>solve(\"s0\",val(\"wc0\")===P_().spot[0][1])","ok":"()=>`Yes: ${FEAT[P_().spot[0][1]].n}, ${FEAT[P_().spot[0][1]].def}.`","no":"()=>val(\"wc0\")?`Not quite. Check the definition: ${FEAT[val(\"wc0\")].n} is ${FEAT[val(\"wc0\")].def}. Does that fit?`:\"Choose a feature first.\"","okIsFn":true,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"Line 4: “Roots rattle, rocks rip at the rubber,” Which feature stands out? Choose…hyperboleassonanceonomatopoeiaalliteration","check":"()=>solve(\"s1\",val(\"wc1\")===P_().spot[1][1])","ok":"()=>`Yes: ${FEAT[P_().spot[1][1]].n}. Now you could explain its effect.`","no":"()=>val(\"wc1\")?`Not quite. Read the line aloud and ask what the words are doing.`:\"Choose a feature first.\"","okIsFn":true,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"Line 5 uses a simile: “blurs like a video on fast-forward”. Pick the best explanation. The writer uses a simile to make the poem more interesting.The writer uses a simile ('blurs like a video on fast-forward'), which is comparing two things using 'like' or 'as'.The writer uses a simile ('blurs like a video on fast-forward') to show how fast the rider is going, so the trees smear past, which makes the reader feel the dizzy, rushing speed of the ride.","check":"()=>solve(\"best\",rval(\"wc2\")===\"c\")","ok":"Yes: it names the feature, quotes briefly, says the effect in this line and how the reader responds.","no":"()=>rval(\"wc2\")?\"Not that one. One option is vague and one just defines the feature. Which one says what this line actually does?\":\"Pick an option first.\"","okIsFn":false,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"This explanation is weak: “The writer uses a metaphor ('My heart is a drum') to show it is good, which makes the reader want to read on.” Which ending fixes it? to make it sound good, which makes the reader enjoy itto show the rider's heart pounding loudly with nerves, which makes the reader hear and feel the rider's fear before the dropto describe it, which makes the reader read on","check":"()=>solve(\"fix\",rval(\"wc3\")===\"b\")","ok":"()=>`Yes. Now the whole sentence works: ${sentence(P_().fix)}`","no":"()=>rval(\"wc3\")?\"That ending is still vague. Look for the one that's specific to this line.\":\"Pick an option first.\"","okIsFn":true,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"What is the tone of the whole poem? Choose…furiousthrilledboredgloomy","check":"()=>solve(\"tone\",val(\"wc4\")===P_().tone)","ok":"()=>`Yes: ${P_().tone}. In the exam, back it up with two quotes from the poem.`","no":"()=>val(\"wc4\")?\"Not quite. Look at the verbs and the last line: what attitude do they show?\":\"Choose a tone first.\"","okIsFn":true,"noIsFn":true}],"controls":[{"type":"select","id":"ctx","label":"Your world"},{"type":"select","id":"sch","label":"Channel"},{"type":"button","id":"play","label":"▶ Play the line"},{"type":"toggle","id":"mute","label":"Sound off"},{"type":"select","id":"cch","label":"Feature"},{"type":"toggle","id":"hyper","label":"Hyperbole"},{"type":"toggle","id":"rep","label":"Repetition"},{"type":"toggle","id":"rq","label":"Rhetorical question"},{"type":"toggle","id":"emo","label":"Emotive language"},{"type":"select","id":"tone","label":"Tone"},{"type":"button","id":"next","label":"Next line →"}],"shell":"kit"},"essay":{"id":"essay","title":"The paragraph machine","href":"m/essay.html","desc":"Read an unfamiliar text for purpose, audience and techniques, answer retrieve, infer and explain-the-effect questions, build a TEEL paragraph, sharpen vague words, and plan an essay where every paragraph plugs into the thesis.","blurb":"Retrieve, infer and explain the effect, then build a TEEL paragraph and plan an essay.","tags":["Year 9","Year 10","English"],"built":true,"eyebrow":"English · Reading and writing","crumb":"Year 9 and 10 English: unfamiliar texts, paragraphs and essays","badge":{"label":"English · Year 9","examText":"Tue 3 Nov","exam":"2026-11-03T09:00:00+13:00"},"papers":[{"label":"NZQA: English past exams","url":"https://www2.nzqa.govt.nz/ncea/subjects/past-exams-and-exemplars/"}],"truth":"The letter and the story are original, written for this machine, and kept short and simple so the skills stand out. TEEL is one common way to structure an analysis paragraph; your teacher may call it PEEL or SEXY and want slightly different parts. Check your school's exam guidance for exactly what your teacher expects.","footer":"Covers Year 9–10 English: reading unfamiliar texts for purpose, audience and techniques; retrieve, infer and explain-the-effect questions; TEEL analysis paragraphs; precise vocabulary; and planning a short essay with a thesis. Leads into NCEA Level 1 English.","mainLabel":"A letter to the editor under three reading lenses, sentence blocks that build a TEEL paragraph, and an essay plan wired together with cables","kupu":[{"mi":"tuhinga","en":"text, writing","ta":8648,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"read, questions"},{"mi":"kaituhi","en":"writer, author","ta":2031,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"read"},{"mi":"kaupapa","en":"topic, theme, purpose","ta":2439,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"read, plan"},{"mi":"kōwae","en":"paragraph","ta":3169,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"teel, plan"},{"mi":"rerenga kōrero","en":"sentence","ta":6727,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"teel"},{"mi":"taunakitanga","en":"evidence","ta":14911,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"teel, sharpen"},{"mi":"whakamārama","en":"explanation","ta":9594,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"teel"},{"mi":"tohutoro","en":"reference (where a quote comes from)","ta":12623,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"sharpen"}],"steps":[{"k":"read","lab":"Read the text","cap":"An unfamiliar text, read three ways. Switch the lens to find the writer's purpose, the audience, and the techniques they use.","exam":"This letter to the editor is written to persuade the local council to build a pump track. Its audience is the council and adult readers of the local paper, which is why the writer stays polite and reasonable: 'We are not asking for a stadium.'","trap":"Saying the audience is 'everyone'. Look for clues: who is the writer asking, and who has the power to say yes?","big":"The big idea: before you answer anything, work out why the text was written, who it's for, and how the writer tries to get what they want. Every answer you write hangs off those three.","kupu":["tuhinga","kaituhi","kaupapa"]},{"k":"questions","lab":"Three kinds of question","cap":"Comprehension questions come in three kinds. Choose one, then tap the sentence in the letter where the answer lives.","exam":"Kids ride in the car park behind the library.","trap":"Writing an inference with no evidence. An infer answer needs your idea, then 'because', then a quote that proves it.","big":"The big idea: retrieve means find it, infer means work it out from clues, and explain the effect means say what the writing does to the reader. Each kind needs a different-shaped answer.","kupu":["tuhinga"]},{"k":"teel","lab":"Build a TEEL paragraph","cap":"Six sentences, but only four belong in an analysis paragraph. Tap them in order: Topic, Evidence, Explain, Link. Tap a numbered one again to take it out.","exam":"The writer persuades the council by making the problem feel urgent and the solution feel easy. The letter asks, 'Is that really the best our town can offer?' This rhetorical question makes adult readers feel a little embarrassed that young people are stuck dodging cars in a car park. Once readers feel responsible for the problem, a simple, affordable pump track seems like the obvious answer.","trap":"Retelling the text instead of analysing it. 'The letter has four paragraphs' describes it; it doesn't explain how the writer persuades.","big":"The big idea: Topic sentence makes your point. Evidence is a short quote. Explain how the quote proves the point. Link back to the question. Personal opinions and retelling don't count as analysis.","kupu":["kōwae","rerenga kōrero","taunakitanga","whakamārama"]},{"k":"sharpen","lab":"Sharpen it","cap":"This paragraph has the right shape but vague words. Tap each highlighted phrase to swap it for something more precise, and fill the meter.","exam":"The writer uses a list of three ('little kids on balance bikes, teenagers learning tricks, parents chatting') to emphasise that a pump track is good for the whole community, not just teenagers. This makes parents picture their own children there and makes the council more likely to say yes.","trap":"Using 'shows' for everything. Choose a verb that says how the writing works: suggests, emphasises, exaggerates, contrasts, challenges.","big":"The big idea: markers reward precise words. Name the exact technique, use a verb that says how (emphasises, suggests, contrasts), and say exactly who feels what.","kupu":["taunakitanga","tohutoro"]},{"k":"plan","lab":"Plan the essay","cap":"An essay is an intro with a thesis, two or three body paragraphs, and a conclusion. Pick a thesis and watch whether the body paragraphs can plug into it.","exam":"In the letter 'Give Us Somewhere to Ride', a Year 10 rider writes to the local paper asking for a pump track. The writer persuades the council by making the problem feel urgent and the solution feel easy and fair.","trap":"A thesis that only announces the topic ('This essay is about…'). A thesis makes a claim your body paragraphs can prove.","big":"The big idea: the thesis is your answer to the essay question in one sentence. Each body paragraph proves one part of it. If a paragraph can't plug into the thesis, either the paragraph or the thesis needs to change.","kupu":["kaupapa","kōwae"]},{"k":"test","lab":"Test yourself","cap":"A new unseen text. Read it twice, then answer each challenge and press Check.","exam":"","trap":"","big":"Exam tip: in the exam, read the questions first, then the text. Underline the evidence as you go, and match the shape of your answer to the kind of question.","kupu":[]}],"challenges":[{"q":"Retrieve. What time did the lights come back on? Choose…7:14Ten o'clockMidnightThe next morning","check":"()=>solve(\"c0\",val(\"ec0\")===\"ten\")","ok":"Yes: 'When the lights snapped back on at ten'. Retrieve answers can be short.","no":"()=>val(\"ec0\")?\"Not quite. 7:14 is when the power went off. Look near the end.\":\"Choose an answer first.\"","okIsFn":false,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"Infer. How does the family feel when the lights come back on, and what's the best evidence? Relieved: 'the lights snapped back on'Disappointed: 'we all groaned'Scared: 'long, wobbling shadows'","check":"()=>solve(\"c1\",rval(\"ec1\")===\"b\")","ok":"Yes. They groan because the cosy night is over, which the last line confirms: 'the best night in months'.","no":"()=>rval(\"ec1\")?\"Not that one. Find the moment the lights come back on: what do they all do?\":\"Pick an option first.\"","okIsFn":false,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"Explain the effect. Which is the best explanation of “the wind shoved at the windows as if it wanted to come in”? The writer uses personification to make the story more interesting.Personification is when you give human qualities to something that isn't human.The writer uses personification ('shoved at the windows') to make the storm seem like an aggressive intruder, which makes the reader feel how safe and cosy the family is inside.","check":"()=>solve(\"c2\",rval(\"ec2\")===\"c\")","ok":"Yes: feature, short quote, effect, and the reader's response.","no":"()=>rval(\"ec2\")?\"Not that one. One is vague and one only defines the feature.\":\"Pick an option first.\"","okIsFn":false,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"Topic sentence. For an essay on how the writer shows a power cut bringing a family together, which topic sentence is strongest? In this paragraph I will talk about the power cut.The power went out at 7:14 and they ate pizza.By taking away every screen, the writer leaves the family with nothing to do but enjoy each other's company.","check":"()=>solve(\"c3\",rval(\"ec3\")===\"c\")","ok":"Yes: it makes a point that answers the question, and the rest of the paragraph can prove it.","no":"()=>rval(\"ec3\")?\"Not that one. One announces, one retells. Which makes a point you could prove?\":\"Pick an option first.\"","okIsFn":false,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"Precise verb. “The family's groan ___ that they did not want the night to end.” Choose…showssayssuggestsmakes","check":"()=>solve(\"c4\",val(\"ec4\")===\"suggests\")","ok":"Yes: 'suggests' fits an inference. Nobody says it out loud; the groan hints at it.","no":"()=>val(\"ec4\")===\"shows\"?\"'Shows' isn't wrong, but it's vague. Which verb fits something hinted at, not stated?\":val(\"ec4\")?\"That verb doesn't fit the sentence. Try again.\":\"Choose a verb first.\"","okIsFn":false,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"Purpose. What is the main purpose of this text? Choose…To persuadeTo informTo entertain and share an experienceTo instruct","check":"()=>solve(\"c5\",val(\"ec5\")===\"ent\")","ok":"Yes: it's a story that entertains and reflects on an experience. Clue: 'It had been the best night in months.'","no":"()=>val(\"ec5\")?\"Not quite. Is the writer asking for something, teaching facts, or telling a story?\":\"Choose an answer first.\"","okIsFn":false,"noIsFn":true}],"controls":[{"type":"select","id":"lens","label":"Lens"},{"type":"select","id":"qt","label":"Question"},{"type":"button","id":"reset","label":"↺ Start again"},{"type":"button","id":"showme","label":"Show me"},{"type":"button","id":"dreset","label":"↺ Back to vague"},{"type":"select","id":"thesis","label":"Thesis"},{"type":"select","id":"bodies","label":"Body paragraphs"}],"shell":"kit"},"maps":{"id":"maps","title":"The map machine","href":"m/maps.html","desc":"Read a made-up 1:25 000 map of Kōwhai Bay: compass points and bearings, grid references, scale along a winding bike trail, contours with a cross-section that draws itself, latitude and longitude across Aotearoa, and a climate graph.","blurb":"Bearings, grid references, scale, contours and climate graphs around Kōwhai Bay.","tags":["Year 9","Year 10","Social studies","Geography"],"built":true,"eyebrow":"Social studies and geography · Map and graph skills","crumb":"Year 9–10 social studies: compass, grid references, scale, contours, latitude and longitude, climate graphs","badge":{"label":"Year 9 social studies","exam":"2026-11-02T08:30:00+13:00","examText":"Mon 2 Nov"},"papers":[{"label":"NZQA past exams: Social Studies and Geography","url":"https://www2.nzqa.govt.nz/ncea/subjects/past-exams-and-exemplars/"},{"label":"LINZ: NZ topographic maps","url":"https://www.linz.govt.nz/products-services/maps/new-zealand-topographic-maps"}],"truth":"Kōwhai Bay, its marae, trail, heights and climate graph are made up for practice; the climate figures are example data, not real NIWA records. The Aotearoa coastline is simplified. City positions are rounded city-centre coordinates. Real NZ Topo50 maps are 1:50 000 with 20 m contours and use a different grid (NZTM); this map uses a simple 1:25 000 grid like many school exams. Check your school's exam topics.","footer":"Covers the map and graph skills used across Year 9–10 social studies and geography: direction and bearings, four- and six-figure grid references, scale and distance, contours and cross-sections, latitude and longitude, and climate graphs.","mainLabel":"A made-up topographic map of a coastal valley with a stream, a marae, a town, a mountain-bike trail and fishing rocks; a map of Aotearoa; and a climate graph","kupu":[{"mi":"raki","en":"north","ta":6446,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"compass"},{"mi":"tonga","en":"south","ta":8433,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"compass"},{"mi":"rāwhiti","en":"east","ta":6663,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"compass"},{"mi":"uru","en":"west","ta":8912,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"compass"},{"mi":"kāpehu","en":"compass","ta":2226,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"compass"},{"mi":"mahere","en":"map, plan, chart","ta":3350,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"grid, scale"},{"mi":"āwhata","en":"scale (of a map)","ta":15342,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Reo Pāngarau (MoE 2010) cited in entry","stepsNote":"scale"},{"mi":"teitei","en":"height, altitude","ta":7895,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Reo Pūtaiao (MoE 2009) cited in entry","stepsNote":"contour"}],"steps":[{"k":"compass","lab":"Which way?","cap":"The compass sits on the place you start from. Tap anywhere on the map to drop a pin, or pick a place, and watch the needle swing round to it.","exam":"Lookout Hill summit is south-west of the marae, on a bearing of 237° and about 2.5 km away. I measured the bearing clockwise from north, starting at the marae.","trap":"Measuring from the wrong place. \"The summit from the marae\" means stand at the marae and look towards the summit. Swap them and the direction flips: north-east becomes south-west.","big":"The big idea: there are 8 compass points, 45° apart. A bearing is the angle measured clockwise from north, always written with three figures: north-east is 045°.","kupu":["raki","tonga","rāwhiti","uru","kāpehu"]},{"k":"grid","lab":"Grid references","cap":"Tap the map to drop a pin. The machine reads its four-figure reference (the square) and its six-figure reference (the exact spot, to the nearest 100 m).","exam":"The marae is in grid square 2342. Its six-figure grid reference is 238 429: easting 238 (along the corridor) then northing 429 (up the stairs).","trap":"Reading the northing first, or using the lines at the top or right of the square. Always use the easting line on the left and the northing line along the bottom of the square.","big":"The big idea: eastings first, then northings: \"along the corridor, then up the stairs\". A four-figure reference names a 1 km square; a six-figure one adds tenths to pin a point within 100 m.","kupu":["mahere"]},{"k":"scale","lab":"Scale and distance","cap":"Ride the mountain-bike trail from the car park (P) to the summit. The ruler only measures straight lines; a piece of string follows every bend.","exam":"The trail from the car park to the summit is about 4.4 km long, but the straight-line distance is only 3.2 km. I laid string along the trail (17.5 cm on the 1:25 000 map) and multiplied by 250 m per cm.","trap":"Measuring a winding road or river with a straight ruler. That gives the straight-line distance, which is always shorter. Follow the bends with string or the edge of a piece of paper.","big":"The big idea: scale links the map to real life. On a 1:25 000 map, 1 cm stands for 25 000 cm (250 m), so 4 cm is 1 km.","kupu":["āwhata","mahere"]},{"k":"contour","lab":"Contours and height","cap":"Contour lines join places at the same height. Drag A and B anywhere on the map, or pick a line, and the cross-section draws itself in the close-up.","exam":"Contour lines join points of equal height above sea level. On the slope above the stream they are close together, so the land is steep; on the valley floor they are far apart, so it is gentle. The cross-section shows a valley, with the stream at its lowest point.","trap":"Thinking close contours mean high land. Close means steep. To find the height, read the numbers on the lines and count up or down in 50 m steps.","big":"The big idea: close-together contours mean steep land; far-apart contours mean gentle land. A cross-section is the side view you'd see if you sliced the land along a line.","kupu":["teitei"]},{"k":"latlong","lab":"Where on Earth?","cap":"Pick a city or slide the crosshair. Latitude says how far south of the equator; longitude says how far east of Greenwich.","exam":"Tāmaki Makaurau Auckland is at about 36.85°S, 174.76°E (36°51′S, 174°46′E). It is 36.85° south of the equator and 174.76° east of the prime meridian at Greenwich. Ōtautahi Christchurch, at 43.53°S, is further from the equator.","trap":"Swapping them. Latitude lines run across the map (east–west), but they measure how far north or south you are. And don't forget the S and E.","big":"The big idea: latitude and longitude give any place on Earth an exact address. Say latitude first, then longitude, with N/S and E/W.","kupu":[]},{"k":"climate","lab":"Climate graph","cap":"A year of weather in Kōwhai Bay. Bars show rainfall (left axis), the line shows temperature (right axis). Tap a month or slide through the year.","exam":"Kōwhai Bay is warmest in February (20.0 °C) and coolest in July (11.2 °C), a temperature range of 8.8 °C. Rainfall is highest in winter, peaking in July at 140 mm, and lowest in January (70 mm). It gets about 1,215 mm of rain a year.","trap":"Reading temperature off the rainfall axis. Bars belong to the left axis (mm), the line to the right axis (°C). And in Aotearoa, July is the middle of winter.","big":"The big idea: a climate graph shows average temperature and rainfall for each month. Describe the pattern, quote numbers with units, and point out any exception.","kupu":[]},{"k":"test","lab":"Test yourself","cap":"Six challenges. Choose a map layer, use the controls or tap the map, then press Check.","exam":"","trap":"","big":"Exam tip: always give units and the hemisphere letters, say where you measured from, and quote numbers from the map or graph to back up what you say.","kupu":[]}],"challenges":[{"q":"Drop a pin on the marae (Grid references layer). What's its six-figure grid reference?","check":"()=>Math.hypot(s.pin.X-M.X,s.pin.Y-M.Y)<0.15","ok":"()=>`Yes: the marae is at ${grid6(s.pin)}, in square ${grid4(s.pin)}.`","no":"()=>`Your pin is at ${grid6(s.pin)}. The marae is the little house with the pink roof, north of the stream.`","okIsFn":true,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"Drop a pin that is south-east of Lookout Hill summit and at least 1 km from it.","check":"()=>{const b=bearing(LK,s.pin);return b>=112.5&&b<157.5&&Math.hypot(s.pin.X-LK.X,s.pin.Y-LK.Y)>=1}","ok":"()=>`Yes: bearing ${b3(bearing(LK,s.pin))} from the summit, ${fmt(Math.hypot(s.pin.X-LK.X,s.pin.Y-LK.Y),1)} km away.`","no":"()=>{const b=bearing(LK,s.pin);return `From the summit your pin is ${dir8(b)} (${b3(b)}), ${fmt(Math.hypot(s.pin.X-LK.X,s.pin.Y-LK.Y),1)} km away. South-east is between 112.5° and 157.5°. Tip: set From to summit.`}","okIsFn":true,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"Ride exactly 3.0 km along the trail (within 0.1 km).","check":"()=>Math.abs(Math.min(s.trail,TRL)-3)<=0.1","ok":"Yes: 3 km along the trail. On a 1:25 000 map that's 12 cm of string.","no":"()=>`You're at ${fmt(Math.min(s.trail,TRL),2)} km. Use the Along the trail slider.`","okIsFn":false,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"Drag A or B so the cross-section goes over the top of Lookout Hill (above 400 m).","check":"()=>profile().P.some(p=>p.h>400)","ok":"()=>`Yes: the highest point on your line is ${Math.round(Math.max(...profile().P.map(p=>p.h)))} m. The summit is inside the 400 m contour.`","no":"()=>`The highest point on your line is ${Math.round(Math.max(...profile().P.map(p=>p.h)))} m. Find the ring of contours with the ▲ inside it.`","okIsFn":true,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"Set the crosshair within 0.1° of 45.87°S, 170.50°E. Which city is it?","check":"()=>Math.abs(s.lat-45.87)<=0.1&&Math.abs(s.lon-170.5)<=0.1","ok":"Yes: that's Ōtepoti Dunedin, about 2.3° further south than Ōtautahi Christchurch.","no":"()=>`You're at ${fmt(s.lat,2)}°S, ${fmt(s.lon,2)}°E. Latitude first: slide down to 45.87°S.`","okIsFn":false,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"Set the climate graph to Kōwhai Bay's coldest month.","check":"()=>s.month===TEMP.indexOf(TMIN)","ok":"Yes: July, 11.2 °C. It's midwinter in the southern hemisphere.","no":"()=>`${MONL[s.month]} averages ${fmt(TEMP[s.month],1)} °C. Look for the lowest point on the line.`","okIsFn":false,"noIsFn":true}],"controls":[{"type":"play","id":"play","label":""},{"type":"select","id":"from","label":"From"},{"type":"select","id":"to","label":"To"},{"type":"button","id":"pinat","label":"📍 Pin the marae"},{"type":"range","id":"trail","label":"Along the trail"},{"type":"select","id":"sec","label":"Cross-section"},{"type":"button","id":"redraw","label":"✏️ Draw it again"},{"type":"select","id":"city","label":"City"},{"type":"range","id":"lat","label":"Latitude"},{"type":"range","id":"lon","label":"Longitude"},{"type":"range","id":"month","label":"Month"},{"type":"select","id":"view","label":"Map layer"}],"shell":"kit"},"market":{"id":"market","title":"The market machine","href":"m/market.html","desc":"Sort needs from wants in a vending machine, find your opportunity cost, run an ice-block stall at the school fair, shift supply and demand until the price settles, then weigh up perspectives on a bike park.","blurb":"Needs and wants, opportunity cost, and supply and demand at the school fair.","tags":["Year 9","Year 10","Social studies"],"built":true,"eyebrow":"Social studies · Economics and social inquiry","crumb":"Year 9–10 social studies: needs and wants, scarcity, opportunity cost, producers and consumers, supply and demand, perspectives and values","badge":{"label":"Year 9 social studies","exam":"2026-11-02T08:30:00+13:00","examText":"Mon 2 Nov"},"papers":[{"label":"NZQA past exams: Social Studies and Geography","url":"https://www2.nzqa.govt.nz/ncea/subjects/past-exams-and-exemplars/"}],"truth":"The vending machine, prices, the stall's demand, supply and costs, the people and the Kōwhai Reserve bike-park issue are all made up for practice; real markets are messier. The people are fictional and don't speak for any real group. Check your school's exam topics.","footer":"Covers Year 9–10 social studies economics (needs and wants, scarcity, choices and opportunity cost, producers and consumers, supply and demand) and social inquiry (focus questions, sources, perspectives and values). Non-partisan: no real parties or politicians.","mainLabel":"A pastel vending machine, an ice-block stall at a school fair, a supply and demand graph and a values continuum with seven people","kupu":[{"mi":"ōhanga","en":"economy","ta":4704,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Reo Pāngarau (MoE 2010) cited in entry","stepsNote":"(machine list only)"},{"mi":"matea","en":"needs","ta":3846,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"needs"},{"mi":"hiahia","en":"want, desire","ta":1132,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"needs"},{"mi":"ngōuruuru","en":"scarcity, shortage","ta":4681,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"choice"},{"mi":"utu","en":"price, cost","ta":8937,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"choice, market"},{"mi":"kaiwhakaputa","en":"producer","ta":45702,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Reo Pāngarau (MoE 2010) cited in entry","stepsNote":"stall"},{"mi":"kiritaki","en":"consumer, customer","ta":11034,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"stall"},{"mi":"pakihi","en":"business","ta":5016,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"stall"}],"steps":[{"k":"needs","lab":"Needs and wants","cap":"Tap each item in the vending machine to sort it: tap once for NEED, twice for WANT. Sort all eight and the machine marks your sort.","exam":"Needs are things people must have to survive and stay healthy, such as food, water, clothing, shelter and health care. Wants are things that make life more enjoyable but aren't essential, such as bubble tea or headphones. A rain jacket is a need because clothing protects us from the weather.","trap":"Calling something a need just because you really, really want it. Ask: could I survive and stay healthy without it? If yes, it's a want.","big":"The big idea: needs are what people must have to survive and stay well: food, water, clothing, shelter and health care. Wants make life more enjoyable but aren't essential.","kupu":["matea","hiahia"]},{"k":"choice","lab":"Scarcity and choice","cap":"You have limited money. Tap items in the order you want them: 1st, 2nd, 3rd… The machine buys down your list while the money lasts. The first thing you miss out on is your opportunity cost.","exam":"Scarcity means our wants are unlimited but our resources, like money and time, are limited, so we have to make choices. Opportunity cost is the next-best choice we give up. With $20 I bought bubble tea, game credit and lollies, so my opportunity cost was the sandwich, my next-best choice, which I couldn't afford.","trap":"Listing everything you didn't buy as the opportunity cost. It's only the single next-best thing you gave up.","big":"The big idea: scarcity means wants are unlimited but resources (money, time, materials) are limited, so we must choose. Every choice has an opportunity cost: the next-best thing you give up.","kupu":["ngōuruuru","utu"]},{"k":"stall","lab":"Producer, consumer","cap":"You run the ice-block stall at the school fair. You're the producer; the students buying are consumers. Change your price and watch the queue, the money and the profit.","exam":"A producer makes goods or provides services, and a consumer buys and uses them. At the stall I am the producer and the students are consumers: ice blocks go from me to them, and money comes back to me. When I buy fruit to make the ice blocks, I am a consumer too. If I raise my price too much, fewer consumers buy, so my profit falls.","trap":"Thinking a higher price always means more profit. Too high and customers stop buying; too low and you barely cover your costs.","big":"The big idea: producers make goods or provide services; consumers buy and use them. Goods and services flow one way, money flows the other. Most people are both.","kupu":["kaiwhakaputa","kiritaki","pakihi"]},{"k":"market","lab":"Supply and demand","cap":"Slide the price and watch for a shortage (a queue) or a surplus (melting leftovers). Then make something happen, a hot day or a fruit price rise, and let the price settle.","exam":"On a hot day the demand for ice blocks increases, so the demand curve shifts right. At the old price of $1.75 there is a shortage, because more people want ice blocks than the stall makes. The price rises until the quantity demanded equals the quantity supplied, at a new equilibrium of $2.00, with 60 sold an hour.","trap":"Mixing up a movement and a shift. A change in the ice block's own price moves you along a curve; something else changing (weather, costs) shifts the whole curve.","big":"The big idea: when the price rises, people want to buy less (demand) but sellers want to make more (supply). The equilibrium price is where they match: no shortage, no surplus.","kupu":["utu"]},{"k":"inquiry","lab":"Social inquiry","cap":"A made-up issue: should Kōwhai Reserve get a mountain-bike park? Tap each person to hear their perspective, then slide yourself onto the values continuum. The close-up has focus questions and sources.","exam":"Grace is against the bike park because she values protecting native species: she thinks new trails and noise would disturb the kererū and tūī nesting in the reserve. Sam supports it because he values young people's health and wellbeing, and says there is nowhere safe to ride nearby. They disagree because they hold different values.","trap":"Describing what someone thinks without the value behind it. Say who, what they think, and why: \"… because they value …\".","big":"The big idea: a perspective is a way of seeing an issue, shaped by values: what people believe is important. People can share the facts and still disagree because their values differ.","kupu":[]},{"k":"test","lab":"Test yourself","cap":"Five challenges. Choose a scene, use the controls or tap the picture, then press Check.","exam":"","trap":"","big":"Exam tip: for cause and effect, chain it: \"because … this means … as a result …\". For perspectives, always add the value: \"… because they value …\".","kupu":[]}],"challenges":[{"q":"Sort all eight items correctly into needs and wants.","check":"()=>{const sc=sortScore();return sc.done===8&&sc.right===8}","ok":"Yes: food, water, clothing and health care are needs; the rest are wants.","no":"()=>{const sc=sortScore();return sc.done<8?`You've sorted ${sc.done} of 8. Choose the Needs and wants scene and tap each item.`:`${sc.right} of 8 right. Tap the ✗ items to move them.`}","okIsFn":false,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"Rank at least three items, then set your wallet so your opportunity cost is your number 2 choice.","check":"()=>{const sh=shop();return s.rank.length>=3&&sh.oc===s.rank[1]}","ok":"()=>`Yes: you gave up the ${ITEMS[shop().oc].n.toLowerCase()}, your next-best choice after the ${ITEMS[s.rank[0]].n.toLowerCase()}.`","no":"()=>{const sh=shop();return s.rank.length<3?\"Rank at least three items first (Scarcity and choice scene).\":`Right now your opportunity cost is ${sh.oc>=0?\"the \"+ITEMS[sh.oc].n.toLowerCase():\"nothing\"}. You want enough money for your 1st choice but not your 2nd.`}","okIsFn":true,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"Set the stall price that makes the most profit.","check":"()=>Math.abs(s.sp-1.75)<0.01","ok":"()=>`Yes: ${$$(s.sp)} gives ${$$(profit(s.sp))} an hour. Cheaper sells more but earns less on each; dearer scares buyers off.`","no":"()=>`At ${$$(s.sp)} you make ${$$(profit(s.sp))} an hour. Look for the top of the profit curve.`","okIsFn":true,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"It's a hot day AND fruit prices rise. Set the price where there's no shortage and no surplus.","check":"()=>s.dS===1&&s.sS===-1&&Math.abs(Qd(s.P)-Qs(s.P))<0.5","ok":"()=>`Yes: ${$$(s.P)}, ${fmt(Qd(s.P))} an hour. Both changes push the price up.`","no":"()=>s.dS!==1||s.sS!==-1?\"First set Demand to a hot day and Supply to fruit prices rise.\":`At ${$$(s.P)} there's a ${Qd(s.P)>Qs(s.P)?\"shortage\":\"surplus\"} of ${fmt(Math.abs(Qd(s.P)-Qs(s.P)))}. Try “Let the price settle”.`","okIsFn":true,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"In the social inquiry, tap a person who is against the bike park. What value is behind their view?","check":"()=>{const P=PEOPLE.find(p=>p.id===s.sel);return !!P&&P.st<-0.6}","ok":"()=>{const P=PEOPLE.find(p=>p.id===s.sel);return `Yes: ${P.n} is against it because ${P.because}.`}","no":"()=>{const P=PEOPLE.find(p=>p.id===s.sel);return P?`${P.n.split(\",\")[0]} is ${P.st>0.6?\"for it\":\"unsure\"}. Look at the left (against) end of the continuum.`:\"Tap a person first.\"}","okIsFn":true,"noIsFn":true}],"controls":[{"type":"play","id":"play","label":""},{"type":"button","id":"resetSort","label":"↺ Empty the bins"},{"type":"range","id":"budget","label":"Wallet"},{"type":"button","id":"clear","label":"↺ Clear my ranking"},{"type":"range","id":"sp","label":"Stall price"},{"type":"range","id":"P","label":"Market price"},{"type":"select","id":"dS","label":"Demand"},{"type":"select","id":"sS","label":"Supply"},{"type":"button","id":"settle","label":"⚖️ Let the price settle"},{"type":"range","id":"you","label":"Where you stand"},{"type":"select","id":"tool","label":"Close-up"},{"type":"select","id":"view","label":"Scene"}],"shell":"kit"},"claw":{"id":"claw","title":"The claw machine","href":"m/claw.html","desc":"A pastel arcade claw machine: relative frequency, the long run, two-step tree diagrams, expected value (is it worth $2?) and spotting a machine that pays out on a schedule.","blurb":"Relative frequency, tree diagrams and expected value: is it worth $2?","tags":["Year 9","Year 10","Maths","Probability"],"built":true,"eyebrow":"Maths · Probability and statistics","crumb":"Years 9 and 10 Maths (probability, relative frequency, expected value) and NCEA Level 1 ideas","badge":{"label":"Probability · Years 9–11"},"papers":[{"label":"NZQA: Mathematics and Statistics","url":"https://www2.nzqa.govt.nz/ncea/subjects/past-exams-and-exemplars/"}],"truth":"A simulated claw machine with chances you set. Real machines vary, and some can be set to pay out on a schedule; this shows how the data would look either way. No real money involved.","footer":"Covers Years 9–10 probability (experimental and theoretical probability, relative frequency, independent events, tree diagrams, expected value) leading into NCEA Level 1 statistics and probability.","mainLabel":"A claw machine full of plush toys beside a scoreboard of plays, prizes and expected value","kupu":[{"mi":"tūponotanga","en":"probability, chance","ta":46069,"note":"","chip":["p"],"evidence":"Te Reo Pāngarau (MoE 2010) cited in entry","stepsNote":"long, tree, value"},{"mi":"whakamātau tūponotanga","en":"probability experiment","ta":46107,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Reo Pāngarau (MoE 2010) cited in entry","stepsNote":"play"},{"mi":"pāpono","en":"event (in probability)","ta":45828,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Reo Pāngarau (MoE 2010) cited in entry","stepsNote":"tree"},{"mi":"putanga","en":"outcome","ta":6346,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Reo Pāngarau (MoE 2010) cited in entry","stepsNote":"play, tree"},{"mi":"hoahoa rākau","en":"tree diagram","ta":45657,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Reo Pāngarau (MoE 2010) cited in entry","stepsNote":"tree"},{"mi":"raraunga","en":"data","ta":6555,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"play, long, rigged"},{"mi":"tauanga","en":"statistics","ta":44587,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Reo Pāngarau (MoE 2010) cited in entry","stepsNote":"rigged"},{"mi":"uara","en":"value (maths)","ta":8853,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Reo Pāngarau (MoE 2010) cited in entry","stepsNote":"value"}],"steps":[{"k":"play","lab":"Have a go","cap":"Press Play once to drop the claw, or play 10 at once. Count your prizes. Is it as easy as it looks?","exam":"Relative frequency is an estimate of probability from an experiment: wins ÷ total plays. With a small number of trials it can be far from the true probability.","trap":"Treating a few lucky wins as proof the machine is generous. Small samples vary a lot.","big":"The big idea: relative frequency = number of wins ÷ number of plays. With only a few plays it jumps around a lot.","kupu":["whakamātau tūponotanga","putanga","raraunga"]},{"k":"long","lab":"The long run","cap":"Now play 1,000 times. Watch the red line settle towards the green dashed line: the true chance of winning.","exam":"As the number of trials increases, the relative frequency tends to get closer to the theoretical probability. After 1,000 plays the estimate is far more reliable than after 10.","trap":"Thinking a win is 'due' after lots of losses. Each play is independent.","big":"The big idea: the law of large numbers: the more trials, the closer the relative frequency gets to the theoretical probability.","kupu":["tūponotanga","raraunga"]},{"k":"tree","lab":"Two steps to win","cap":"To win, the claw has to grab the prize AND hold on all the way to the chute. Change each chance and see the tree diagram multiply them.","exam":"Winning needs two independent events: the claw grabs the toy (0.6) and keeps hold of it (0.25). P(win) = 0.6 × 0.25 = 0.15, so on average 15 wins in every 100 plays.","trap":"Adding the probabilities instead of multiplying them. 'And' means multiply.","big":"The big idea: for independent events, P(A and B) = P(A) × P(B). Two decent chances multiply into a small one.","kupu":["pāpono","putanga","hoahoa rākau","tūponotanga"]},{"k":"value","lab":"Is it worth it?","cap":"Each play costs money and each prize is worth something. The expected value tells you what you win or lose per play on average.","exam":"Expected value per play = P(win) × value of prize − cost = 0.15 × $8 − $2 = −$0.80. On average a player loses 80 cents a play, so a prize costs about $2 ÷ 0.15 ≈ $13.33 to win.","trap":"Comparing the prize value with one play's cost. Compare it with the cost of all the plays it takes to win.","big":"The big idea: expected value = P(win) × prize value − cost. If it's negative, you lose money on average the more you play.","kupu":["uara","tūponotanga"]},{"k":"rigged","lab":"Random or rigged?","cap":"Some claw machines can be set to let a win through every so many plays. Switch to payout mode and watch the pattern in the results.","exam":"In a random process, wins are scattered unpredictably and each play is independent. If wins happen at regular intervals (every 12th play), the outcomes are not independent, and the probability changes depending on how many plays since the last win.","trap":"Assuming every game of chance is fair and random. Check the data for patterns.","big":"The big idea: a random process has no memory. A machine set to pay out on a schedule does: the pattern gives it away in the data.","kupu":["tauanga","raraunga"]},{"k":"test","lab":"Test yourself","cap":"Three challenges. Use the controls, then press Check.","exam":"","trap":"","big":"Exam tip: show the calculation: P(A) × P(B) for 'and', wins ÷ plays for relative frequency, and P × value − cost for expected value.","kupu":[]}],"challenges":[{"q":"Set the chances so P(win) is exactly 0.2.","check":"()=>Math.abs(s.pGrab*s.pHold-0.2)<0.001&&s.mode===\"random\"","ok":"()=>`Yes: ${fmt(s.pGrab,2)} × ${fmt(s.pHold,2)} = 0.2.`","no":"()=>`Not yet: ${fmt(s.pGrab,2)} × ${fmt(s.pHold,2)} = ${fmt(s.pGrab*s.pHold,3)}. Try 0.8 × 0.25 or 0.4 × 0.5.`","okIsFn":true,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"With $2 plays and P(win) = 0.15, what's the smallest whole-dollar prize value that makes the expected value zero or better? Set it.","check":"()=>s.cost===2&&Math.abs(pWin()-0.15)<0.001&&s.prize===14","ok":"Yes: 0.15 × $14 = $2.10, which beats the $2 cost. $13 gives $1.95.","no":"()=>s.cost!==2||Math.abs(pWin()-0.15)>0.001?\"Set $2 a play and P(win) = 0.15 (0.6 and 0.25) first.\":`Not yet: 0.15 × $${s.prize} = $${fmt(0.15*s.prize,2)}. You need at least $2.`","okIsFn":false,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"Play at least 1,000 times and get a relative frequency within 0.02 of P(win).","check":"()=>s.plays>=1000&&Math.abs(s.wins/s.plays-pWin())<=0.02","ok":"()=>`Yes: ${fmt(s.wins/s.plays,3)} vs ${fmt(pWin(),3)} after ${fmt(s.plays)} plays.`","no":"()=>s.plays<1000?`Only ${fmt(s.plays)} plays so far.`:`Off by ${fmt(Math.abs(s.wins/s.plays-pWin()),3)}. Reset and try again; more plays help.`","okIsFn":true,"noIsFn":true}],"controls":[{"type":"button","id":"play1","label":"▶ Play once"},{"type":"button","id":"play10","label":"Play 10"},{"type":"button","id":"play100","label":"Play 100"},{"type":"button","id":"play1000","label":"Play 1,000"},{"type":"button","id":"reset","label":"Reset"},{"type":"range","id":"pGrab","label":"P(claw grabs it)"},{"type":"range","id":"pHold","label":"P(holds on)"},{"type":"range","id":"cost","label":"Cost a play"},{"type":"range","id":"prize","label":"Prize worth"},{"type":"select","id":"mode","label":"Machine"},{"type":"range","id":"every","label":"Win every"}],"shell":"kit"},"ai":{"id":"ai","title":"Train your own AI","href":"m/ai.html","desc":"Draw two kinds of thing and teach a real machine-learning model to tell them apart. Test it fairly, then find and fix its bias. Runs on your device; nothing is sent anywhere.","blurb":"Teach a real machine-learning model, test it fairly, then fix its bias. Runs on your device.","tags":["Year 9","Year 10","Digital technologies","AI"],"built":true,"eyebrow":"Digital technologies · Artificial intelligence","crumb":"Years 9–10 Technology and Digital Technologies: data, algorithms and AI ethics","badge":{"label":"AI and digital tech · Years 9–11"},"papers":[{"label":"NZQA: Digital Technologies","url":"https://www2.nzqa.govt.nz/ncea/subjects/subject/digital-technologies/"}],"truth":"A real machine-learning method (k-nearest neighbours, k = 3) running entirely on this page. Your drawings stay on your device and are gone when you leave. The built-in test set is generated drawings, not anyone's data.","footer":"Covers Years 9–10 Technology (computational thinking, data and algorithms, the ethics and bias of digital systems). Leads into NCEA Digital Technologies and the new Applied Intelligent Systems subject planned from 2029.","mainLabel":"A drawing pad on the left, your training examples on the right and the AI's guess","kupu":[{"mi":"rorohiko","en":"computer","ta":6926,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"guess"},{"mi":"raraunga","en":"data","ta":6555,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"draw, score"},{"mi":"hātepe","en":"algorithm","ta":917,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Reo Pāngarau (MoE 2010) cited in entry","stepsNote":"guess"},{"mi":"whakangungu","en":"to train, training","ta":9686,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"draw"},{"mi":"tauira","en":"example, pattern, model","ta":7706,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"draw"},{"mi":"tīpako","en":"sample","ta":8150,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Reo Pāngarau (MoE 2010) cited in entry","stepsNote":"score"},{"mi":"tīpako haukume","en":"biased sample","ta":46051,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Reo Pāngarau (MoE 2010) cited in entry","stepsNote":"bias"},{"mi":"tīpako matapōkere","en":"random sample","ta":46052,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"bias"}],"steps":[{"k":"draw","lab":"Teach it","cap":"You're the teacher. Draw a fish, press Add as Fish. Draw a wheel, press Add as Wheel. Give it at least five of each, all a bit different.","exam":"Machine learning is when a computer learns patterns from training data instead of being given step-by-step rules. The quality and variety of the training data decides how well the model works on new examples.","trap":"Thinking the computer 'understands' what a fish is. It only compares numbers with the examples it was given.","big":"The big idea: an AI like this doesn't follow rules you write. It learns from examples, so the examples you give it decide what it can do.","kupu":["whakangungu","tauira","raraunga"]},{"k":"guess","lab":"Let it guess","cap":"Now draw something new and press Guess. The AI compares your drawing with every example and votes using the three closest.","exam":"To classify a new drawing, the model converts it into numbers and finds the training examples with the smallest distance. The most common label among those nearest neighbours becomes the prediction.","trap":"Treating confidence as certainty. 90% sure can still be wrong.","big":"The big idea: this AI is a nearest-neighbour classifier: it says 'this looks most like those', using distance between grids of numbers.","kupu":["hātepe","rorohiko"]},{"k":"score","lab":"Test it fairly","cap":"Don't just test with your own drawings. Press Run the test set: 40 drawings the AI has never seen, half fish and half wheels.","exam":"A model is evaluated on a separate test set that was not used in training, so its accuracy reflects how well it generalises to new data rather than how well it memorised the training data.","trap":"Testing on the same examples you trained with. That only shows it memorised them.","big":"The big idea: we judge an AI on test data it wasn't trained on. Doing well on the training data proves very little.","kupu":["tīpako","raraunga"]},{"k":"bias","lab":"Find the bias","cap":"Load a lopsided dataset: every training drawing is big and in the top-left corner. Then draw a small one in the bottom-right and ask for a guess. Watch it fail, then fix it.","exam":"Bias in an AI system can come from unrepresentative training data. If every training example is large and in one corner, the model has not learned what a fish looks like elsewhere. Adding varied examples, or centring and scaling each drawing first, reduces this bias.","trap":"Blaming the user for drawing it 'wrong'. The model should be trained on the variety of inputs real people will give it.","big":"The big idea: if the training data only covers some situations, the AI fails in the rest. That's bias, and the fix is better data or better features, not a cleverer excuse.","kupu":["tīpako haukume","tīpako matapōkere"]},{"k":"test","lab":"Test yourself","cap":"Three challenges. Use what you've learned.","exam":"","trap":"","big":"Real AIs: image and language models learn the same way, from examples, just with billions of numbers instead of 256. Their training data decides what they're good at and where they're biased.","kupu":[]}],"challenges":[{"q":"Get three right guesses in a row on your own new drawings.","check":"()=>s.streak>=3","ok":"Yes. More varied examples make it more reliable.","no":"()=>`Your streak is ${s.streak}. Add more varied examples, then keep testing.`","okIsFn":false,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"Teach it well enough to score at least 80% on the test set (with centring off).","check":"()=>!s.center&&s.acc!=null&&s.acc>=0.8&&!s.biasLoaded","ok":"()=>`Yes: ${fmt(s.acc*100)}%. Your examples covered enough variety.`","no":"()=>s.biasLoaded?\"Clear the lopsided dataset first: it's loaded. Reload the page or teach it from scratch.\":s.acc==null?\"Run the test set first.\":`Not yet: ${fmt(s.acc*100)}%. Add examples in different sizes and places.`","okIsFn":true,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"Load the lopsided dataset, then get it above 75% on the test set without adding any examples.","check":"()=>s.biasLoaded&&s.ex[0].length===8&&s.ex[1].length===8&&s.acc>=0.75","ok":"()=>`Yes: ${fmt(s.acc*100)}%. Centring and scaling removed the position and size bias.`","no":"()=>!s.biasLoaded?\"Press Load a lopsided dataset first.\":`Not yet: ${s.acc==null?\"run the test set\":fmt(s.acc*100)+\"%\"}. What setting stops position and size mattering?`","okIsFn":true,"noIsFn":true}],"controls":[{"type":"button","id":"addA","label":"Add as Fish"},{"type":"button","id":"addB","label":"Add as Wheel"},{"type":"button","id":"guess","label":"Guess"},{"type":"button","id":"right","label":"✓ It was right"},{"type":"button","id":"wrong","label":"✗ It was wrong"},{"type":"button","id":"clear","label":"Clear drawing"},{"type":"button","id":"score","label":"Run the test set"},{"type":"button","id":"bias","label":"Load a lopsided dataset"},{"type":"toggle","id":"center","label":"Centre and scale each drawing"},{"type":"select","id":"nameA","label":"First thing"},{"type":"select","id":"nameB","label":"Second thing"}],"shell":"kit"},"vinyl":{"id":"vinyl","title":"The vinyl machine","href":"m/vinyl.html","desc":"A turntable under a magnifier: circumference and rpm, the groove as a wave, why sped-up songs sound higher, sound waves in air, decibels and safe listening. With real tones to hear.","blurb":"Circumference, rpm, sound waves and safe decibels on a turntable, plus NCEA circular motion.","tags":["Year 9","Year 10","Science","Maths","NCEA L2 Physics 91171","NCEA L3 Physics 91524"],"built":true,"eyebrow":"Science and maths · Waves, sound and circles","crumb":"Year 9 and 10 Science (waves and sound) and Maths (circles, rates and ratios), plus three NCEA Level 2–3 steps on circular motion (AS91171, AS91524)","badge":{"label":"Years 9–11 · NCEA L2 91171 · L3 91524"},"papers":[{"label":"NZQA: Physics, Earth and Space Science","url":"https://www2.nzqa.govt.nz/ncea/subjects/past-exams-and-exemplars/physics-earth-and-space-science/"},{"label":"NZQA: Mathematics and Statistics","url":"https://www2.nzqa.govt.nz/ncea/subjects/past-exams-and-exemplars/"},{"label":"NZQA: L2 Physics 91171 (mechanics)","url":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/ncea/assessment/view-detailed.do?standardNumber=91171"},{"label":"NZQA: L3 Physics 91524 (mechanical systems)","url":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/ncea/assessment/view-detailed.do?standardNumber=91524"}],"truth":"The NCEA steps use the practice pack's radii (0.146 m LP, 0.088 m single); the platter's 1.5 kg and 0.15 m for the Level 3 figures are estimates. A 12-inch LP with grooves from about 14.6 cm to 6.0 cm from the centre. Speed of sound 343 m/s (air at 20°C). Safe listening uses the WHO guide of about 80 dB for up to 40 hours a week for adults (lower for children), halving for every 3 dB louder. The groove drawing is exaggerated so you can see it, and the waves are slowed down.","footer":"Covers Year 9–10 waves and sound (frequency, wavelength, amplitude, v = fλ, longitudinal waves, decibels) and maths (circumference, rates, ratios). Waves ideas lead into NCEA physics. The three NCEA steps cover circular motion for L2 AS91171 (ω = 2πf, v = rω, a = v²/r) and the rotational ideas of L3 AS91524 (I, L = Iω, ½Iω²).","mainLabel":"A turntable with a record spinning, a magnifier showing the groove's wiggles, and a speaker pushing air","kupu":[{"mi":"oro","en":"sound","ta":4804,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"air, loud"},{"mi":"ngaru","en":"wave","ta":4584,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"groove"},{"mi":"ngaru pou","en":"longitudinal wave","ta":40248,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Reo Pūtaiao (MoE 2009) cited in entry","stepsNote":"air"},{"mi":"auau","en":"frequency","ta":523,"note":"","chip":["f","heard"],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"groove, pitch"},{"mi":"hauoro","en":"pitch (of a sound)","ta":39275,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Reo Pūtaiao (MoE 2009) cited in entry","stepsNote":"pitch"},{"mi":"tōiriiri","en":"vibration","ta":15290,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Reo Pūtaiao (MoE 2009) cited in entry","stepsNote":"groove, air"},{"mi":"kahaoro","en":"volume, loudness","ta":45548,"note":"","chip":["db"],"evidence":"Te Reo o Ngā Toi Puoro (MoE 2015) cited in entry","stepsNote":"loud"},{"mi":"pūngao oro","en":"sound energy","ta":47724,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"air"}],"steps":[{"k":"spin","lab":"Spin it","cap":"A record turns at 33⅓ revolutions per minute. Every groove takes the same time to go round, but the outer groove has further to travel.","exam":"At 33⅓ rpm the record makes 33⅓ ÷ 60 = 0.556 turns a second. The outer groove (r = 0.146 m) has a circumference of 2π × 0.146 = 0.917 m, so it moves at 0.917 × 0.556 ≈ 0.51 m/s. The inner groove (r = 0.06 m) moves at only 0.21 m/s.","trap":"Using the diameter in C = 2πr. Use the radius, or use C = πd.","big":"The big idea: one turn covers one circumference, C = 2πr. Same turns a second, bigger circle, faster speed.","kupu":[]},{"k":"groove","lab":"The groove is a wave","cap":"Under the magnifier, the groove wiggles. One full wiggle is one wavelength. Higher notes squeeze more wiggles into each millimetre.","exam":"The groove passes the needle at about 0.51 m/s near the edge. For a 440 Hz note, the wavelength in the groove is λ = v ÷ f = 0.51 ÷ 440 ≈ 0.0012 m, about 1.2 mm. Near the centre the groove moves slower, so the same note is squeezed into a shorter wavelength.","trap":"Mixing up frequency and wavelength. Higher frequency means a shorter wavelength when the speed is the same.","big":"The big idea: wave speed = frequency × wavelength (v = fλ). Here the 'wave speed' is how fast the groove passes the needle.","kupu":["ngaru","auau","tōiriiri"]},{"k":"pitch","lab":"Sped-up songs","cap":"Play a 33⅓ record at 45 rpm and everything happens 1.35 times faster: every frequency goes up, so every note sounds higher. That's how a sped-up song works.","exam":"Playing a 33⅓ rpm record at 45 rpm multiplies every frequency by 45 ÷ 33⅓ = 1.35. A 440 Hz note (A) is heard at 440 × 1.35 = 594 Hz, about five semitones higher (near D). Higher frequency means higher pitch.","trap":"Thinking louder means higher. Loudness is amplitude; pitch is frequency.","big":"The big idea: pitch depends on frequency. Speed the record up by a ratio and every frequency is multiplied by the same ratio.","kupu":["auau","hauoro"]},{"k":"air","lab":"Sound in the air","cap":"The speaker pushes the air back and forth. Bunched-up air (compressions) and spread-out air (rarefactions) travel away at 343 m/s. Slide the frequency and watch the wavelength change.","exam":"Sound is a longitudinal wave: air particles vibrate parallel to the direction the wave travels, making compressions and rarefactions. The speed of sound in air is about 343 m/s, so a 440 Hz note has a wavelength of λ = v ÷ f = 343 ÷ 440 ≈ 0.78 m.","trap":"Thinking the air itself travels from the speaker to your ear. The particles vibrate in place; the energy travels.","big":"The big idea: sound is a longitudinal wave: air particles vibrate back and forth in the same direction the wave travels. v = fλ.","kupu":["oro","ngaru pou","tōiriiri","pūngao oro"]},{"k":"loud","lab":"Loud and safe","cap":"Bigger vibrations mean more energy and a louder sound, measured in decibels. Every 3 dB more doubles the energy, so your safe listening time halves.","exam":"A louder sound has a larger amplitude, so the particles vibrate further and carry more energy. On the decibel scale each increase of 10 dB is ten times the sound energy, and about +3 dB doubles it, so the safe listening time halves.","trap":"Thinking 100 dB is only a bit more than 80 dB. It is about 100 times the sound energy.","big":"The big idea: loudness depends on amplitude. Decibels are a scale where +10 dB is ten times the sound energy.","kupu":["kahaoro","oro"]},{"k":"omega","lab":"NCEA: angular velocity","cap":"Level 2 and 3. Physicists measure spin in radians a second: ω = 2πf. Show that 33⅓ rpm is about 3.5 rad/s. The pink arrow is the velocity of a dust speck on the groove; the mint arrow is its acceleration.","exam":"f = 33.33 ÷ 60 = 0.556 Hz, so ω = 2πf = 2π × 0.556 = 3.49 rad s⁻¹. The period is T = 1 ÷ f = 1.8 s. (Level 3: a 1.5 kg, 0.15 m platter has I = ½MR² = 0.017 kg m², so L = Iω = 0.059 kg m² s⁻¹.)","trap":"Putting rpm straight into ω. Divide by 60 for revolutions a second, then multiply by 2π for radians a second.","big":"The big idea: one turn is 2π radians, so ω = 2πf = 2π ÷ T. Level 3 adds the platter's rotational inertia I = ½MR², angular momentum L = Iω and rotational kinetic energy ½Iω².","kupu":["auau"]},{"k":"centripetal","lab":"NCEA: centripetal acceleration","cap":"A dust speck on the outer groove moves at v = rω and, because its direction keeps changing, it accelerates toward the centre at a = v²/r. Move the needle and watch both arrows.","exam":"v = rω = 0.146 × 3.49 = 0.51 m s⁻¹. a = v² ÷ r = 0.51² ÷ 0.146 ≈ 1.78 m s⁻², directed toward the centre of the record. The friction between the speck and the vinyl provides the centripetal force F = ma.","trap":"Drawing the acceleration along the velocity. In uniform circular motion the acceleration is perpendicular to the velocity, pointing at the centre.","big":"The big idea: uniform circular motion has constant speed but changing velocity, so there is an acceleration, toward the centre, of size v²/r = ω²r. Something must supply the force (friction, for the speck).","kupu":["auau"]},{"k":"formats","lab":"NCEA: LP vs single","cap":"A 7-inch single spins faster (45 rpm) but its outer groove is at only r = 0.088 m. Compare the track speed with the LP's, then use v = rω to explain why inner grooves carry less information per second.","exam":"Single: ω = 2π × 45 ÷ 60 = 4.71 rad s⁻¹, v = rω = 0.088 × 4.71 = 0.41 m s⁻¹, slower than the LP's outer groove (0.51 m s⁻¹) despite the faster spin, because v = rω scales with radius. On any record the inner grooves pass the stylus at lower v, so fewer metres of groove pass each second and less waveform detail fits: inner grooves carry less information per second.","trap":"Assuming faster rpm means a faster groove. Compare rω, not ω: a small record at 45 rpm can be slower at the edge than a big one at 33⅓.","big":"The big idea: v = rω: at the same ω, speed scales with radius. More groove passing the stylus each second means more room for waveform detail, which is why engineers keep the loudest tracks near the outer edge.","kupu":[]},{"k":"test","lab":"Test yourself","cap":"Six challenges: three on sound, three on circular motion. Use the controls, then press Check.","exam":"","trap":"","big":"Exam tip: write the formula, put the numbers in with units, then say what it means: \"so the note sounds higher\".","kupu":[]}],"challenges":[{"q":"Play a 33⅓ record so that a 440 Hz A is heard at about 594 Hz.","check":"()=>Math.abs(heard()-594)<4","ok":"()=>`Yes: 440 × ${fmt(pitchRatio(),3)} = ${fmt(heard())} Hz. That's 45 ÷ 33⅓ = 1.35.`","no":"()=>`Not yet: you hear ${fmt(heard())} Hz. Which turntable speed multiplies 440 by 1.35?`","okIsFn":true,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"Find a needle position where a 1000 Hz note is exactly 0.30 mm long in the groove (33⅓ rpm).","check":"()=>Math.abs(s.rpm-100/3)<0.01&&s.f===1000&&Math.abs(speedAt(s.r)/s.f*1000-0.30)<0.02","ok":"()=>`Yes: λ = v ÷ f = ${fmt(speedAt(s.r),3)} ÷ 1000 = ${fmt(speedAt(s.r)/s.f*1000,2)} mm at ${fmt(s.r*100,1)} cm.`","no":"()=>s.f!==1000||Math.abs(s.rpm-100/3)>0.01?\"Set 33⅓ rpm and a 1000 Hz note first.\":`Not yet: ${fmt(speedAt(s.r)/s.f*1000,2)} mm. Move the needle: closer to the centre is slower and shorter.`","okIsFn":true,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"Set the loudness where the safe listening time is about 10 hours a week.","check":"()=>Math.abs(40*Math.pow(2,-(s.db-80)/3)-10)<2","ok":"()=>`Yes: ${s.db} dB. From 80 dB, +6 dB is two doublings, so 40 h ÷ 4 = 10 h.`","no":"()=>`Not yet: ${s.db} dB allows about ${fmt(40*Math.pow(2,-(s.db-80)/3),1)} h. Each +3 dB halves the time from 40 h at 80 dB.`","okIsFn":true,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"NCEA: set the turntable so the angular velocity is about 4.7 rad/s.","check":"()=>Math.abs(omega()-4.71)<0.1","ok":"()=>`Yes: ${fmt(s.rpm,1)} rpm = ${fmt(rps(),3)} Hz, ω = 2π × ${fmt(rps(),3)} = ${fmt(omega(),2)} rad/s.`","no":"()=>`Not yet: ω = ${fmt(omega(),2)} rad/s at ${fmt(s.rpm,1)} rpm. Which speed gives f = 4.7 ÷ 2π = 0.75 Hz?`","okIsFn":true,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"NCEA: on the LP at 33⅓ rpm, put the needle where the centripetal acceleration is 1.0 m/s².","check":"()=>s.format===\"lp\"&&Math.abs(s.rpm-100/3)<0.01&&Math.abs(acen(s.r)-1.0)<0.03","ok":"()=>`Yes: a = ω²r = ${fmt(omega(),2)}² × ${fmt(s.r,3)} = ${fmt(acen(s.r),2)} m/s² at ${fmt(s.r*100,1)} cm.`","no":"()=>s.format!==\"lp\"||Math.abs(s.rpm-100/3)>0.01?\"Choose the 12-inch LP at 33⅓ first.\":`Not yet: ${fmt(acen(s.r),2)} m/s² at ${fmt(s.r*100,1)} cm. a = ω²r, so r = 1.0 ÷ ${fmt(omega()*omega(),2)} = ${fmt(100/(omega()*omega()),1)} cm.`","okIsFn":true,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"NCEA: find the LP needle position (33⅓ rpm) where the groove speed equals the 7-inch single's outer groove at 45 rpm.","check":"()=>s.format===\"lp\"&&Math.abs(s.rpm-100/3)<0.01&&Math.abs(vlin(s.r)-R_SINGLE*2*Math.PI*0.75)<0.012","ok":"()=>`Yes: the single's edge moves at 0.088 × 4.71 = ${fmt(R_SINGLE*2*Math.PI*0.75,2)} m/s; on the LP that is r = v ÷ ω = ${fmt(R_SINGLE*2*Math.PI*0.75/omega()*100,1)} cm, well inside the edge.`","no":"()=>s.format!==\"lp\"||Math.abs(s.rpm-100/3)>0.01?\"Choose the 12-inch LP at 33⅓ first.\":`Not yet: ${fmt(vlin(s.r),2)} m/s at ${fmt(s.r*100,1)} cm. The single's edge does 0.41 m/s; r = v ÷ ω.`","okIsFn":true,"noIsFn":true}],"controls":[{"type":"play","id":"play","label":""},{"type":"select","id":"rpm","label":"Turntable"},{"type":"range","id":"f","label":"Note recorded"},{"type":"range","id":"needle","label":"Needle position"},{"type":"range","id":"amp","label":"Volume (amplitude)"},{"type":"range","id":"db","label":"Loudness"},{"type":"button","id":"hear","label":"▶ Hear it"},{"type":"select","id":"format","label":"Record"}],"shell":"kit"},"bike":{"id":"bike","title":"The downhill machine","href":"m/bike.html","desc":"Drop into a redwood-forest downhill trail with a helmet cam: speed–time graphs, balanced forces, energy into brake heat, a gap jump, stopping distance in the mud and a suspension spring.","blurb":"Speed–time graphs, forces, brake heat and stopping distance on a redwood trail, plus an NCEA ski run.","tags":["Year 9","Year 10","Science","Forces and motion","NCEA L2 Physics 91171","NCEA L3 Physics 91524"],"built":true,"eyebrow":"Science · Forces and motion","crumb":"Year 9 and 10 Science · forces, motion and energy, with NCEA Level 1 ideas, plus four NCEA Level 2–3 steps on a ski run (AS91171, AS91524)","badge":{"label":"Years 9–11 · NCEA L2 91171 · L3 91524"},"papers":[{"label":"NZQA: Physics, Earth and Space Science","url":"https://www2.nzqa.govt.nz/ncea/subjects/past-exams-and-exemplars/physics-earth-and-space-science/"},{"label":"NZQA: Science past exams","url":"https://www2.nzqa.govt.nz/ncea/subjects/past-exams-and-exemplars/"},{"label":"NZQA: L2 Physics 91171 (mechanics)","url":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/ncea/assessment/view-detailed.do?standardNumber=91171"},{"label":"NZQA: L3 Physics 91524 (mechanical systems)","url":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/ncea/assessment/view-detailed.do?standardNumber=91524"}],"truth":"The NCEA ski-run steps use the practice pack's figures (120 m drop over 400 m, 72 kg, 34 m/s); the turn's radius, entry speed and snow grip μ are estimates, and the skier is animated at three times real time with a constant average acceleration. A fictional trail inspired by the Redwoods, Whakarewarewa Forest, Rotorua. Uses g = 9.8 N/kg and air density 1.2 kg/m³. Bike masses (14.5–18.4 kg), drag areas (0.47–0.65 m²), rolling resistance (0.03–0.08) and tyre grip (μ 0.22–0.78) are typical estimates, not measurements of real bikes. The fork spring also stands in for your legs, so k is stiffer than a real fork spring. Rotor temperatures assume all the braking heat stays in the rotors (a top estimate; real rotors also lose heat to the air). The rider brakes to hold the speed you choose. Bikes and riders are drawn larger than the trail so you can see them.","footer":"Covers Year 9–10 forces and motion: mass and weight, speed, distance–time and speed–time graphs, balanced and unbalanced forces, a = F/m, Ep and Ek, heat and Q = mcΔT, projectiles, stopping distance, pressure and Hooke's law. Energy ideas link to NCEA L1 AS 92047. The four NCEA steps cover L2 AS91171 (energy conservation, work, force components, circular motion) and L3 AS91524 (circular motion with multiple forces, banked turns).","mainLabel":"A downhill mountain bike and rider in a redwood forest, with a helmet camera view and force arrows","kupu":[{"mi":"tōpana","en":"force","ta":40375,"note":"","chip":["net"],"evidence":"Te Reo Pūtaiao (MoE 2009) cited in entry","stepsNote":"forces, fork"},{"mi":"tōpana taurite","en":"balanced forces","ta":40395,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"forces"},{"mi":"tere","en":"speed","ta":7953,"note":"","chip":["speed","avg","lip"],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"drop, graph, jump, stop"},{"mi":"whakahohoro","en":"acceleration, speeding up","ta":12447,"note":"","chip":["accel"],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"drop, graph"},{"mi":"pūngao","en":"energy","ta":40075,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Reo Pūtaiao (MoE 2009) cited in entry","stepsNote":"energy"},{"mi":"pūngao neke","en":"kinetic energy","ta":47721,"note":"","chip":["ek"],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"energy"},{"mi":"pūngao moe","en":"potential energy","ta":47720,"note":"","chip":["ep"],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"energy"},{"mi":"waku","en":"friction","ta":12421,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Reo Pūtaiao (MoE 2009) cited in entry","stepsNote":"garage, forces, stop"}],"steps":[{"k":"garage","lab":"The garage","cap":"Choose a bike and build it. Every part changes a real number in the physics, and the rest of the machine uses your build.","exam":"Mass is the amount of matter in an object, measured in kilograms. Weight is the force of gravity on that mass, measured in newtons: W = mg. A 72 kg rider and bike has a weight of 72 × 9.8 ≈ 706 N on Earth.","trap":"Mixing up mass and weight. Mass is in kg and is the same everywhere. Weight is a force in N and depends on gravity (W = mg).","big":"The big idea: mass, grip, rolling resistance, suspension stiffness and air resistance are all numbers you can change. Change the bike and every later step changes with it.","kupu":["waku"]},{"k":"drop","lab":"Drop in","cap":"Press play to drop into the trail. The helmet cam shows your speed. Speed = distance ÷ time: watch how far you go each second.","exam":"Average speed = distance ÷ time. If the rider covers 248 m in 22 s, the average speed is 248 ÷ 22 ≈ 11.3 m/s, which is 11.3 × 3.6 ≈ 41 km/h. The instantaneous speed is higher on the steep chute and lower in the berm.","trap":"Mixing units. Change km/h to m/s by dividing by 3.6 before using it in a formula.","big":"The big idea: average speed = total distance ÷ total time. Your speed at any moment (instantaneous speed) changes all the way down.","kupu":["tere","whakahohoro"]},{"k":"graph","lab":"Speed–time graph","cap":"The speed–time graph builds as you ride. Steep parts are big accelerations. Flat parts mean steady speed, where you're dragging the brakes. Change your build in the garage and race your last run.","exam":"On a speed–time graph the gradient shows acceleration: a = change in speed ÷ time. A horizontal line means constant speed, so the acceleration is zero. The area under the graph is the distance travelled.","trap":"Reading a distance–time graph as if it were speed–time. A flat line on distance–time means stopped; on speed–time it means moving at a steady speed.","big":"The big idea: on a speed–time graph, the gradient is the acceleration and the area under the line is the distance travelled.","kupu":["tere","whakahohoro"]},{"k":"forces","lab":"Forces on the rider","cap":"Arrows show the forces on the rider. When your speed is steady, the down-slope pull is cancelled by friction, air and brakes. Slide the speed limit up to let go of the brakes, or sit up and feel the air.","exam":"When the rider travels at a constant speed the forces along the slope are balanced: the component of weight down the slope equals friction, air resistance and the braking force, so the net force is zero and there is no acceleration. When the rider releases the brakes, the forces become unbalanced and the rider accelerates (a = F_net ÷ m).","trap":"Thinking you need a forward force to keep moving at a steady speed. At constant speed the forces are balanced.","big":"The big idea: balanced forces mean steady speed (or staying still). Unbalanced forces change speed: a = F ÷ m.","kupu":["tōpana","tōpana taurite","waku"]},{"k":"energy","lab":"Where the energy goes","cap":"Every metre you drop turns gravitational potential energy into other forms. Watch how much becomes speed and how much becomes heat in your brake rotors.","exam":"As the rider descends, gravitational potential energy (Ep = mgh) is transformed into kinetic energy (Ek = ½mv²). Some energy is transformed into heat in the brake rotors and by friction and air resistance, so Ek at the bottom is less than the Ep lost. Energy is not used up; it changes form.","trap":"Saying energy is 'lost' or 'used up'. Say it is transformed (changed) into heat and sound.","big":"The big idea: energy is conserved. Ep lost = Ek gained + heat (brakes, friction and air resistance).","kupu":["pūngao","pūngao moe","pūngao neke"]},{"k":"jump","lab":"The gap jump","cap":"At the lip you become a projectile. Gravity pulls you down at 9.8 m/s² while you keep moving forwards. Too slow and you'll case the landing. Your fork catches the landing.","exam":"Once the rider leaves the lip, the only significant force is gravity (plus a little air resistance). The rider keeps their horizontal speed but accelerates downwards at 9.8 m/s², so the path is a curve. A higher speed at the lip means the rider travels further horizontally before landing.","trap":"Thinking a heavier rider falls faster in the air. Without air resistance, mass does not change how fast you fall.","big":"The big idea: in the air the only forces are gravity and air resistance. Faster at the lip means more distance before you land.","kupu":["tere"]},{"k":"stop","lab":"Stopping distance","cap":"A log has fallen across the trail 25 m ahead. You need time to react, then the brakes need distance to stop you. Try mud, wet roots, more speed, a slower reaction, or different tyres in the garage.","exam":"Stopping distance = thinking distance + braking distance. Thinking distance = speed × reaction time, so it doubles when speed doubles. Braking distance depends on speed squared, so doubling the speed makes it about four times longer. Mud lowers the friction between the tyres and the trail, so the braking distance increases.","trap":"Assuming double the speed means double the braking distance. It's four times as far.","big":"The big idea: stopping distance = thinking distance + braking distance. Braking distance grows with speed squared.","kupu":["waku","tere"]},{"k":"fork","lab":"Suspension spring","cap":"Drop off a ledge and the fork spring catches you. A stiffer spring compresses less. Too soft and you bottom out the 200 mm of travel on your DROP.","exam":"The fork spring obeys Hooke's law, F = kx: the force is proportional to the compression. A spring with a larger spring constant k compresses less for the same force. On landing, the rider's kinetic energy is transferred into elastic potential energy in the spring, and the damper turns some of it into heat so the bike doesn't bounce.","trap":"Using millimetres in F = kx when k is in N/m. Convert to metres first: 120 mm = 0.12 m.","big":"The big idea: a spring obeys Hooke's law, F = kx. It stores energy when it's squashed (elastic potential energy) and gives it back.","kupu":["tōpana"]},{"k":"run","lab":"NCEA: the ski run","cap":"Level 2 and 3. Think Coronet Peak: a run dropping 120 m over 400 m of track. A 72 kg skier reaches the bottom at 34 m/s. Press play; the bars show the energy the slope gave and where it went.","exam":"Ep = mgh = 72 × 9.8 × 120 = 8.47 × 10⁴ J. At the bottom Ek = ½mv² = 0.5 × 72 × 34² = 4.16 × 10⁴ J, so (8.47 − 4.16) ÷ 8.47 ≈ 51 % of the original energy was dissipated by friction and air resistance.","trap":"Using the track length (400 m) as h in mgh. Only the vertical drop counts for gravitational potential energy.","big":"The big idea: energy conservation with a leak. Ep lost = Ek gained + work done against friction and air. The gap between mgh and ½mv² is what the run dissipated.","kupu":["pūngao moe","pūngao neke","waku"]},{"k":"work","lab":"NCEA: forces on the slope","cap":"Where did 43 kJ go? Work = force × distance along the track. Resolve the skier's weight: the component down the slope drives the run; an average resistive force opposes it.","exam":"sin θ = 120 ÷ 400 = 0.30, so θ = 17.5°. The component of weight down the slope is mg sin θ = 72 × 9.8 × 0.30 = 212 N. The 4.31 × 10⁴ J dissipated over 400 m gives an average resistive force of W ÷ d = 108 N, so the net force is about 104 N and the average acceleration is F ÷ m ≈ 1.4 m s⁻².","trap":"Resolving with cos instead of sin. Down the slope is mg sin θ; the normal force is mg cos θ.","big":"The big idea: resolve weight into mg sin θ (down the slope) and mg cos θ (into the slope). Work done against resistance W = Fd lets you turn an energy gap into an average force.","kupu":["tōpana","waku","whakahohoro"]},{"k":"model","lab":"NCEA: the frictionless model","cap":"The lab's twin computes an 'exit speed' with v = √(2gh), frictionless. Compare it with the real 34 m/s, and see why a course designer uses trail shape, not just slope, to keep skiers safe on a steeper pitch.","exam":"The frictionless prediction is v = √(2 × 9.8 × 120) = 48.5 m s⁻¹ against the real 34 m s⁻¹, an overestimate of about 43 % in speed, because the model ignores the work done against friction (W = Fd over 400 m) and drag, which grows with v². Designers add traverses, check turns and rolling terrain: shortening the effective fall line converts less Ep per metre travelled, so trail shape, not just slope, sets speed.","trap":"Comparing energies as if they were speeds. Halving the kinetic energy only cuts the speed to 71 % (v ∝ √E).","big":"The big idea: a model is only as good as what it leaves out. Because v ∝ √E, a 51 % energy loss is a 30 % speed loss, and the frictionless number overestimates by about 43 %. Excellence answers quantify the gap and say what to do about it.","kupu":["tere","pūngao"]},{"k":"turn","lab":"NCEA: the check turn","cap":"Level 2 circular motion, with a Level 3 berm. In a turn, friction between edged skis and snow must supply the centripetal force mv²/r. Change the radius, the entry speed and the grip: does the turn hold?","exam":"For a 30 m radius turn with μ = 0.5, the maximum friction is μmg = 0.5 × 72 × 9.8 = 353 N, and the turn needs mv²/r = 72 × 20² ÷ 30 = 960 N at 20 m s⁻¹, so the skier skids out. The fastest speed the turn can hold is v = √(μgr) = √(0.5 × 9.8 × 30) = 12.1 m s⁻¹. Level 3: on a banked turn with no friction, tan θ = v²/(rg).","trap":"Treating centripetal force as a separate force to add to the diagram. Draw the real forces (friction, normal, weight); their inward resultant is the centripetal force.","big":"The big idea: centripetal force is not an extra force; it is the name for the net inward force. On flat snow that is friction, limited to μN, so v max = √(μgr). On a bermed turn part of the normal force points inward too.","kupu":["tōpana","tere"]},{"k":"test","lab":"Test yourself","cap":"Eight challenges: five on the trail and three on the NCEA ski run. Use the controls from any step and the garage, then press Check.","exam":"","trap":"","big":"Exam tip: say which forces act, whether they're balanced, and what that means for the speed. Show the formula, the numbers and the unit.","kupu":[]}],"challenges":[{"q":"Clear the gap and finish in under 25 seconds without crashing. (Go to Drop in, set the brake speed, ride it, then come back.)","check":"()=>!!(s.lastRun&&s.lastRun.cleared&&s.lastRun.time<25)","ok":"()=>`Yes: ${fmt(s.lastRun.time,1)} s on ${s.lastRun.build} ${s.lastRun.limit>=80?\"with no brakes\":\"braking above \"+s.lastRun.limit+\" km/h\"}.`","no":"()=>s.lastRun?`Your last run: ${fmt(s.lastRun.time,1)} s, gap ${s.lastRun.cleared?\"cleared\":\"not cleared\"}. Brake less (raise the brake speed).`:\"No finished run yet. Ride the trail from Drop in.\"","okIsFn":true,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"In the mud at 40 km/h, choose a reaction time that stops you before the log at 25 m.","check":"()=>s.grip===\"mud\"&&s.v0===40&&stopNums().total<25","ok":"()=>`Yes: ${fmt(stopNums().total,1)} m. Braking alone takes ${fmt(stopNums().brake,1)} m in mud on ${WH().n}, so you can only spend ${fmt(25-stopNums().brake,1)} m thinking.`","no":"()=>s.grip!==\"mud\"||s.v0!==40?\"Set the trail to wet and muddy and the speed to 40 km/h first.\":stopNums().brake+11.11*0.3>=25?`Braking alone takes ${fmt(stopNums().brake,1)} m on ${WH().n}, so even the fastest reaction won't do it. Fit grippier wheels in the garage.`:`Not yet: ${fmt(stopNums().total,1)} m. Thinking distance = 11.1 m/s × reaction time.`","okIsFn":true,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"Set the spring so a 1.5 m drop uses between 75% and 95% of your DROP's 200 mm of travel.","check":"()=>s.fh===1.5&&forkNums().x>=0.75*TRAVEL()&&forkNums().x<=0.95*TRAVEL()","ok":"()=>`Yes: ${fmt(forkNums().x*1000)} mm of ${fmt(TRAVEL()*1000)} mm with k = ${s.k} N/mm. Plenty of travel used, no bottoming out.`","no":"()=>s.fh!==1.5?\"Set the drop height to 1.5 m first (Suspension spring step).\":`Not yet: ${fmt(forkNums().x*1000)} mm of ${fmt(TRAVEL()*1000)} mm. ${forkNums().x>0.95*TRAVEL()?\"Stiffer spring (bigger k).\":\"Softer spring (smaller k).\"}`","okIsFn":true,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"Garage: on wet roots at 30 km/h with a 0.5 s reaction time, build a bike that stops within 15 m.","check":"()=>stopNums(30,0.5,\"roots\").total<=15","ok":"()=>{const N=stopNums(30,0.5,\"roots\");return `Yes: ${WH().n} grip μ = ${fmt(N.mu,2)} on roots. ${fmt(N.think,1)} m thinking + ${fmt(N.brake,1)} m braking = ${fmt(N.total,1)} m.`}","no":"()=>{const N=stopNums(30,0.5,\"roots\");return `${WH().n}: μ = ${fmt(N.mu,2)} on roots, ${fmt(N.think,1)} + ${fmt(N.brake,1)} = ${fmt(N.total,1)} m. Braking distance = v² ÷ (2μg), so you need a bigger μ. Swap wheels in the garage.`}","okIsFn":true,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"Garage: FLOW has small 180 mm rotors. Build a FLOW, brake above 45 km/h (or lower) on dry hardpack, and finish a run with the rotors under 125 °C. Which parts help, and why?","check":"()=>!!(s.lastRun&&s.lastRun.model===\"flow\"&&s.lastRun.grip===\"dry\"&&s.lastRun.limit<=45&&s.lastRun.rotorT<ROTOR_MAX)","ok":"()=>`Yes: ${fmt(s.lastRun.rotorT)} °C on ${s.lastRun.build}. Less mass means less Ep to get rid of; heavier rotors need more energy per degree (Q = mcΔT); more rolling resistance and drag take some energy so the brakes don't have to.`","no":"()=>!s.lastRun?\"No finished run yet. Build a FLOW, then ride from Drop in with the brake speed at 45 km/h or less.\":s.lastRun.model!==\"flow\"?`Your last run was on ${s.lastRun.build}. Choose FLOW in the garage, then ride again.`:s.lastRun.grip!==\"dry\"?\"Set the trail to dry hardpack (Forces step) and ride again.\":s.lastRun.limit>45?`Your last run ${s.lastRun.limit>=80?\"had no brakes\":\"braked above \"+s.lastRun.limit+\" km/h\"}. Set the brake speed to 45 km/h or less and ride again.`:`Your last run: ${fmt(s.lastRun.rotorT)} °C on ${s.lastRun.build}. Think Q = mcΔT: what makes Q smaller, or m bigger?`","okIsFn":true,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"NCEA ski run (120 m drop): set a real exit speed at which less than 40% of the energy is dissipated.","check":"()=>s.ski.h===120&&SK.loss()<0.4","ok":"()=>`Yes: at ${fmt(s.ski.v)} m/s, Ek = ${kJ(SK.ek())} of ${kJ(SK.ep())}, so only ${fmt(SK.loss()*100)}% was lost.`","no":"()=>s.ski.h!==120?\"Set the vertical drop back to 120 m first.\":`Not yet: ${fmt(SK.loss()*100)}% lost at ${fmt(s.ski.v)} m/s. You need ½mv² > 0.6 × mgh, so v > √(1.2 × 9.8 × 120) = ${fmt(Math.sqrt(1.2*g*120),1)} m/s.`","okIsFn":true,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"Check turn: with μ = 0.50 and a 20 m/s entry, find the smallest radius (to the nearest 5 m) that holds the turn.","check":"()=>Math.abs(s.ski.mu-0.5)<1e-9&&s.ski.vt===20&&s.ski.r>=SK.rmin()&&s.ski.r<SK.rmin()+5","ok":"()=>`Yes: r min = v² ÷ (μg) = 400 ÷ 4.9 = ${fmt(SK.rmin(),1)} m, so ${fmt(s.ski.r)} m holds: friction ${fmt(s.ski.mu*s.ski.m*g)} N against mv²/r = ${fmt(SK.fc())} N.`","no":"()=>Math.abs(s.ski.mu-0.5)>1e-9||s.ski.vt!==20?\"Set μ = 0.50 and the entry speed to 20 m/s first.\":s.ski.r<SK.rmin()?`Not yet: at ${fmt(s.ski.r)} m the turn needs ${fmt(SK.fc())} N but friction can only give ${fmt(s.ski.mu*s.ski.m*g)} N. r ≥ v² ÷ (μg).`:`That holds, but a smaller radius would too. r min = v² ÷ (μg) = ${fmt(SK.rmin(),1)} m.`","okIsFn":true,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"Trail design: keep the frictionless exit speed √(2gh) under 30 m/s by limiting the vertical drop.","check":"()=>SK.vfree()<30","ok":"()=>`Yes: a ${fmt(s.ski.h)} m drop gives √(2 × 9.8 × ${fmt(s.ski.h)}) = ${fmt(SK.vfree(),1)} m/s even with no friction. That is what traverses do: they split a big drop into small ones.`","no":"()=>`Not yet: ${fmt(s.ski.h)} m gives ${fmt(SK.vfree(),1)} m/s. v² = 2gh, so h must be under 30² ÷ (2 × 9.8) = ${fmt(900/(2*g),1)} m.`","okIsFn":true,"noIsFn":true}],"controls":[{"type":"play","id":"play","label":""},{"type":"button","id":"restart","label":"Restart"},{"type":"toggle","id":"slow","label":"Slow motion"},{"type":"toggle","id":"pose","label":"Attack position"},{"type":"range","id":"limit","label":"Brake above"},{"type":"range","id":"m","label":"Rider mass"},{"type":"select","id":"grip","label":"Trail"},{"type":"range","id":"v0","label":"Speed"},{"type":"range","id":"tr","label":"Reaction time"},{"type":"range","id":"k","label":"Spring"},{"type":"range","id":"fh","label":"Drop height"},{"type":"range","id":"skm","label":"Skier mass"},{"type":"range","id":"skh","label":"Vertical drop"},{"type":"range","id":"skl","label":"Track length"},{"type":"range","id":"skv","label":"Real exit speed"},{"type":"range","id":"skr","label":"Turn radius"},{"type":"range","id":"skvt","label":"Entry speed"},{"type":"range","id":"skmu","label":"Snow grip μ"}],"shell":"kit"},"lines":{"id":"lines","title":"The straight-line machine","href":"m/lines.html","desc":"Two tanks and a plotter: y = mx + c, gradients, intercepts, two lines crossing, inequalities and a scooter-hire showdown.","blurb":"y = mx + c, gradients, intercepts and inequalities with two tanks and a plotter.","tags":["Year 9","Year 10","NCEA L1 Maths 91947"],"built":true,"eyebrow":"Maths · Linear relationships","crumb":"Year 9–10 maths · NCEA Level 1 Mathematics and Statistics","badge":{"label":"NCEA L1 Maths 91947","exam":"2026-11-10T09:30:00+13:00","examText":"Tue 10 Nov, 9:30am"},"papers":[{"label":"NZQA: Mathematics and Statistics","url":"https://www2.nzqa.govt.nz/ncea/subjects/subject/mathematics-and-statistics"}],"truth":"The scooter companies and their prices are made up for practice. Tanks are idealised: the flow rate never changes.","footer":"Linear relationships (y = mx + c, rules from tables, simultaneous equations, inequalities) are Year 9–10 maths content and are examined through reasoning in NCEA Level 1 standard 91947.","mainLabel":"Water tanks filling and draining, with a graph of water against time","kupu":[{"mi":"kauwhata","en":"graph","ta":2460,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"He Pātaka Kupu (Te Taura Whiri 2008) cited in entry","stepsNote":"start"},{"mi":"kauwhata rārangi","en":"line graph","ta":45723,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Reo Pāngarau (MoE 2010) cited in entry","stepsNote":"meet"},{"mi":"rārangi torotika","en":"straight line","ta":45905,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"(machine list only)"},{"mi":"pānga rārangi","en":"linear relationship","ta":45815,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Reo Pāngarau (MoE 2010) cited in entry","stepsNote":"rate, scooter"},{"mi":"rōnaki","en":"gradient, slope","ta":13240,"note":"","chip":["m"],"evidence":"Te Reo Pāngarau (MoE 2010) cited in entry","stepsNote":"rate, drain"},{"mi":"haukotinga","en":"intercept","ta":45643,"note":"","chip":["c"],"evidence":"Te Reo Pāngarau (MoE 2010) cited in entry","stepsNote":"start"},{"mi":"taurangi","en":"variable","ta":7765,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Reo Pūtaiao (MoE 2009) cited in entry","stepsNote":"rule"},{"mi":"whārite","en":"equation","ta":10185,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"He Pātaka Kupu (Te Taura Whiri 2008) cited in entry","stepsNote":"rule, meet"}],"steps":[{"k":"start","lab":"Start level","cap":"Set how much water is in the tank before the tap opens. On the graph, that's where the line starts: the y-intercept.","exam":"The y-intercept is 10, which means the tank starts with 10 litres of water before any time has passed.","trap":"Reading the intercept off the horizontal axis. The y-intercept is on the vertical axis, where time = 0.","big":"The big idea: in y = mx + c, c is the value when x is 0, the starting amount. It's where the line crosses the vertical axis.","kupu":["kauwhata","haukotinga"]},{"k":"rate","lab":"Gradient","cap":"Open the tap wider. More litres every minute makes a steeper line. The gradient is rise over run.","exam":"The gradient is 3 because the water rises by 15 litres over 5 minutes, and 15 ÷ 5 = 3. This means the tank fills at 3 litres per minute.","trap":"Giving the gradient without units or meaning. Say what it is in context: \"3 litres per minute\".","big":"The big idea: the gradient m is the rate of change: how much y goes up for every 1 that x goes up.","kupu":["rōnaki","pānga rārangi"]},{"k":"drain","lab":"Draining","cap":"Make the rate negative and the tank drains. The line slopes down, and where it hits zero is when the tank is empty.","exam":"The rule is L = −4t + 80. The tank is empty when L = 0, so −4t + 80 = 0, which gives t = 20. The tank is empty after 20 minutes.","trap":"Dropping the negative sign. A draining tank must have a negative gradient.","big":"The big idea: a negative gradient means y goes down as x goes up. Solve mx + c = 0 to find when it runs out.","kupu":["rōnaki"]},{"k":"rule","lab":"Find the rule","cap":"Work backwards. Read the start from the table at t = 0 and the gradient from how much it changes each minute.","exam":"From the table, the water increases by 5 litres every minute, so m = 5. At t = 0 there are 20 litres, so c = 20. The rule is L = 5t + 20.","trap":"Using the first value in the table as c when the table doesn't start at x = 0. Work back to x = 0 first.","big":"The big idea: a table of values gives you the rule: the constant difference is m, the value at x = 0 is c.","kupu":["whārite","taurangi"]},{"k":"meet","lab":"Two tanks","cap":"Tank A fills while tank B drains. Where the lines cross, both tanks hold the same amount: the solution to both equations at once.","exam":"The tanks hold the same amount when 3t + 10 = −2t + 80. Adding 2t and subtracting 10 gives 5t = 70, so t = 14. At 14 minutes both tanks hold 52 litres.","trap":"Stopping at t = 14. Finish the answer: substitute back to get the amount, and say what it means.","big":"The big idea (Merit and Excellence): the point where two lines cross solves both equations at once. Set them equal and solve.","kupu":["whārite","kauwhata rārangi"]},{"k":"scooter","lab":"Scooter hire","cap":"Two made-up scooter companies. A charges $1 to unlock plus 40c a minute; B has no unlock fee but 60c a minute. Which is cheaper?","exam":"Scooter A costs C = 0.4t + 1 and scooter B costs C = 0.6t. They cost the same when 0.4t + 1 = 0.6t, so 0.2t = 1 and t = 5. For rides shorter than 5 minutes B is cheaper; for longer rides A is cheaper.","trap":"Answering \"A is cheaper\" without saying when. The answer depends on the length of the ride.","big":"The big idea: the unlock fee is the intercept and the per-minute price is the gradient. The cheaper option flips where the lines cross.","kupu":["pānga rārangi"]},{"k":"ineq","lab":"Inequalities","cap":"When does the tank hold at least 50 litres? Solve the inequality, and watch the shaded band on the time axis.","exam":"The tank holds at least 50 litres when 3t + 10 ≥ 50. Subtracting 10 gives 3t ≥ 40, so t ≥ 13⅓. From about 13.3 minutes on, the tank holds 50 litres or more.","trap":"Forgetting to flip the inequality sign when you divide by a negative number.","big":"The big idea: an inequality has a range of answers. Solve it like an equation, then describe the range.","kupu":[]},{"k":"test","lab":"Test yourself","cap":"Set the controls to answer each one, then press Check. The machine marks you with the same rules that draw the lines.","exam":"","trap":"","big":"Exam tip: for Merit, show each step in order. For Excellence, explain what your answer means and check it makes sense.","kupu":[]}],"challenges":[{"q":"Make tank A's line pass through (0, 10) and (10, 30).","check":"()=>s.cA===10&&s.mA===2","ok":"Yes: it rises 20 litres in 10 minutes, so m = 2, and starts at 10. L = 2t + 10.","no":"()=>`Not yet: your line is ${rule(s.mA,s.cA)}. Work out the rise over the run first.`","okIsFn":false,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"Make the two tanks hold the same amount at exactly 8 minutes.","check":"()=>{const X=meet(s.mA,s.cA,s.mB,s.cB);return X&&Math.abs(X.t-8)<0.01}","ok":"()=>{const X=meet(s.mA,s.cA,s.mB,s.cB);return`Yes: both hold ${fmtN(Math.round(X.y*10)/10)} L at 8 minutes.`}","no":"()=>{const X=meet(s.mA,s.cA,s.mB,s.cB);return X?`Not yet: they meet at ${fmtN(Math.round(X.t*100)/100)} min.`:\"Not yet: parallel lines never meet. Give them different rates.\"}","okIsFn":true,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"Make tank A run empty at exactly 12 minutes.","check":"()=>{const e=emptyAt(s.mA,s.cA);return e!==null&&Math.abs(e-12)<0.01}","ok":"Yes: c ÷ (rate) = 12. For example 60 L draining at 5 L a minute.","no":"Not yet. The rate must be negative, and the start divided by the drain rate must be 12.","okIsFn":false,"noIsFn":false}],"controls":[{"type":"play","id":"play","label":""},{"type":"button","id":"reset","label":"Back to 0 min"},{"type":"range","id":"cA","label":"A starts"},{"type":"range","id":"mA","label":"A rate"},{"type":"range","id":"cB","label":"B starts"},{"type":"range","id":"mB","label":"B rate"},{"type":"range","id":"feeA","label":"A unlock"},{"type":"range","id":"rateA","label":"A per min"},{"type":"range","id":"feeB","label":"B unlock"},{"type":"range","id":"rateB","label":"B per min"}],"shell":"kit"},"particles":{"id":"particles","title":"The particle machine","href":"m/particles.html","desc":"Ice to steam on a hotplate: heating curves, melting and boiling, why water boils at 87°C on Aoraki, and gas pressure in a syringe.","blurb":"Heating curves, melting, boiling and gas pressure, from ice to steam.","tags":["Year 9","Year 10","Science"],"built":true,"eyebrow":"Science · Matter and particles","crumb":"Year 9–10 science · foundation for NCEA Level 1 Chemistry and Biology","badge":null,"papers":[],"truth":"A simplified model: 0.5 kg of water, standard textbook values for specific heat and latent heat, no heat lost to the room, and particles drawn far bigger than they are. Boiling points use the standard atmosphere and are close to real values.","footer":"Matter and the particle model is core Year 9–10 science and underpins NCEA Level 1 Chemistry and Biology.","mainLabel":"Half a kilogram of ice in a beaker on a hotplate, shown as particles, with a thermometer","kupu":[{"mi":"matūriki","en":"particle","ta":50542,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"particles, heat"},{"mi":"totoka","en":"solid","ta":8538,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Reo Pūtaiao (MoE 2009) cited in entry","stepsNote":"particles, melt"},{"mi":"wē","en":"liquid","ta":13057,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"particles, melt"},{"mi":"haurehu","en":"gas","ta":39276,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Reo Pūtaiao (MoE 2009) cited in entry","stepsNote":"particles, boil, pressure"},{"mi":"paemahana","en":"temperature","ta":39927,"note":"","chip":["temp","boilpt"],"evidence":"He Pātaka Kupu (Te Taura Whiri 2008) cited in entry","stepsNote":"heat, warm"},{"mi":"rewa","en":"to melt","ta":6746,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"melt"},{"mi":"pae rewa","en":"melting point","ta":40108,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Reo Pūtaiao (MoE 2009) cited in entry","stepsNote":"melt"},{"mi":"koropupū","en":"to boil, bubble up","ta":3056,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"boil, mountain"}],"steps":[{"k":"particles","lab":"Particles","cap":"Everything is made of tiny particles that never stop moving. In ice they are locked in place and can only vibrate.","exam":"In a solid the particles are held tightly in a fixed pattern by strong forces of attraction, so they can only vibrate on the spot. This is why a solid keeps its shape.","trap":"Saying the particles in a solid don't move. They do: they vibrate in fixed positions.","big":"The big idea: solids, liquids and gases are the same particles. What changes is how much they move and how tightly they hold on to each other.","kupu":["matūriki","totoka","wē","haurehu"]},{"k":"heat","lab":"Heat it","cap":"Turn on the hotplate. Heat energy makes the ice particles vibrate faster, so the temperature rises.","exam":"When the ice is heated, its particles gain kinetic energy and vibrate faster, so the temperature of the ice rises.","trap":"Mixing up heat and temperature. Heat is energy going in; temperature is how fast the particles are moving.","big":"The big idea: temperature measures how fast the particles are moving on average. Adding heat speeds them up.","kupu":["paemahana","matūriki"]},{"k":"melt","lab":"Melting","cap":"At 0°C the temperature stops rising even though heat keeps going in. The energy is breaking the ice's hold on its particles.","exam":"While the ice melts, the temperature stays at 0°C. The heat energy is used to overcome the forces of attraction holding the particles in their fixed positions, not to make them move faster.","trap":"Expecting the thermometer to keep climbing during melting. Look for the flat part of the heating curve.","big":"The big idea: during a change of state, heat breaks the forces between particles instead of speeding them up, so the temperature stays flat.","kupu":["rewa","pae rewa","totoka","wē"]},{"k":"warm","lab":"Warming water","cap":"Now it's all liquid. The particles slide past each other and move faster as the water heats up.","exam":"In a liquid the particles are close together but not in fixed positions, so they can slide past each other. As the water is heated, the particles move faster and the temperature rises.","trap":"Drawing liquid particles spread far apart. They stay almost as close as in a solid.","big":"The big idea: in a liquid the particles are still close together but can move past each other, so a liquid flows and takes the shape of its container.","kupu":["paemahana"]},{"k":"boil","lab":"Boiling","cap":"At boiling point the temperature goes flat again. Particles gain enough energy to escape as a gas.","exam":"At 100°C the water boils and the temperature stays the same. The heat energy is used to overcome the forces between the particles so they can escape and spread out as a gas.","trap":"Thinking the bubbles in boiling water are air. They are water vapour.","big":"The big idea: boiling takes far more energy than melting: 2,260 kJ for each kilogram, nearly seven times more.","kupu":["koropupū","haurehu"]},{"k":"mountain","lab":"Up a mountain","cap":"Take the experiment up Ruapehu or Aoraki. Lower air pressure lets particles escape more easily, so water boils at a lower temperature.","exam":"At high altitude the air pressure is lower, so the water particles need less energy to escape from the liquid. This means water boils at a lower temperature, about 87°C on the summit of Aoraki / Mt Cook.","trap":"Thinking water always boils at 100°C. That is only at sea-level pressure.","big":"The big idea: boiling point depends on the air pressure pushing on the water. On the summit of Aoraki / Mt Cook water boils at about 87°C.","kupu":["koropupū"]},{"k":"pressure","lab":"Gas pressure","cap":"Push the plunger in. The same gas particles hit the walls more often in a smaller space, so the pressure goes up.","exam":"Gas pressure is caused by particles colliding with the walls of their container. When the volume is made smaller, the particles hit the walls more often, so the pressure increases.","trap":"Saying the particles get smaller when a gas is squashed. The particles stay the same size; the spaces between them shrink.","big":"The big idea: gas pressure is particles hitting the walls. Halve the volume and the pressure doubles; heat the gas and the hits get harder and more frequent.","kupu":["haurehu"]},{"k":"test","lab":"Test yourself","cap":"Use the controls to set up each one, then press Check. The machine marks you using the same model that moves it.","exam":"","trap":"","big":"Exam tip: name the particles, say what they're doing, then link it to what you can see: \"the particles…, so…\".","kupu":[]}],"challenges":[{"q":"Get the water boiling on Aoraki / Mt Cook. What temperature does the thermometer show?","check":"()=>s.alt===\"3724\"&&stateFrom(s.Q,Tb()).zone===3","ok":"()=>`Yes: it boils at ${Tb().toFixed(1)}°C because the air pressure up there is only about ${pressureAt(3724).toFixed(0)} kPa.`","no":"Not yet. Set Where to Aoraki / Mt Cook, then heat until the water boils.","okIsFn":true,"noIsFn":false},{"q":"Set the syringe so the pressure is double normal air pressure (about 200 kPa) at 20°C.","check":"()=>Math.abs(pressure()-202.6)<12&&s.gasT===20","ok":"Yes. Halving the volume from 60 mL to 30 mL doubles the pressure.","no":"()=>`Not yet: ${pressure().toFixed(0)} kPa. Keep the gas at 20°C and change the volume.`","okIsFn":false,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"Stop the experiment while the ice is exactly half melted.","check":"()=>{const S=stateFrom(s.Q,Tb());return S.zone===1&&Math.abs(S.liquid-0.5)<0.08}","ok":"Yes. Notice the thermometer: still 0°C, even though heat has been going in the whole time.","no":"Not yet. Go back to Melting, press Play, and pause when the chip shows about 50% melted.","okIsFn":false,"noIsFn":false}],"controls":[{"type":"play","id":"play","label":""},{"type":"button","id":"restart","label":"Restart"},{"type":"range","id":"power","label":"Hotplate"},{"type":"select","id":"alt","label":"Where"},{"type":"range","id":"vol","label":"Volume"},{"type":"range","id":"gasT","label":"Gas temp"}],"shell":"kit"},"energy":{"id":"energy","title":"The energy machine","href":"m/energy.html","desc":"A Sky Tower drop, a jug, a vacuum flask and a circuit: Ep, Ek, E = Pt, mcΔT, mL, V = IR and P = VI with worked numbers.","blurb":"Ep, Ek, E = Pt, mcΔT and circuits on the Sky Tower, a jug and a flask.","tags":["NCEA L1 PESS 92047","Year 10"],"built":true,"eyebrow":"Physics · Energy","crumb":"NCEA Level 1 Physics, Earth and Space Science · energy in physical systems","badge":{"label":"NCEA L1 PESS 92047","exam":"2026-11-12T14:00:00+13:00","examText":"Thu 12 Nov, 2pm"},"papers":[{"label":"NZQA: Physics, Earth and Space Science","url":"https://www2.nzqa.govt.nz/ncea/subjects/past-exams-and-exemplars/physics-earth-and-space-science/"}],"truth":"Uses g = 9.8 N/kg, c = 4200 J/kg°C and L = 2.26 × 10⁶ J/kg. SkyJump height (192 m) and top speed (about 85 km/h) are from the operator's published information; the braking profile is simplified. Cooling curves are illustrative.","footer":"Covers AS 92047: mechanical, thermal and electrical energy in physical systems, using Ek = ½mv², Ep = mgh, W = Fd, E = Pt, E = mcΔT, E = mL, P = VI and V = IR.","mainLabel":"Energy scenes: a jumper on the Sky Tower, a jug, a vacuum flask and a circuit","kupu":[{"mi":"pūngao","en":"energy","ta":40075,"note":"","chip":["epower"],"evidence":"Te Reo Pūtaiao (MoE 2009) cited in entry","stepsNote":"lift, wire"},{"mi":"pūngao moe","en":"potential energy","ta":47720,"note":"","chip":["ep"],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"lift, drop, wire"},{"mi":"pūngao neke","en":"kinetic energy","ta":47721,"note":"","chip":["ek"],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"drop, wire"},{"mi":"teitei","en":"height","ta":7895,"note":"","chip":["height"],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"lift"},{"mi":"wera","en":"heat","ta":9229,"note":"","chip":["heat"],"evidence":"He Pātaka Kupu (Te Taura Whiri 2008) cited in entry","stepsNote":"heat, boil, flask"},{"mi":"paemahana","en":"temperature","ta":39927,"note":"","chip":["temp"],"evidence":"He Pātaka Kupu (Te Taura Whiri 2008) cited in entry","stepsNote":"heat, boil, flask"},{"mi":"hiko","en":"electricity","ta":1171,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"circuit"},{"mi":"pūngao hiko","en":"electrical energy","ta":47713,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"circuit"}],"steps":[{"k":"lift","lab":"Lift it","cap":"Winch a 70 kg jumper 192 m up the Sky Tower. Every metre adds gravitational potential energy: work done against gravity.","exam":"Lifting the 70 kg jumper 192 m does work against gravity: W = Fd = (70 × 9.8) × 192 = 131,712 J, about 132 kJ. This work is stored as gravitational potential energy, Ep = mgh.","trap":"Using the mass as the force. The force is the weight: mass × 9.8.","big":"The big idea: work done lifting something becomes stored gravitational potential energy, Ep = mgh.","kupu":["pūngao moe","teitei","pūngao"]},{"k":"drop","lab":"Drop it","cap":"Imagine no wire and no air. All the potential energy turns into kinetic energy and the jumper hits about 220 km/h.","exam":"If no energy is lost, all the gravitational potential energy becomes kinetic energy: mgh = ½mv². The mass cancels, so v = √(2gh) = √(2 × 9.8 × 192) = 61.3 m/s, about 220 km/h.","trap":"Thinking a heavier jumper falls faster with no air. The mass cancels out, so the speed is the same.","big":"The big idea: energy is conserved. With no losses, Ep at the top equals Ek at the bottom, so ½mv² = mgh.","kupu":["pūngao neke","pūngao moe"]},{"k":"wire","lab":"SkyJump","cap":"The real SkyJump runs on a guide wire with a brake that holds the speed to about 85 km/h. The rest of the energy becomes heat in the brake.","exam":"At 85 km/h (23.6 m/s) the 70 kg jumper has Ek = ½ × 70 × 23.6² = 19.5 kJ. The jumper started with 132 kJ of potential energy, so about 112 kJ has been transformed into heat in the brake and the surrounding air by then.","trap":"Saying energy is lost. Say it is transformed into heat (and some sound), which spreads into the surroundings.","big":"The big idea: energy isn't lost, it's transformed. The brake turns most of the 132 kJ into heat, so only a little ends up as kinetic energy.","kupu":["pūngao moe","pūngao neke","pūngao"]},{"k":"heat","lab":"Heat it","cap":"A 2.2 kW jug heats 1 kg of water from 15°C. Watch the energy from E = Pt pour into the water as E = mcΔT.","exam":"To heat 1 kg of water from 15°C to 100°C needs E = mcΔT = 1 × 4200 × 85 = 357,000 J. A 2200 W jug supplies 2200 J each second, so t = E ÷ P = 357,000 ÷ 2200 = 162 s, about 2.7 minutes, if no heat is lost.","trap":"Mixing up c (specific heat, per °C) and L (latent heat, for a change of state).","big":"The big idea: power is energy per second. E = Pt tells you energy delivered; E = mcΔT tells you how far that energy heats the water.","kupu":["wera","paemahana"]},{"k":"boil","lab":"Boil it","cap":"At 100°C the temperature stops rising. Now the energy boils water away instead: E = mL.","exam":"Once the water reaches 100°C, the energy goes into changing its state, not raising its temperature. Boiling 0.1 kg of water needs E = mL = 0.1 × 2.26 × 10⁶ = 226,000 J, which takes 226,000 ÷ 2200 = 103 s.","trap":"Expecting the temperature to go above 100°C while water is boiling at sea level.","big":"The big idea: changing state takes latent heat without changing temperature. Boiling 1 kg of water takes 2.26 MJ, over six times the energy to heat it from 15°C.","kupu":["wera","paemahana"]},{"k":"flask","lab":"Keep it warm","cap":"Two teas at 92°C. The open mug loses heat by conduction, convection and radiation. The vacuum flask blocks all three.","exam":"The vacuum flask keeps the tea hot because the vacuum between its walls has no particles, so heat cannot travel across it by conduction or convection. The shiny silver walls reflect infrared radiation back towards the tea, and the lid stops convection currents and evaporation from the surface.","trap":"Saying a vacuum stops radiation. Radiation can cross a vacuum; the shiny surfaces reduce it.","big":"The big idea: to slow heat loss, block each way heat moves: conduction and convection need particles, radiation doesn't.","kupu":["wera","paemahana"]},{"k":"circuit","lab":"Circuits","cap":"Turn up the voltage or change the lamp's resistance. The current, the power and the brightness follow V = IR and P = VI.","exam":"With a 12 V battery and a 24 Ω lamp, the current is I = V ÷ R = 12 ÷ 24 = 0.5 A. The power is P = VI = 12 × 0.5 = 6 W, so the lamp transforms 6 joules of electrical energy into light and heat every second.","trap":"Writing the unit of power as J. Power is in watts: joules per second.","big":"The big idea: voltage pushes, resistance resists, and current is the result. Power is the rate electrical energy is transformed.","kupu":["hiko","pūngao hiko"]},{"k":"test","lab":"Test yourself","cap":"Use the sliders to set up each one, then press Check. The machine marks you with the same formulas it runs.","exam":"","trap":"","big":"Exam tip: write the formula, substitute with units, give the answer with units, then say what it means in the context.","kupu":[]}],"challenges":[{"q":"Set the height so a no-losses fall reaches 50 m/s at the bottom.","check":"()=>Math.abs(Math.sqrt(2*g*s.h)-50)<0.6","ok":"()=>`Yes: h = v² ÷ 2g = 2500 ÷ 19.6 ≈ 128 m. You set ${s.h} m.`","no":"()=>`Not yet: from ${s.h} m the speed would be ${fmt(Math.sqrt(2*g*s.h),1)} m/s. Rearrange v = √(2gh) for h.`","okIsFn":true,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"Make the lamp use exactly 6 W from a 12 V battery.","check":"()=>s.V===12&&s.R===24","ok":"Yes: I = 12 ÷ 24 = 0.5 A, and P = 12 × 0.5 = 6 W.","no":"()=>`Not yet: ${fmt(s.V*s.V/s.R,1)} W. Use P = V²/R to find R.`","okIsFn":false,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"Set the jug so 1.5 kg of water heats from 15°C to 100°C in under 3 minutes.","check":"()=>s.km===1.5&&1.5*C_W*85/s.kP<180","ok":"()=>`Yes: ${fmt(1.5*C_W*85/s.kP)} s with a ${fmt(s.kP)} W jug.`","no":"()=>s.km!==1.5?\"Set the water to 1.5 kg first.\":`Not yet: ${fmt(1.5*C_W*85/s.kP)} s. You need more power.`","okIsFn":true,"noIsFn":true}],"controls":[{"type":"play","id":"play","label":""},{"type":"button","id":"restart","label":"Restart"},{"type":"range","id":"m","label":"Mass"},{"type":"range","id":"h","label":"Height"},{"type":"range","id":"km","label":"Water"},{"type":"range","id":"kP","label":"Jug power"},{"type":"range","id":"V","label":"Voltage"},{"type":"range","id":"R","label":"Resistance"}],"shell":"kit"},"genes":{"id":"genes","title":"The gene pool machine","href":"m/genes.html","desc":"An island of birds: Punnett squares, mutation, small populations (the black robin), survival, migration and a DNA-profile paternity test.","blurb":"Punnett squares, mutation and the black robin on an island of birds.","tags":["NCEA L1 Chem & Bio 92022","Year 10"],"built":true,"eyebrow":"Biology · Genetic variation","crumb":"NCEA Level 1 Chemistry and Biology · genetic variation · also Year 9–10 genetics","badge":{"label":"NCEA L1 Chem & Bio 92022","exam":"2026-11-19T14:00:00+13:00","examText":"Thu 19 Nov, 2pm"},"papers":[{"label":"NZQA: Chemistry and Biology","url":"https://www2.nzqa.govt.nz/ncea/subjects/past-exams-and-exemplars/chemistry-and-biology/"},{"label":"DOC: black robin","url":"https://www.doc.govt.nz/news/media-releases/2025-media-releases/more-habitat-needed-for-black-robins/"}],"truth":"The birds and their feather gene are invented to show the ideas; real traits often involve many genes. Black robin facts (five birds in 1980, all descended from Old Blue, about 445 today) are from the Department of Conservation.","footer":"Covers AS 92022: sources of variation (mutation, sexual reproduction), what changes it in populations (small populations, survival, migration), allele frequency, and gene tracking with DNA profiles.","mainLabel":"A population of birds with dark and pale feathers","kupu":[{"mi":"ira","en":"gene","ta":1778,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"cross, pool, mutation"},{"mi":"pītau ira","en":"DNA","ta":50987,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"mutation, dna"},{"mi":"pūira","en":"chromosome","ta":40067,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Reo Pūtaiao (MoE 2009) cited in entry","stepsNote":"cross"},{"mi":"mātāira","en":"gene pool","ta":50460,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"pool, small, migration"},{"mi":"mātai iranga","en":"genetics","ta":39853,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"cross"},{"mi":"taupori","en":"population","ta":38688,"note":"","chip":["N"],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"pool, small, migration"},{"mi":"momo","en":"type, kind, species","ta":4184,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"(machine list only)"},{"mi":"rauropi","en":"organism","ta":38797,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Reo Pūtaiao (MoE 2009) cited in entry","stepsNote":"survival"}],"steps":[{"k":"cross","lab":"Variation","cap":"Two dark parents can have a pale chick. Each parent passes on one of its two alleles at random, so sexual reproduction reshuffles variation every generation.","exam":"Each parent has two alleles for feather colour and passes one on, chosen at random during meiosis. Two Dd parents can both pass on d, giving a dd chick with pale feathers. The Punnett square predicts 25% pale chicks, but in a small brood the actual number varies by chance.","trap":"Treating the Punnett square as a guarantee. It gives probabilities, not exact counts.","big":"The big idea: meiosis and random fertilisation create new combinations of alleles, so siblings differ. That's variation from sexual reproduction.","kupu":["ira","pūira","mātai iranga"]},{"k":"pool","lab":"Gene pool","cap":"A big population of 500 birds. Every bird carries two alleles; together they make the gene pool. With nothing pushing it, the allele frequency stays steady.","exam":"The allele frequency of d is the proportion of all the feather-colour alleles in the population that are d. In a large population with no selection, migration or mutation, it stays about the same from one generation to the next.","trap":"Confusing allele frequency with the share of pale birds. Many d alleles are hidden in dark Dd carriers.","big":"The big idea: allele frequency is the share of all the alleles in a population that are one type. It changes only when something changes who survives or breeds.","kupu":["mātāira","ira","taupori"]},{"k":"mutation","lab":"Mutation","cap":"Mutation is the only source of brand-new alleles. Here a small mutation rate slowly turns d alleles into D.","exam":"A mutation is a permanent change in the DNA sequence of a gene. Mutations create new alleles, which is the original source of genetic variation. A mutation is only passed on if it happens in the cells that make gametes.","trap":"Saying mutations happen because an animal needs them. They're random.","big":"The big idea: mutations are random changes in DNA. Most do nothing, some harm, a few help. They're the original source of all variation.","kupu":["ira","pītau ira"]},{"k":"small","lab":"Small populations","cap":"In 1980 only five Chatham Island black robins were left. In a tiny population, chance decides which alleles get passed on, and alleles can vanish forever.","exam":"In a small population, chance events decide which individuals breed, so allele frequencies can change a lot between generations and alleles can be lost completely. The black robin fell to five birds in 1980, so today's population of about 445 has very little genetic variation, which makes it more vulnerable to disease.","trap":"Thinking a population that grows back also gets its variation back. Lost alleles only return through mutation or migration.","big":"The big idea: in small populations allele frequencies drift by chance, and alleles get lost. Every black robin alive today descends from one female, Old Blue.","kupu":["taupori","mātāira"]},{"k":"survival","lab":"Survival","cap":"A new predator spots pale birds more easily. Fewer pale birds survive to breed, so the d allele gets rarer, but never quite vanishes, because carriers hide it.","exam":"Pale dd birds are more likely to be eaten, so fewer survive to breed and pass on the d allele. Over generations the frequency of d falls. It falls slowly once it's rare, because most d alleles are in dark Dd carriers that aren't selected against.","trap":"Saying the birds change colour to survive. Individuals don't change; the population's allele frequencies do.","big":"The big idea: when one phenotype survives better, its alleles are passed on more. That's natural selection changing allele frequency.","kupu":["rauropi"]},{"k":"migration","lab":"Migration","cap":"Birds fly in from another island where nearly all carry D. Gene flow pulls this population's frequency towards theirs.","exam":"Migration brings new individuals into the population, and they breed and add their alleles to the gene pool. Because the migrants mostly carry D, the frequency of D increases. Migration can also bring in alleles that had been lost, increasing variation.","trap":"Forgetting that migrants must breed to change the gene pool.","big":"The big idea: migration moves alleles between populations: it can add new variation and makes populations more alike.","kupu":["taupori","mātāira"]},{"k":"dna","lab":"DNA profiling","cap":"Gene tracking: compare the chick's DNA bands with its mother and three possible fathers. Every band must come from one parent.","exam":"In a DNA profile each band is an allele at a marker. A chick inherits one allele per marker from each parent, so every band in its profile must match either the mother or the father. The male whose bands account for all of the chick's remaining bands is the father.","trap":"Looking for the male with the most matching bands overall. He must match every band the mother can't explain.","big":"The big idea: DNA profiling, sequencing and pedigrees let scientists track alleles through families, which is how conservation teams manage breeding in tiny populations.","kupu":[]},{"k":"test","lab":"Test yourself","cap":"Use the controls to answer each one, then press Check. The machine marks you with the same model it runs.","exam":"","trap":"","big":"Exam tip: name the source of variation (mutation, sexual reproduction) or the cause of change (small population, survival, migration), then explain how it changes the allele frequency.","kupu":[]}],"challenges":[{"q":"Pick the chick's real father from the DNA profile.","check":"()=>s.gelPick===G.real","ok":"Yes: he carries every band the chick didn't get from its mother.","no":"Not that one. Find a chick band the mother doesn't have, then find the male who does.","okIsFn":false,"noIsFn":false},{"q":"Choose two parents that can never have a pale chick, but can have chicks that carry pale.","check":"()=>{const set=[s.pA,s.pB].sort().join();return set===\"DD,Dd\"||set===\"Dd,DD\"||set===\"DD,dd\"||set===\"dd,DD\"}","ok":"Yes: with one DD parent every chick gets a D, so all are dark, but some carry d.","no":"()=>`Not yet: ${s.pA} × ${s.pB} gives ${expPale()}. One parent must be DD and the other must carry d.`","okIsFn":false,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"Run a population until one allele is lost completely.","check":"()=>s.p===0||s.p===1","ok":"Yes: with so few birds, chance alone removed an allele. Only mutation or migration can bring it back.","no":"Not yet. Make the population very small, press Play and watch the graph.","okIsFn":false,"noIsFn":false}],"controls":[{"type":"play","id":"play","label":""},{"type":"button","id":"restart","label":"Restart"},{"type":"range","id":"N","label":"Birds"},{"type":"range","id":"sel","label":"Pale birds eaten"},{"type":"range","id":"mig","label":"Migrants"},{"type":"range","id":"mu","label":"Mutation"},{"type":"select","id":"pA","label":"Mother"},{"type":"select","id":"pB","label":"Father"},{"type":"button","id":"breed","label":"Breed again"},{"type":"toggle","id":"pick0","label":"Male 1"},{"type":"toggle","id":"pick1","label":"Male 2"},{"type":"toggle","id":"pick2","label":"Male 3"},{"type":"button","id":"newgel","label":"New family"}],"shell":"kit"},"ecology":{"id":"ecology","title":"The ecology machine","href":"m/ecology.html","desc":"A shingle beach: walk a transect, link plant height to soil depth, explain distribution factors, and run a food chain with dotterels and stoats.","blurb":"Transects, plant distribution and a food chain on a shingle beach.","tags":["Year 9","Year 10","Science"],"built":true,"eyebrow":"Science · Ecology","crumb":"Year 9–10 science · ecosystems, distribution, food chains","badge":null,"papers":[{"label":"DOC: banded dotterels on shingle beaches","url":"https://www.doc.govt.nz/news/media-releases/2021-media-releases/banding-together-for-the-banded-dotterel/"}],"truth":"The transect data are made up for practice, following the pattern found on real shingle beaches. Population numbers in the food chain are illustrative, not survey counts.","footer":"Ecosystems, distribution of organisms and food chains are Year 9–10 science topics in the New Zealand Curriculum.","mainLabel":"Cross-section of a shingle beach with plants, soil and the sea","kupu":[{"mi":"pūnaha hauropi","en":"ecosystem","ta":40334,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Reo Pūtaiao (MoE 2009) cited in entry","stepsNote":"habitat"},{"mi":"nōhanga","en":"habitat","ta":4432,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Reo Pūtaiao (MoE 2009) cited in entry","stepsNote":"habitat, factors"},{"mi":"rauropi","en":"organism","ta":38797,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Reo Pūtaiao (MoE 2009) cited in entry","stepsNote":"chain"},{"mi":"momo","en":"type, kind, species","ta":4184,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"transect"},{"mi":"taupori","en":"population","ta":38688,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"transect, pyramid"},{"mi":"oneone","en":"soil","ta":4770,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"soil"},{"mi":"urutau","en":"to adapt","ta":40435,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Reo Pūtaiao (MoE 2009) cited in entry","stepsNote":"factors"},{"mi":"raupapa kame","en":"food chain","ta":40170,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Reo Pūtaiao (MoE 2009) cited in entry","stepsNote":"chain, pyramid"}],"steps":[{"k":"habitat","lab":"The beach","cap":"A shingle beach from the top (0 m) down to the sea (40 m). The plants change along the way. Your job: work out why.","exam":"The distribution of an organism is where it is found in an environment. It depends on abiotic factors, such as soil depth, light, temperature and salt spray, and biotic factors, such as food, predators and competition.","trap":"Calling soil or temperature biotic. Biotic means living things; abiotic means non-living conditions.","big":"The big idea: organisms are found where the conditions suit them. Abiotic factors (non-living: soil, water, light, temperature, salt) and biotic factors (living: food, predators, competition) decide the distribution.","kupu":["nōhanga","pūnaha hauropi"]},{"k":"transect","lab":"Walk a transect","cap":"Lay a tape from the top of the beach to the sea and place a quadrat every 5 m. Press Play to walk it and record the plant height in each quadrat.","exam":"The students used a line transect, measuring the height of the plants in a quadrat every 5 metres from the top of the beach towards the sea. The average plant height decreased from 42 cm at the top of the beach to 0 cm at 35 m, where no plants were found.","trap":"Describing the trend without numbers. Quote values from the data, with units.","big":"The big idea: a line transect with quadrats at regular intervals shows how organisms change across an environment.","kupu":["momo","taupori"]},{"k":"soil","lab":"Soil depth","cap":"The same transect, now measuring soil depth. Compare the two graphs: where the soil is deep, the plants are tall.","exam":"As the distance from the top of the beach increases, the soil gets shallower, from 18 cm at 0 m to 0 cm at 35 m. The plants also get shorter and fewer. This suggests deeper soil helps plants grow, because it holds more water and nutrients and gives roots something to anchor in.","trap":"Saying soil depth definitely causes the change. Other factors, like salt spray, also change along the beach, so say \"suggests\".","big":"The big idea (Merit and Excellence): link two sets of data and explain the link with science, not just describe it.","kupu":["oneone"]},{"k":"factors","lab":"Why there?","cap":"Pick a factor to see how it shapes where things live on this beach. Each one is a classic exam question.","exam":"Availability of nesting sites affects distribution because animals can only breed where there is a safe place to nest. For example, banded dotterels nest on open shingle above the high-tide line, so they are found at the upper part of shingle beaches.","trap":"Giving a factor without an example. These questions ask for a plant or animal each time.","big":"The big idea: for each factor, say what it is, how it changes across the environment, and give a real plant or animal example.","kupu":["urutau","nōhanga"]},{"k":"chain","lab":"Food chain","cap":"Who eats whom on the beach. Add stoats and watch the dotterels drop, and the insects they eat increase.","exam":"In the food chain plants → insects → banded dotterel → stoat, the plants are producers because they make their own food by photosynthesis. When stoats increase, they eat more dotterels and their eggs, so the dotterel population falls, and with fewer dotterels eating them, the insect population can increase.","trap":"Drawing arrows the wrong way. Arrows point to the eater: they show which way the energy goes.","big":"The big idea: a food chain shows the flow of energy. Change one level and the levels around it change too.","kupu":["raupapa kame","rauropi"]},{"k":"pyramid","lab":"Trophic levels","cap":"Each step up the food chain is a trophic level. Only about a tenth of the energy makes it to the next level.","exam":"Only about 10% of the energy at one trophic level is passed on to the next. The rest is used by the organisms for respiration and movement and is lost as heat. This is why food chains rarely have more than four or five levels.","trap":"Saying energy is recycled. Nutrients are recycled; energy flows through and is lost as heat.","big":"The big idea: energy is lost at every trophic level, mostly as heat from respiration, which is why there are far fewer top predators than plants.","kupu":["raupapa kame","taupori"]},{"k":"test","lab":"Test yourself","cap":"Use the controls to answer each one, then press Check.","exam":"","trap":"","big":"Exam tip: describe the pattern with numbers, explain it with a factor, and give a named example.","kupu":[]}],"challenges":[{"q":"Walk the transect to the first quadrat with no plants. How far from the top is it?","check":"()=>s.walk>=35&&s.walk<40","ok":"Yes: 35 m. Past here it is bare shingle with no soil, and salt spray from the waves.","no":"()=>`Not yet: you're at ${Math.floor(s.walk/5)*5} m. Press Play and pause at the first empty quadrat.`","okIsFn":false,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"Set the number of stoats that gives the dotterels the best chance.","check":"()=>s.stoats===0","ok":"Yes: with no stoats the dotterel population is at its highest. That's why DOC and community groups trap predators around nesting beaches.","no":"Not yet. Which number of predators lets the most dotterels survive?","okIsFn":false,"noIsFn":false},{"q":"Choose the factor that best explains why plants are tallest at the top of the beach.","check":"()=>s.factor===\"nutrients\"","ok":"Yes: deeper soil holds more nutrients and water, so plants grow taller.","no":"Not quite. Look back at the soil-depth data.","okIsFn":false,"noIsFn":false}],"controls":[{"type":"play","id":"play","label":""},{"type":"button","id":"restart","label":"Restart walk"},{"type":"select","id":"factor","label":"Factor"},{"type":"range","id":"stoats","label":"Stoats"}],"shell":"kit"},"atoms":{"id":"atoms","title":"The atom machine","href":"m/atoms.html","desc":"Build atoms from protons, neutrons and electrons: shells, the first 20 elements, ions, isotopes, and Rutherford's gold foil.","blurb":"Shells, the first 20 elements, ions, isotopes and Rutherford’s gold foil.","tags":["Year 9","Year 10","Science"],"built":true,"eyebrow":"Science · Atoms","crumb":"Year 9–10 science · atoms, the periodic table, ions and isotopes","badge":null,"papers":[{"label":"Te Ara: Ernest Rutherford","url":"https://teara.govt.nz/en/biographies/3r37/rutherford-ernest"}],"truth":"Electrons are drawn as dots in fixed shells, a simplified model used at school. Sizes are not to scale. The gold foil simulation is a picture of the result, not a physics model.","footer":"Atomic structure, ions and isotopes are Year 9–10 science (matter and the periodic table) and lead into NCEA Level 1 Chemistry and Biology.","mainLabel":"An atom with its nucleus and electron shells","kupu":[{"mi":"ngota","en":"atom","ta":14992,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Reo Pūtaiao (MoE 2009) cited in entry","stepsNote":"build, foil"},{"mi":"karihi","en":"nucleus","ta":15725,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Reo Pūtaiao (MoE 2009) cited in entry","stepsNote":"build, foil"},{"mi":"iraoho","en":"proton","ta":40328,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Reo Pūtaiao (MoE 2009) cited in entry","stepsNote":"build"},{"mi":"iramoe","en":"neutron","ta":40330,"note":"","chip":["neut"],"evidence":"Te Reo Pūtaiao (MoE 2009) cited in entry","stepsNote":"build, isotopes"},{"mi":"irahiko","en":"electron","ta":40517,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Reo Pūtaiao (MoE 2009) cited in entry","stepsNote":"build, shells"},{"mi":"pūmotu","en":"element","ta":40052,"note":"","chip":["el"],"evidence":"Te Reo Pūtaiao (MoE 2009) cited in entry","stepsNote":"numbers"},{"mi":"tau iraoho","en":"atomic number","ta":40327,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Reo Pūtaiao (MoE 2009) cited in entry","stepsNote":"numbers"},{"mi":"tau karihi","en":"mass number","ta":40329,"note":"","chip":["mass"],"evidence":"Te Reo Pūtaiao (MoE 2009) cited in entry","stepsNote":"numbers"}],"steps":[{"k":"build","lab":"Build an atom","cap":"Add protons and the element changes. Protons decide what the atom is; neutrons add mass; electrons orbit in shells.","exam":"An atom has a nucleus containing protons (positive) and neutrons (no charge), with electrons (negative) moving around it in shells. A carbon atom has 6 protons, 6 neutrons and 6 electrons, so it has no overall charge.","trap":"Saying neutrons decide the element. Only the number of protons does.","big":"The big idea: an atom is a tiny nucleus of protons and neutrons with electrons around it. The number of protons is the atomic number, and it decides the element.","kupu":["ngota","karihi","iraoho","iramoe","irahiko"]},{"k":"numbers","lab":"Mass and atomic number","cap":"The small top number is the mass number: protons plus neutrons. The bottom number is the atomic number: protons.","exam":"Sodium has an atomic number of 11 and a mass number of 23. So it has 11 protons, 23 − 11 = 12 neutrons and, as a neutral atom, 11 electrons.","trap":"Adding electrons into the mass number. Mass number counts only protons and neutrons.","big":"The big idea: neutrons = mass number − atomic number. Electrons have almost no mass, so they're not counted.","kupu":["tau iraoho","tau karihi","pūmotu"]},{"k":"shells","lab":"Electron shells","cap":"Electrons fill shells from the inside out: 2 in the first, then 8, then 8. Watch calcium fill 2,8,8,2.","exam":"Calcium has 20 electrons arranged 2,8,8,2. It has four shells, so it is in period 4, and two electrons in its outer shell, so it is in group 2.","trap":"Putting 8 electrons in the first shell. The first shell only holds 2.","big":"The big idea: the electron arrangement gives the period (number of shells) and group (outer electrons) on the periodic table.","kupu":["irahiko"]},{"k":"ions","lab":"Ions","cap":"Take an electron away from sodium, or give one to chlorine. Atoms lose or gain electrons to get a full outer shell, becoming charged ions.","exam":"A sodium atom (2,8,1) loses its one outer electron to get a full outer shell. It now has 11 protons but only 10 electrons, so it is a sodium ion with a charge of 1+ (Na⁺).","trap":"Changing the protons to make an ion. Ions form by gaining or losing electrons only.","big":"The big idea: metals lose electrons to form positive ions; non-metals gain electrons to form negative ions. The protons never change.","kupu":[]},{"k":"isotopes","lab":"Isotopes","cap":"Same protons, different neutrons. Carbon-12 and carbon-14 are both carbon; carbon-14 is radioactive and is used to date old things.","exam":"Carbon-12 and carbon-14 are isotopes of carbon. Both have 6 protons, so they are the same element, but carbon-12 has 6 neutrons and carbon-14 has 8, so their mass numbers are different.","trap":"Saying isotopes have different numbers of protons. Then they'd be different elements.","big":"The big idea: isotopes are atoms of the same element with different numbers of neutrons, so they have different mass numbers.","kupu":["iramoe"]},{"k":"foil","lab":"Rutherford's foil","cap":"Fire alpha particles at thin gold foil, like Ernest Rutherford's team did. Most go straight through. A very few bounce back.","exam":"Most alpha particles passed straight through the gold foil, which showed that an atom is mostly empty space. A few were deflected or bounced back, which showed there is a very small, dense, positively charged nucleus that repels the positive alpha particles.","trap":"Saying the particles bounced off electrons. Electrons are far too light; it's the dense positive nucleus.","big":"The big idea: the gold foil experiment showed atoms are mostly empty space with a tiny, dense, positive nucleus. Rutherford grew up near Nelson.","kupu":["karihi","ngota"]},{"k":"test","lab":"Test yourself","cap":"Set the protons, neutrons and electrons for each one, then press Check.","exam":"","trap":"","big":"Exam tip: show your counting. Protons from the atomic number, neutrons by subtracting, electrons from the charge.","kupu":[]}],"challenges":[{"q":"Build a chloride ion, Cl⁻ (chlorine-35).","check":"()=>s.p===17&&s.n===18&&s.e===18","ok":"Yes: 17 protons, 18 neutrons, 18 electrons. One extra electron gives a full outer shell (2,8,8) and a 1− charge.","no":"()=>`Not yet: you have ${s.p} protons, ${s.n} neutrons, ${s.e} electrons.`","okIsFn":false,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"Build carbon-14.","check":"()=>s.p===6&&s.n===8&&s.e===6","ok":"Yes: 6 protons and 8 neutrons give mass number 14.","no":"Not yet. Carbon always has 6 protons; the mass number is protons plus neutrons.","okIsFn":false,"noIsFn":false},{"q":"Build a neutral atom with three shells and a full outer shell.","check":"()=>s.p===18&&s.e===18","ok":"Yes: argon, 2,8,8. A noble gas, so it hardly reacts.","no":"Not yet. Which element fills its third shell exactly?","okIsFn":false,"noIsFn":false}],"controls":[{"type":"play","id":"play","label":""},{"type":"button","id":"reset","label":"Clear screen"},{"type":"range","id":"p","label":"Protons"},{"type":"range","id":"n","label":"Neutrons"},{"type":"range","id":"e","label":"Electrons"}],"shell":"kit"},"shapes":{"id":"shapes","title":"The shape workshop","href":"m/shapes.html","desc":"Grid-paper shapes you can stretch: areas of parallelograms, triangles and trapeziums, circles unrolled, Pythagoras poured, parallel-line angles and prism volume.","blurb":"Areas, circles, Pythagoras, angles and prism volume on stretchy grid paper.","tags":["Year 9","Year 10","NCEA L1 Maths 91947"],"built":true,"eyebrow":"Maths · Measurement and geometry","crumb":"Year 9–10 maths · area, circles, Pythagoras, angles and volume · also NCEA Level 1 91947","badge":null,"papers":[{"label":"NZQA: Mathematics and Statistics","url":"https://www2.nzqa.govt.nz/ncea/subjects/past-exams-and-exemplars/mathematics-and-statistics/"}],"truth":"Shapes are drawn to scale on the grid (one square = 1 m), except the unrolled circle edge, which is drawn at half scale.","footer":"Area, circles, Pythagoras, angles with parallel lines and volume are Year 9–10 maths and are examined in NCEA Level 1 standard 91947.","mainLabel":"Shapes drawn on grid paper with their measurements","kupu":[{"mi":"horahanga","en":"area","ta":45667,"note":"","chip":["area","carea"],"evidence":"Te Reo Pāngarau (MoE 2010) cited in entry","stepsNote":"para, tri, trap, prism"},{"mi":"tapawhā whakarara","en":"parallelogram","ta":45970,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"para"},{"mi":"tapatoru","en":"triangle","ta":7470,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"He Pātaka Kupu (Te Taura Whiri 2008) cited in entry","stepsNote":"tri"},{"mi":"porowhita","en":"circle","ta":5893,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"circle"},{"mi":"pūtoro","en":"radius","ta":45887,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Reo Pāngarau (MoE 2010) cited in entry","stepsNote":"circle"},{"mi":"paenga","en":"circumference, perimeter","ta":4870,"note":"","chip":["circ"],"evidence":"Te Reo Pāngarau (MoE 2010) cited in entry","stepsNote":"circle"},{"mi":"tāroa","en":"hypotenuse","ta":7603,"note":"","chip":["hyp"],"evidence":"Te Reo Pāngarau (MoE 2010) cited in entry","stepsNote":"pyth"},{"mi":"koki hāngai","en":"right angle","ta":45739,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"pyth"}],"steps":[{"k":"para","lab":"Parallelogram","cap":"Slide the top of the shape sideways. The area doesn't change, because the base and the perpendicular height don't change.","exam":"The area of the parallelogram is base × perpendicular height = 8 × 5 = 40 m². The slanted side isn't used because area depends on the height at right angles to the base.","trap":"Multiplying by the slanted side instead of the perpendicular height.","big":"The big idea: area = base × perpendicular height, for rectangles and parallelograms alike. Use the height at right angles to the base, not the slanted side.","kupu":["tapawhā whakarara","horahanga"]},{"k":"tri","lab":"Triangle","cap":"A triangle is exactly half of a parallelogram with the same base and height.","exam":"The area of the triangle is ½ × base × height = ½ × 8 × 5 = 20 m², which is half the area of the parallelogram with the same base and height.","trap":"Forgetting the ½.","big":"The big idea: area of a triangle = ½ × base × perpendicular height.","kupu":["tapatoru","horahanga"]},{"k":"trap","lab":"Trapezium","cap":"A trapezium has two parallel sides, a and b. Its area is the average of those sides times the height.","exam":"The area of the trapezium is ½ × (a + b) × h = ½ × (4 + 8) × 5 = 30 m².","trap":"Using a slanted side as the height.","big":"The big idea: area = ½ (a + b) × h. Add the parallel sides first, then halve, then times the height.","kupu":["horahanga"]},{"k":"circle","lab":"Circle","cap":"Press Play to unroll the edge of the circle. Its length, the circumference, is 2πr, a little over 6 radiuses.","exam":"For a circle with radius 3 m, the circumference is 2πr = 2 × π × 3 = 18.8 m, and the area is πr² = π × 3² = 28.3 m² (1 dp).","trap":"Using the diameter in πr². Halve the diameter first.","big":"The big idea: circumference = 2πr (a length, in m) and area = πr² (a space, in m²). Don't mix them up.","kupu":["porowhita","pūtoro","paenga"]},{"k":"pyth","lab":"Pythagoras","cap":"The two small squares on the short sides add up exactly to the big square on the longest side. Press Play to pour them in.","exam":"Using Pythagoras' theorem, c² = a² + b² = 3² + 4² = 9 + 16 = 25, so c = √25 = 5 m.","trap":"Adding the sides before squaring. Square first, then add, then square-root.","big":"The big idea: in a right-angled triangle, a² + b² = c², where c is the hypotenuse, the longest side opposite the right angle.","kupu":["tāroa","koki hāngai"]},{"k":"angles","lab":"Parallel lines","cap":"A line crossing two parallel lines makes matching angles. Change the angle and watch the others follow.","exam":"The two angles marked are corresponding angles on parallel lines, so they are equal: both are 62°. The angle next to it on the straight line is 180° − 62° = 118°.","trap":"Giving the angle without the reason. Geometry answers need the rule named.","big":"The big idea: corresponding angles are equal, alternate angles are equal, and co-interior angles add to 180°.","kupu":[]},{"k":"prism","lab":"Volume","cap":"A prism has the same shape all the way through. Its volume is the area of that shape times the length.","exam":"The cross-section is a triangle with area ½ × 8 × 5 = 20 m². The prism is 10 m long, so its volume is 20 × 10 = 200 m³.","trap":"Writing m² for a volume. Volume is in cubic units.","big":"The big idea: volume of any prism = area of cross-section × length. Units are cubed: m³.","kupu":["horahanga"]},{"k":"test","lab":"Test yourself","cap":"Set each shape up with the sliders, then press Check.","exam":"","trap":"","big":"Exam tip: write the formula, substitute, calculate, and give units. That earns the marks even if the arithmetic slips.","kupu":[]}],"challenges":[{"q":"Make a right-angled triangle whose longest side is exactly 10 m.","check":"()=>Math.abs(Math.hypot(s.ta,s.tb)-10)<1e-9","ok":"Yes: 6² + 8² = 36 + 64 = 100, and √100 = 10.","no":"()=>`Not yet: √(${s.ta}² + ${s.tb}²) = ${f(Math.hypot(s.ta,s.tb),2)} m. Hint: double a 3-4-5 triangle.`","okIsFn":false,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"Make a trapezium with an area of exactly 36 m².","check":"()=>(s.a+s.b)*s.h/2===36","ok":"()=>`Yes: ½ × (${s.a} + ${s.b}) × ${s.h} = 36 m².`","no":"()=>`Not yet: ½ × (${s.a} + ${s.b}) × ${s.h} = ${f((s.a+s.b)*s.h/2)} m².`","okIsFn":true,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"Find a circle whose area is bigger than 100 m².","check":"()=>Math.PI*s.r*s.r>100","ok":"()=>`Yes: π × ${s.r}² = ${f(Math.PI*s.r*s.r)} m².`","no":"()=>`Not yet: ${f(Math.PI*s.r*s.r)} m².`","okIsFn":true,"noIsFn":true}],"controls":[{"type":"play","id":"play","label":""},{"type":"range","id":"b","label":"Base"},{"type":"range","id":"h","label":"Height"},{"type":"range","id":"sh","label":"Slide top"},{"type":"range","id":"a","label":"Top side a"},{"type":"range","id":"r","label":"Radius"},{"type":"range","id":"ta","label":"Side a"},{"type":"range","id":"tb","label":"Side b"},{"type":"range","id":"ang","label":"Angle"},{"type":"range","id":"len","label":"Length"}],"shell":"kit"},"civics":{"id":"civics","title":"The MMP and law machine","href":"m/civics.html","desc":"Fill a debating chamber with fictional parties: the 5% rule, electorates and overhang, forming a government, and a bill's journey to Royal assent.","blurb":"The 5% rule, electorates, forming a government and a bill’s path to Royal assent.","tags":["Year 9","Year 10","Social studies"],"built":true,"eyebrow":"Social studies · Civics","crumb":"Year 9–10 social studies · civics: how government is formed and how a law is passed","badge":null,"papers":[{"label":"Electoral Commission: how MMP works","url":"https://vote.nz/"},{"label":"Parliament: how a bill becomes law","url":"https://www.parliament.nz/"}],"truth":"The parties and their votes are made up. Seat counting follows MMP's rules: 120 seats (71 electorates and 49 list seats in 2026), the 5% or one-electorate threshold, Sainte-Laguë allocation and overhang. Real elections have more detail, such as electorates won by independents.","footer":"Civics, including how New Zealand's government is formed and how a law is passed, is Year 9 social sciences content in the refreshed curriculum. This machine is non-partisan and does not represent any real party.","mainLabel":"Seats in Parliament coloured by fictional parties","kupu":[{"mi":"manapori","en":"democracy","ta":11071,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"govt"},{"mi":"Pāremata","en":"Parliament","ta":5295,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"vote, bill"},{"mi":"pōti","en":"to vote; a vote, an election","ta":5918,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"vote, electorate"},{"mi":"rōpū tōrangapū","en":"political party","ta":6909,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"vote, threshold"},{"mi":"rohe pōti","en":"electorate","ta":6860,"note":"","chip":["elecsum"],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"electorate"},{"mi":"Pirimia","en":"Prime Minister","ta":5645,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"cabinet"},{"mi":"rūnanga o te kāwanatanga","en":"Cabinet","ta":13091,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"cabinet"},{"mi":"pire","en":"bill (a proposed law)","ta":13133,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"bill"}],"steps":[{"k":"vote","lab":"Party vote","cap":"You get two votes. The party vote decides how many of the 120 seats each party gets. Slide the votes and watch the chamber fill.","exam":"Under MMP each voter has two votes: an electorate vote for a local MP and a party vote. The party vote decides each party's share of the 120 seats, so a party with about 30% of the party vote gets about 30% of the seats.","trap":"Thinking the electorate vote decides the overall result. It's the party vote that sets each party's share.","big":"The big idea: MMP (mixed member proportional) means a party's share of seats roughly matches its share of the party vote.","kupu":["pōti","rōpū tōrangapū","Pāremata"]},{"k":"threshold","lab":"The 5% rule","cap":"The Lantern Party has 4.5% and no electorate seat, so it gets nothing. Now give it one electorate seat and watch it bring list MPs in with it.","exam":"A party only gets list seats if it wins at least 5% of the party vote or wins an electorate seat. A party on 4.5% with no electorate gets no seats, but if it wins one electorate it gets seats in proportion to its 4.5%.","trap":"Saying a party under 5% can never get into Parliament. Winning one electorate gets it in.","big":"The big idea: a party needs 5% of the party vote OR one electorate seat to share in the list seats.","kupu":["rōpū tōrangapū"]},{"k":"electorate","lab":"Electorates","cap":"71 MPs win a local electorate. List seats top each party up to its fair share. If a party wins more electorates than its share, it keeps them: an overhang.","exam":"Parties keep every electorate seat they win, and list seats are added until each party reaches its share of the party vote. If a party wins more electorate seats than its share, it keeps them and Parliament has more than 120 MPs. This is called an overhang.","trap":"Adding electorate and list seats separately. List seats fill the gap between a party's fair share and its electorate seats.","big":"The big idea: electorate seats come first, list seats top up. Extra electorate wins make Parliament bigger than 120.","kupu":["rohe pōti","pōti"]},{"k":"govt","lab":"Make a government","cap":"Tap parties to join the government. They need more than half the seats to pass laws and survive confidence votes.","exam":"To form a government, a party or group of parties needs a majority of seats in Parliament, more than half. Because MMP rarely gives one party a majority, parties usually form a coalition or agree to confidence and supply.","trap":"Thinking the party with the most votes automatically forms the government. It needs a majority, often with partners.","big":"The big idea: under MMP one party rarely has a majority, so parties usually work together in a coalition or with a confidence and supply deal.","kupu":["manapori"]},{"k":"cabinet","lab":"PM and Cabinet","cap":"The government's leader becomes Prime Minister and picks ministers. The biggest party outside government leads the Opposition.","exam":"The Prime Minister leads the government and chooses ministers, who are responsible for areas like health and education. The most senior ministers form Cabinet, which makes the government's main decisions. The Opposition questions and challenges the government.","trap":"Mixing up Parliament and the government. Parliament is all 120-plus MPs; the government is the parties with the majority.","big":"The big idea: Parliament makes laws; the Executive (PM, Cabinet and ministers) runs the country; the courts apply the law. That's the separation of powers.","kupu":["Pirimia","rūnanga o te kāwanatanga"]},{"k":"bill","lab":"A bill becomes law","cap":"Press Play to send a fictional bill through Parliament. It needs a majority at each reading. Try it with and without a majority.","exam":"A bill becomes law after it passes a first reading, is studied by a select committee, passes a second reading, is debated clause by clause in the committee of the whole House, and passes a third reading. It then receives Royal assent from the Governor-General and becomes an Act.","trap":"Leaving out the Governor-General. A bill isn't law until it gets Royal assent.","big":"The big idea: a bill goes through three readings and a select committee, then the Governor-General's Royal assent makes it an Act: the law.","kupu":["pire","Pāremata"]},{"k":"committee","lab":"Have your say","cap":"At select committee anyone, including you, can send a submission about a bill and sometimes speak to the MPs in person.","exam":"Citizens can participate in law-making by making a submission to a select committee. The committee hears submissions from the public and experts, and can recommend changes to the bill before it goes back to Parliament.","trap":"Thinking you have to be 18 to make a submission. Anyone can make one.","big":"The big idea: select committees are the main way ordinary people take part in law-making between elections.","kupu":[]},{"k":"test","lab":"Test yourself","cap":"Set up each one with the sliders and party buttons, then press Check. The machine counts seats with the real MMP rules.","exam":"","trap":"","big":"Exam tip: name the rule (5% threshold, overhang, majority), then explain what it does to the result.","kupu":[]}],"challenges":[{"q":"Get the Lantern Party into Parliament without raising its party vote above 4.9%.","check":"()=>{const A=allocate();return s.vote[5]<5&&A.final[5]>0}","ok":"Yes: winning an electorate seat gets a party past the threshold, so its party vote counts.","no":"Not yet. Keep its vote under 5% and find another way in.","okIsFn":false,"noIsFn":false},{"q":"Make an overhang so Parliament has more than 120 seats.","check":"()=>allocate().total>120","ok":"()=>`Yes: ${allocate().total} seats. A party won more electorates than its party vote entitles it to and keeps them.`","no":"Not yet. Give a small party more electorate seats than its party vote would earn.","okIsFn":true,"noIsFn":false},{"q":"Form a government with the smallest number of parties that still has a majority.","check":"()=>{const G=gov();if(G.g<G.need)return false;const A=G.A;const n=s.gov.filter(Boolean).length;\n      // any smaller combination with a majority?\n      for(let m=0;m<64;m++){const c=[...Array(6).keys()].filter(i=>m>>i&1);if(c.length>=n)continue;if(c.reduce((a,i)=>a+A.final[i],0)>=G.need)return false}return true}","ok":"Yes: a majority with as few partners as possible.","no":"()=>{const G=gov();return G.g<G.need?`Not yet: ${G.g} seats, ${G.need} needed.`:\"That works, but you could do it with fewer parties.\"}","okIsFn":false,"noIsFn":true}],"controls":[{"type":"play","id":"play","label":""},{"type":"button","id":"restart","label":"Restart bill"},{"type":"range","id":"v0","label":"Harbour vote"},{"type":"range","id":"v1","label":"Summit vote"},{"type":"range","id":"v2","label":"Forest vote"},{"type":"range","id":"v3","label":"Tide vote"},{"type":"range","id":"v4","label":"Plains vote"},{"type":"range","id":"v5","label":"Lantern vote"},{"type":"range","id":"e0","label":"Harbour electorates"},{"type":"range","id":"e1","label":"Summit electorates"},{"type":"range","id":"e2","label":"Forest electorates"},{"type":"range","id":"e3","label":"Tide electorates"},{"type":"range","id":"e4","label":"Plains electorates"},{"type":"range","id":"e5","label":"Lantern electorates"},{"type":"toggle","id":"g0","label":"Harbour Party"},{"type":"toggle","id":"g1","label":"Summit Party"},{"type":"toggle","id":"g2","label":"Forest Party"},{"type":"toggle","id":"g3","label":"Tide Party"},{"type":"toggle","id":"g4","label":"Plains Party"},{"type":"toggle","id":"g5","label":"Lantern Party"}],"shell":"kit"},"sky":{"id":"sky","title":"The Sun–Earth–Moon machine","href":"m/sky.html","desc":"An orrery for Aotearoa: seasons, NZ day lengths, Moon phases, tides and eclipses, with exam-ready sentences.","blurb":"Seasons, NZ day lengths, Moon phases, tides and eclipses on an orrery.","tags":["NCEA L1 PESS 92046","Year 9","Year 10"],"built":true,"eyebrow":"Earth & Space · made for Aotearoa","crumb":"NCEA Level 1 · Physics, Earth and Space Science · also Year 9–10 science","badge":{"label":"NCEA L1 PESS 92046","exam":"2026-11-12T14:00:00+13:00","examText":"Thu 12 Nov, 2pm"},"papers":[{"label":"NZQA: Physics, Earth and Space Science","url":"https://www2.nzqa.govt.nz/ncea/subjects/subject/physics-earth-and-space-science"}],"truth":"A simplified model of the real sky, not a live feed. Sizes and distances are not to scale. Day lengths, noon Sun heights and Moon phases are calculated from standard formulas and are close to real values for 2026–27; tide sizes are relative, not metres for a real harbour.","footer":"Covers the observable patterns in AS 92046: day and night, seasons, Moon phases, tides, eclipses, and how they vary by place.","mainLabel":"Orrery: the Sun at the centre, the Earth on its orbit, the Moon around the Earth","kupu":[{"mi":"rā","en":"sun","ta":6405,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"spin, lat, eclipse"},{"mi":"marama","en":"moon","ta":3673,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"phase, tide, eclipse"},{"mi":"pou tāwhirowhiro","en":"axis of rotation","ta":40009,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Reo Pūtaiao (MoE 2009) cited in entry","stepsNote":"spin, tilt"},{"mi":"kaupeka","en":"season","ta":2441,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"tilt, season"},{"mi":"mata o te marama","en":"phases of the moon","ta":39883,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Reo Pūtaiao (MoE 2009) cited in entry","stepsNote":"phase"},{"mi":"tai pari","en":"high tide","ta":40275,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"tide"},{"mi":"tai timu","en":"low tide","ta":7187,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"tide"},{"mi":"āraitanga","en":"eclipse","ta":347,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Reo Pūtaiao (MoE 2009) cited in entry","stepsNote":"eclipse"}],"steps":[{"k":"spin","lab":"Spin it","cap":"The Earth turns once every 24 hours. Watch the dot for your place ride into the sunlight (sunrise) and out of it (sunset).","exam":"Day and night happen because the Earth spins on its axis once every 24 hours. The half of the Earth facing the Sun has day. As Aotearoa turns into the Sun's light we get sunrise, and as it turns away we get sunset.","trap":"Saying “the Sun moves across the sky” as the cause. It only looks that way because the Earth is turning.","big":"The big idea: the Sun does not go round us. The Earth spins 15° every hour, so each place takes turns facing the Sun.","kupu":["rā","pou tāwhirowhiro"]},{"k":"tilt","lab":"Tilt it","cap":"The axis leans 23.4° and keeps pointing the same way in space all year. Watch the axis on the orrery: it never swings round to follow the Sun.","exam":"The Earth's axis is tilted at 23.4° and points in the same direction in space as the Earth orbits the Sun. This means the Southern Hemisphere is tilted towards the Sun for part of the year and away from it six months later.","trap":"Drawing the axis tilting back and forth during the year. The tilt stays fixed; it is the Earth's position around the Sun that changes.","big":"The big idea: one tilt that never changes direction is enough to make the Southern Hemisphere lean towards the Sun in December and away in June.","kupu":["pou tāwhirowhiro","kaupeka"]},{"k":"season","lab":"Seasons","cap":"Run the year. In December the south leans in: sunlight hits Aotearoa more directly and the day lasts longer. In June it leans away.","exam":"In December the Southern Hemisphere is tilted towards the Sun, so sunlight hits New Zealand more directly and is concentrated on a smaller area, and the days are longer. More energy reaches each square metre for longer, so it is summer. In June we are tilted away, so the light is spread out and days are short, giving winter.","trap":"Blaming distance. The Earth is closest to the Sun in early January, which is summer for us but winter in the Northern Hemisphere, so distance cannot be the cause.","big":"The big idea: summer means direct sunlight plus long days, so more energy lands on each square metre for longer. Winter is the reverse.","kupu":["kaupeka"]},{"k":"lat","lab":"Compare places","cap":"Change the place. Invercargill swings from about 8½ to almost 16 hours of daylight. At the equator it barely moves from 12.","exam":"The further a place is from the equator, the more its day length changes through the year. On 21 December Invercargill gets about 15 h 49 min of daylight but Cape Reinga gets about 14 h 28 min, while at the equator day length stays close to 12 hours all year. This is because the tilt makes the circle of latitude cross more of the lit half of the Earth at higher latitudes.","trap":"Giving the pattern without numbers. Comparing two named places with real figures is what lifts an answer from Merit to Excellence.","big":"The big idea (Excellence): the effect varies with latitude. The further from the equator, the bigger the change in day length and Sun height through the year.","kupu":["rā"]},{"k":"phase","lab":"Moon phases","cap":"Half the Moon is always lit by the Sun. As it goes round us we see more or less of that lit half. From Aotearoa a growing Moon is lit on the left.","exam":"Half of the Moon is always lit by the Sun. As the Moon orbits the Earth we see different amounts of its lit half, which gives the phases. A full cycle of phases takes 29.5 days, a bit longer than the 27.3-day orbit, because the Earth has also moved along its orbit around the Sun.","trap":"Saying the phases are the Earth's shadow on the Moon. Earth's shadow only reaches the Moon during a lunar eclipse.","big":"The big idea: phases are about viewing angle, not shadow. The Moon orbits in 27.3 days, but phases repeat every 29.5 days because the Earth has moved on too.","kupu":["marama","mata o te marama"]},{"k":"tide","lab":"Tides","cap":"The Moon's pull makes two bulges of ocean. When the Sun lines up with the Moon (new or full) the bulges grow: spring tides. At right angles they shrink: neap tides.","exam":"The Moon's gravity pulls the ocean into two bulges, one facing the Moon and one on the opposite side. As the Earth spins through both, each place gets two high tides about every 24 h 50 min, so high tide is about 50 minutes later each day. At new and full Moon the Sun and Moon line up and their pulls add, giving spring tides with the biggest range. At first and last quarter they pull at right angles, giving neap tides with the smallest range.","trap":"Thinking spring tides only happen in spring. They happen twice every month, at new and full Moon.","big":"The big idea: the Sun's tidal pull is about 46% of the Moon's. Lined up they add, at right angles they partly cancel.","kupu":["tai pari","tai timu","marama"]},{"k":"eclipse","lab":"Eclipses","cap":"The Moon's orbit is tilted about 5°. Most months the new or full Moon sails above or below the Sun–Earth line. Run it until they line up.","exam":"The Moon's orbit is tilted about 5° to the Earth's orbit, so most months the new or full Moon passes above or below the line between the Sun and the Earth. An eclipse only happens when a new or full Moon occurs near the points where the two orbits cross. A solar eclipse needs a new Moon, when the Moon's shadow falls on the Earth. A lunar eclipse needs a full Moon, when the Moon moves into the Earth's shadow.","trap":"Asking why there isn't an eclipse every month and leaving out the 5° tilt of the Moon's orbit. That tilt is the whole answer.","big":"The big idea: an eclipse needs a new or full Moon that also sits where the two orbits cross. That only lines up in two short eclipse seasons a year.","kupu":["āraitanga","rā","marama"]},{"k":"test","lab":"Test yourself","cap":"Use the date slider and the place menu to set up each one, then press Check. The machine marks you using the same model that moves it.","exam":"","trap":"","big":"Exam tip: for Merit, explain why with a linking word (because, so, which means). For Excellence, compare how the effect changes with place or time, using numbers.","kupu":[]}],"challenges":[{"q":"Give Invercargill its longest day of the year.","check":"()=>placeIdx===3&&decl(t)<-23.2","ok":"Yes. Around 21 December the south is tilted most towards the Sun, so Invercargill gets about 15 h 49 min of daylight.","no":"()=>placeIdx!==3?\"Pick Invercargill in the place menu first.\":`Not yet: daylight is ${hm(dayLen(-46.41,decl(t)))}. Find when the Sun is overhead at 23.4°S.`","okIsFn":false,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"Find a spring tide.","check":"()=>tidePull(elong(t))>1.38","ok":"Yes. The Sun and Moon are lined up (new or full Moon), so their pulls add.","no":"()=>`Not yet: the Moon is ${phaseName(moonAge(t)).toLowerCase()}. Spring tides come at new and full Moon.`","okIsFn":false,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"Make an eclipse happen.","check":"()=>eclipse(t).includes(\"eclipse\")","ok":"()=>`Yes: ${eclipse(t).toLowerCase()}. The Moon is at a crossing point of the two orbits at the right phase.`","no":"()=>`Not yet: ${eclipse(t)===\"No\"?\"the Moon isn't new or full\":\"the Moon is \"+Math.abs(moonLat(t)).toFixed(1)+\"° off our orbit\"}. Try early February or early August 2027 on the slider.`","okIsFn":true,"noIsFn":true}],"controls":[{"type":"play","id":"play","label":""},{"type":"button","id":"today","label":"Today"},{"type":"range","id":"date","label":"Date"},{"type":"select","id":"place","label":"Place"}],"shell":"kit"},"data":{"id":"data","title":"The data detective","href":"m/data.html","desc":"Read a dot plot, turn it into a box plot, compare two groups, catch a graph with a sneaky axis, see how a biased sample misleads, and decide when to use rates instead of counts.","blurb":"Dot plots, box plots, sneaky graphs, biased samples, and rates vs counts.","tags":["NCEA L1 Maths 91946","Year 11"],"built":true,"eyebrow":"Maths and stats · Data","crumb":"NCEA Level 1 Mathematics and Statistics · interpreting data","badge":{"label":"NCEA L1 Maths 91946","exam":"2026-11-10T09:30:00+13:00","examText":"Tue 10 Nov, 9:30am"},"papers":[{"label":"NZQA: Mathematics and Statistics","url":"https://www2.nzqa.govt.nz/ncea/subjects/past-exams-and-exemplars/mathematics-and-statistics/"}],"truth":"Every number here is made up: the Kōwhai College screen-time survey, the two schools' sleep figures, the 400 students on the map and the two towns' cycling injuries. Quartiles use the medians of the lower and upper halves; outliers are more than 1.5 × IQR beyond the box. Your teacher may use a slightly different quartile method.","footer":"Covers AS 91946: extracting information from unseen graphs and tables, explaining variation, comparing groups, critiquing misleading graphs and biased samples, and using rates to make informed judgements.","mainLabel":"Interactive graphs of made-up survey data","kupu":[],"steps":[{"k":"read","lab":"Read it","cap":"Thirty Year 11s logged their school-day screen time. Drag the line and count who is below it.","exam":"Half of the 30 students spent less than 3.75 hours a day on screens on a school day, and half spent more.","trap":"A stack's height is how many students; its position along the axis is how many hours.","big":"Read the title, the axis and the units before you read a single dot.","kupu":[]},{"k":"spread","lab":"Middle and spread","cap":"Watch the dots drop into a box plot, then switch each part on and off.","exam":"The median school-day screen time was 3.75 hours, and the middle half of students used screens for between 3 and 5 hours (IQR 2 hours). One student's 11 hours is unusually high, which stretches the range to 9.5 hours.","trap":"The median is the middle student's value, not the middle of the axis or the mean.","big":"Five numbers (minimum, Q1, median, Q3, maximum) sum up a whole pile of dots.","kupu":[]},{"k":"compare","lab":"Compare","cap":"Same students, school days vs weekends. Compare the middles, then the overlap.","exam":"For these students, weekend screen time tends to be higher than school-day screen time: the median is 5.75 hours compared with 3.75 hours. The weekend median is above the whole school-day box and the boxes overlap by only 0.5 hours, so the shift is clear.","trap":"Don't write \"always higher\": some students use screens more on a school day than others do at the weekend.","big":"Look for shift (the medians) and overlap (the boxes) before you make a call.","kupu":[]},{"k":"mislead","lab":"Sneaky graphs","cap":"A newspaper drew this chart. Slide the axis start back to 0 and watch the gap shrink.","exam":"The vertical axis starts at 46%, not 0, so Kōwhai College's bar looks about 3 times taller, but 52% is only 1.08 times 48%. The difference is small and the graph exaggerates it.","trap":"Comparing bar heights without checking where the vertical axis starts.","big":"The same numbers can tell a different story depending on how they're drawn.","kupu":[]},{"k":"sample","lab":"Who did they ask?","cap":"400 students, and 38% walk or bike. Choose who to ask and see what your sample says.","exam":"Asking friends or people at a bus stop isn't random: friends often live near each other and everyone at a bus stop is catching the bus, so these samples give a biased answer. A random sample of 100 or more is much more likely to be close to the true proportion.","trap":"A bigger sample doesn't fix a biased one: a thousand people at the bus stop still miss the walkers.","big":"Who you ask matters more than how many you ask.","kupu":[]},{"k":"rates","lab":"Counts or rates?","cap":"Town A has twice as many injuries. Is it riskier? Switch to rates and decide.","exam":"Town A had more injuries (120 vs 60), but it also had 4 times as many cyclists. Per 1000 cyclists, Town A had 30 injuries and Town B had 60, so a cyclist was less likely to be hurt in Town A.","trap":"Comparing raw counts when the groups are different sizes.","big":"Rates (per 100, per 1000) let you compare groups of different sizes fairly.","kupu":[]},{"k":"test","lab":"Test yourself","cap":"Four cases on the board. Use the controls to crack each one.","exam":"Back every judgement with a number from the data and a reason about how the data were collected or drawn.","trap":"","big":"Read it, sum it up, compare it, then question it.","kupu":[]}],"challenges":[{"q":"Move the line to the median, so exactly half the students are below it.","check":"()=>s.line===F.med","ok":"Yes: 3.75 hours. 15 students are below it and 15 above.","no":"()=>{const b=below(s.line);return b<15?`Only ${b} are below ${fh(s.line)} h. Move the line right.`:b>15?`${b} are below ${fh(s.line)} h. Move it left.`:`15 are below, but ${onL(s.line)} sit on the line. Nudge it a quarter-hour.`}","okIsFn":false,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"Make the newspaper's bar chart fair.","check":"()=>s.axis===0","ok":"Fair: with the axis at 0, 52% and 48% look almost the same, because they are.","no":"()=>`The axis still starts at ${s.axis}%. Slide it down to 0.`","okIsFn":false,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"Take a random sample of at least 100 students that lands within 5 points of the true 38%.","check":"()=>s.method===\"random\"&&s.n>=100&&Math.abs(sampPct()-TRUE)<=5","ok":"()=>`Nice: ${pc(sampPct())}% from ${s.sample.length} random students.`","no":"()=>s.method!==\"random\"?`${{friends:\"Asking your friends\",online:\"An online poll\",bus:\"Asking at the bus stop\"}[s.method]} isn't a random sample. Switch to Random.`:s.n<100?`Only ${s.n} students. Ask at least 100.`:`${pc(sampPct())}% this time. Take a new sample: random samples wobble.`","okIsFn":true,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"Show the rates and pick the town where a cyclist is less likely to be hurt.","check":"()=>s.view===\"rates\"&&s.pick===SAFER","ok":"Yes: Town A has 30 injuries per 1000 cyclists, Town B has 60.","no":"()=>s.view!==\"rates\"?\"Switch the view to rates first.\":s.pick?\"Look again at the rate per 1000, not the pile of injuries.\":\"Pick a town.\"","okIsFn":false,"noIsFn":true}],"controls":[{"type":"range","id":"line","label":"Line"},{"type":"button","id":"replay","label":"Drop the dots again"},{"type":"toggle","id":"tMed","label":"Median"},{"type":"toggle","id":"tBox","label":"Middle half (IQR)"},{"type":"toggle","id":"tWh","label":"Range"},{"type":"toggle","id":"tOut","label":"Outlier"},{"type":"toggle","id":"tShade","label":"Medians and overlap"},{"type":"range","id":"axis","label":"Axis starts at"},{"type":"toggle","id":"tBrk","label":"Show the axis break"},{"type":"select","id":"method","label":"Ask"},{"type":"range","id":"n","label":"Random sample"},{"type":"button","id":"resample","label":"Take a new sample"},{"type":"select","id":"view","label":"Show"},{"type":"select","id":"pick","label":"Less risky"}],"shell":"kit"},"text":{"id":"text","title":"The text studio","href":"m/text.html","desc":"An original poem on a lightbox: light up its techniques, name them, grow a web of connotations, trace its shape to the turning point, build a theme, then plan two aspects of your studied text.","blurb":"Light up a poem’s techniques, trace its turning point, build a theme, plan two aspects.","tags":["NCEA L1 English 91927","NCEA L1 English 91925","Year 11"],"built":true,"eyebrow":"English · Unfamiliar and studied texts","crumb":"NCEA Level 1 English · unfamiliar texts (91927) and studied text (91925)","badge":{"label":"NCEA L1 English 91927","exam":"2026-11-17T09:30:00+13:00","examText":"Tue 17 Nov, 9:30am"},"papers":[{"label":"NZQA: English","url":"https://www2.nzqa.govt.nz/ncea/subjects/past-exams-and-exemplars/english/"}],"truth":"“Low Tide” is an original poem written for assembly. The techniques, moods and themes shown are one valid reading; other well-argued readings can earn the same credit.","footer":"Covers AS 91927 (language features, structure, purpose and ideas in unfamiliar texts, with evidence) and AS 91925 (two specific aspects of a studied text, linked to purpose and the wider world).","mainLabel":"The poem Low Tide on a lightbox, with techniques highlighted","kupu":[],"steps":[{"k":"spot","lab":"Lightbox","cap":"Switch on a technique and watch it glow in the poem. Tap a glowing phrase to see its name.","exam":"The poet personifies the estuary, which “breathes out at dawn”, so the place feels alive but tired before the day has even begun.","trap":"Naming a technique earns little on its own: quote the exact words and say what they do.","big":"Techniques are the writer’s tools; marks come from explaining what each one does to us.","kupu":[]},{"k":"name","lab":"Name it","cap":"One glowing phrase at a time. Pick the technique that fits.","exam":"The simile “like a sheet of tinfoil” makes the mud look bright, flat and artificial, so the morning feels harsh rather than beautiful.","trap":"Similes and metaphors both compare; only a simile uses “like” or “as”.","big":"Naming is step one; the effect on the reader is what the exam rewards.","kupu":[]},{"k":"effect","lab":"Zoom in","cap":"Pick a phrase and watch its meanings grow: words, then a feeling, then a big idea.","exam":"The repetition in “grip and grip and grip” suggests desperate holding on, as if the mangroves are afraid to let go. It builds an anxious mood that hints at the grandfather holding on to time.","trap":"Skip “it makes the reader want to read on”: say what the words make us feel and think.","big":"Connotations are the feelings a word carries beyond its dictionary meaning.","kupu":[]},{"k":"shape","lab":"Shape","cap":"Slide through the poem. Tension builds as the tide goes out, then one short line turns it.","exam":"The poem turns at the short sentence “Then the water turns.” After it, the harsh, tense images give way to soft, calm ones, showing acceptance.","trap":"Structure isn’t “it has twelve lines”: explain where the mood shifts and why.","big":"A turning point is where a poem changes direction, mood or idea.","kupu":[]},{"k":"theme","lab":"Theme","cap":"A theme is a subject plus what the text says about it. Build one strong enough for the exam.","exam":"The poem suggests that time can’t be held back, however tightly we grip, but that it can be met calmly, like the tide that returns “soft, certain”.","trap":"One word, like “time”, is a topic, not a theme: add what the text says about it.","big":"Theme = subject + message + evidence.","kupu":[]},{"k":"aspects","lab":"Two aspects","cap":"For your studied text: pick two different aspects and link them to the writer’s purpose and our world.","exam":"In my studied text, the writer uses the setting and a recurring symbol to warn us about [the big idea], a warning that still matters today because [a link to our world].","trap":"“Character” and “characters” are one aspect twice: pick two different, specific aspects.","big":"Strong 91925 answers link both aspects to why the text was written and why it still matters.","kupu":[]},{"k":"test","lab":"Test yourself","cap":"Four quick wins. Tap a tile to jump back to its step.","exam":"","trap":"","big":"","kupu":[]}],"challenges":[{"q":"Light up all seven techniques on the lightbox at once.","check":"()=>litCount()===7","ok":"All seven glowing: that’s a full set of evidence.","no":"()=>`${litCount()} of 7 on. Go to Lightbox and switch on the rest.`","okIsFn":false,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"Name at least 5 phrases correctly in Name it.","check":"()=>score()>=5","ok":"Five or more named. Now say what each one does.","no":"()=>`${score()} right so far. Use the hints in Name it.`","okIsFn":false,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"Put the Shape marker on the poem’s turning point.","check":"()=>s.line===10","ok":"Line 10: “Then the water turns.” The shortest sentence is the turn.","no":"()=>`Line ${s.line} isn’t it. Look for the shortest sentence, where the mood flips.`","okIsFn":false,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"Build a theme statement with strength 3 of 3.","check":"()=>strength()===3","ok":"Subject, message and evidence: that’s a theme.","no":"()=>strength()===0?\"Pick a subject and a message in Theme.\":`Strength ${strength()} of 3. Make the message specific and true to the poem.`","okIsFn":false,"noIsFn":true}],"controls":[{"type":"toggle","id":"t_pers","label":"Personification"},{"type":"toggle","id":"t_meta","label":"Metaphor"},{"type":"toggle","id":"t_sim","label":"Simile"},{"type":"toggle","id":"t_rep","label":"Repetition"},{"type":"toggle","id":"t_sib","label":"Sibilance"},{"type":"toggle","id":"t_short","label":"Short sentence"},{"type":"toggle","id":"t_sym","label":"Symbol"},{"type":"button","id":"allon","label":"All on"},{"type":"button","id":"clear","label":"Clear"},{"type":"button","id":"prev","label":"← Back"},{"type":"button","id":"next","label":"Next phrase →"},{"type":"toggle","id":"a_pers","label":"Personification"},{"type":"toggle","id":"a_meta","label":"Metaphor"},{"type":"toggle","id":"a_sim","label":"Simile"},{"type":"toggle","id":"a_rep","label":"Repetition"},{"type":"toggle","id":"a_sib","label":"Sibilance"},{"type":"toggle","id":"a_short","label":"Short sentence"},{"type":"toggle","id":"a_sym","label":"Symbol"},{"type":"select","id":"ef","label":"Phrase"},{"type":"button","id":"grow","label":"Grow it again"},{"type":"range","id":"line","label":"Line"},{"type":"select","id":"subj","label":"Subject"},{"type":"select","id":"msg","label":"Message"},{"type":"select","id":"a1","label":"Aspect 1"},{"type":"select","id":"a2","label":"Aspect 2"},{"type":"select","id":"purp","label":"Purpose"}],"shell":"kit"},"materials":{"id":"materials","title":"The materials lab","href":"m/materials.html","desc":"Test seven materials on a bench, zoom into their particles, see why some conduct and others shatter, pick the right material for the job, and weigh copper against aluminium for power lines.","blurb":"Test seven materials, zoom into their particles, pick the right one for the job.","tags":["NCEA L1 Chem & Bio 92023","Year 11"],"built":true,"eyebrow":"Chemistry · Materials","crumb":"NCEA Level 1 Chemistry and Biology · physical properties of materials","badge":{"label":"NCEA L1 Chem & Bio 92023","exam":"2026-11-19T14:00:00+13:00","examText":"Thu 19 Nov, 2pm"},"papers":[{"label":"NZQA: Chemistry and Biology","url":"https://www2.nzqa.govt.nz/ncea/subjects/past-exams-and-exemplars/chemistry-and-biology/"}],"truth":"Property values are typical textbook values: wax melts over a range (about 46–68 °C) and HDPE softens near 130 °C depending on the grade. Diamond and graphite change only above about 3500 °C. Particle diagrams are 2D sketches of 3D structures. Power-line cables are sized for the same resistance (0.1 Ω per km) using resistivities of 1.68 × 10⁻⁸ Ω·m for copper and 2.65 × 10⁻⁸ Ω·m for aluminium; sag is drawn in proportion to weight per metre.","footer":"Covers AS 92023: metallic, ionic, molecular, polymer and covalent network materials, how their particles and forces explain density, conductivity, melting point, solubility, malleability and hardness, and how those properties decide their uses.","mainLabel":"The materials lab: samples, tests and particle diagrams","kupu":[],"steps":[{"k":"bench","lab":"Test bench","cap":"Pick a sample and run four quick tests: does it conduct, melt, bend or dissolve?","exam":"Copper conducts and bends without breaking because it is a metal. Sodium chloride doesn't conduct as a solid and shatters when hit because it is ionic.","trap":"Dense doesn't mean hard: diamond is only 3.51 g/cm³ but it's the hardest natural material.","big":"Every property comes from the particles and how strongly they're held together.","kupu":[]},{"k":"zoom","lab":"Zoom in","cap":"Zoom right in to the particles. Turn up the heat: they vibrate harder, then break free and melt.","exam":"Copper is made of positive metal ions in a sea of delocalised electrons. The strong attraction between the ions and electrons acts in all directions, so a lot of energy is needed to separate the particles and it melts at a high temperature (1085 °C).","trap":"Melting a molecular solid doesn't break the bonds inside molecules, only the weak forces between them.","big":"Strong forces in all directions mean a high melting point; weak forces between molecules mean a low one.","kupu":[]},{"k":"conduct","lab":"Why it conducts","cap":"A current needs charged particles that are free to move. Change the state and watch what flows.","exam":"Copper conducts as a solid and when molten because its delocalised electrons are free to move through the structure and carry the current.","trap":"Dissolved salt conducts because its ions move, not because of free electrons.","big":"No charged particles free to move means no current.","kupu":[]},{"k":"hammer","lab":"Hammer test","cap":"Push the top layers along. Metal layers slide and stay bonded; ionic layers line up like charges and crack.","exam":"Metals are malleable because layers of ions can slide over each other while the delocalised electrons keep holding them together. Ionic solids are brittle: when a layer shifts, ions with the same charge line up, repel, and the crystal splits.","trap":"Hard isn't the same as tough: diamond is the hardest material, but a sharp blow can chip it.","big":"Metallic bonding doesn't point in one direction, so metal layers can slide without breaking.","kupu":[]},{"k":"use","lab":"Pick for the job","cap":"Choose a job, then the material whose properties fit it best. Watch the ticks.","exam":"Copper suits electrical wire: its delocalised electrons are free to move, so it conducts very well, and its layers of ions can slide, so it can be drawn into thin wire without breaking.","trap":"Don't just name a property: link it to the particles, then to the job.","big":"Property, particles, use: an Excellence answer explains all three links.","kupu":[]},{"k":"tradeoff","lab":"Trade-off","cap":"Same resistance, two metals. Aluminium needs a thicker cable, but it's about half the weight.","exam":"Copper conducts better, but aluminium's density is only 2.70 g/cm³ compared with 8.96 g/cm³. For the same resistance an aluminium cable has about half the mass, so it sags less, pylons can be further apart and it costs less. Real lines wrap aluminium strands around a steel core for strength.","trap":"The best conductor isn't automatically the best choice: weigh up mass, cost and strength too.","big":"Evaluating means weighing more than one property against the job.","kupu":[]},{"k":"test","lab":"Test yourself","cap":"Four challenges. Use the controls here or go back to any step: your choices carry over.","exam":"For an evaluate question: name the property the use needs, explain it with the particles and forces, then weigh up a trade-off.","trap":"Saying “it's strong” isn't enough: say which particles, which forces, and what that means for the job.","big":"Structure decides properties; properties decide uses.","kupu":[]}],"challenges":[{"q":"Find the material that conducts only when it's molten or dissolved, and make it conduct.","check":"()=>s.mat===\"nacl\"&&(s.cstate===\"molten\"||s.cstate===\"dissolved\")","ok":"Yes: sodium chloride. Its ions are fixed in the solid but free to move when molten or dissolved.","no":"()=>s.mat!==\"nacl\"?\"Not that material. Which one is made of ions?\":\"Right material. Now melt it or dissolve it in water.\"","okIsFn":false,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"Make the ionic crystal shatter in the hammer test.","check":"()=>s.hamMat===\"ionic\"&&s.shift>=.9","ok":"Crack! Like charges lined up and repelled, so the crystal split.","no":"()=>s.hamMat!==\"ionic\"?\"Switch the lattice to ionic (salt) first.\":\"Push the top layers further, until like charges sit on top of each other.\"","okIsFn":false,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"Get a perfect fit for three different jobs.","check":"()=>fullJobs()>=3","ok":"Three perfect fits: property, particles, use.","no":"()=>`${fullJobs()} of 3 so far. Pick a job, then find the material that ticks every need.`","okIsFn":false,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"Choose the metal for long overhead power lines, and why.","check":"()=>s.line===\"al\"&&s.why===\"light\"","ok":"Yes: aluminium. Same resistance at about half the mass.","no":"()=>s.line===\"cu\"?\"Copper conducts better, but compare the mass per km.\":s.line!==\"al\"?\"Choose a metal for the line first.\":s.why===\"cond\"?\"Aluminium's right, but copper actually conducts better. What's aluminium's real advantage?\":\"Aluminium's right. Now pick the reason that matches the numbers.\"","okIsFn":false,"noIsFn":true}],"controls":[{"type":"play","id":"play","label":""},{"type":"select","id":"mat","label":"Material"},{"type":"range","id":"temp","label":"Heat to"},{"type":"select","id":"cstate","label":"State"},{"type":"select","id":"hamMat","label":"Lattice"},{"type":"range","id":"shift","label":"Push top layers"},{"type":"select","id":"job","label":"Job"},{"type":"select","id":"pick","label":"Material for this job"},{"type":"range","id":"len","label":"Line length"},{"type":"select","id":"line","label":"Use for the line"},{"type":"select","id":"why","label":"Because"}],"shell":"kit"},"claims":{"id":"claims","title":"The claim checker","href":"m/claims.html","desc":"Pull apart a hype post for an invented brain drink: who's behind it, how many people were tested, whether the test was fair, linked versus caused, and the words scientists would actually use.","blurb":"Pull apart a hype post: who’s behind it, sample size, fair tests, linked vs caused.","tags":["NCEA L1 Science 91923","Year 11"],"built":true,"eyebrow":"Science · Claims and evidence","crumb":"NCEA Level 1 Science · science-related claims","badge":{"label":"NCEA L1 Science 91923","exam":"2026-11-25T14:00:00+13:00","examText":"Wed 25 Nov, 2pm"},"papers":[{"label":"NZQA: Science","url":"https://www2.nzqa.govt.nz/ncea/subjects/past-exams-and-exemplars/science/"}],"truth":"FocusFizz, @study.with.tia, the post, the sources and every study and data point here are invented for learning. The sample simulation gives both drinks exactly the same effect (none), so every gap you see is chance.","footer":"Covers AS 91923: identifying a science-related claim, critiquing the evidence behind it (sample size, fair testing, correlation and causation), the source and channel, and the use of science language.","mainLabel":"An invented social media post about a brain drink, and the tools to check its claim","kupu":[],"steps":[{"k":"apart","lab":"Pull it apart","cap":"A post makes a big promise. Light up each lens: who's behind it, the hype, the claim and the evidence.","exam":"The claim is that drinking FocusFizz raises test scores by 20%. The only evidence is the company's own study of 12 students, compared with their last quiz instead of a control group, so it can't show the drink caused the change.","trap":"Don't just repeat the claim: say what evidence backs it, and what's missing.","big":"Every claim has three parts to check: what's claimed, the evidence behind it, and who's saying it.","kupu":[]},{"k":"source","lab":"Who says so?","cap":"Same claim, different messenger. Pick a source and watch the trust dial move.","exam":"The post was paid for by FocusFizz, which sells the drink, so the source has a conflict of interest. A peer-reviewed article by independent scientists that shows its data would be more reliable.","trap":"Lots of likes and views don't make a source reliable: popularity isn't peer review.","big":"Trust grows when a source is expert, independent, peer-reviewed and shows its data.","kupu":[]},{"k":"sample","lab":"How many?","cap":"Here the drink does nothing at all. Run studies and watch chance fake a result when groups are small.","exam":"With only 12 students, random variation alone could produce a 20% difference in scores. A much larger sample reduces the effect of chance, so its result would be more reliable.","trap":"A big percentage from a tiny sample isn't strong evidence: it may just be chance.","big":"Small samples swing by chance; bigger samples settle close to the truth.","kupu":[]},{"k":"fair","lab":"Fair test","cap":"Design a fair test for FocusFizz. Each feature you switch on fills the fairness meter.","exam":"A fair test needs a control group given water, students put into groups at random, the same test for both groups, and blinding so nobody knows who had which drink. The FocusFizz study had none of these.","trap":"Comparing with “last time” isn't a control: students may simply have studied more or had an easier quiz.","big":"A fair test changes one thing and keeps everything else the same.","kupu":[]},{"k":"cause","lab":"Linked or caused?","cap":"Ice-block sales and sunburn rise together. Does one cause the other? Reveal what's hiding.","exam":"Ice-block sales and sunburn cases show a strong positive correlation (r ≈ 0.93), but ice blocks don't cause sunburn. Both go up on hot, sunny days, which is the hidden factor.","trap":"Correlation doesn't prove causation: look for a hidden factor that drives both.","big":"Two things rising together doesn't mean one causes the other.","kupu":[]},{"k":"words","lab":"Science words","cap":"Hype words sound scientific. Flip each card to see what a scientist would write instead.","exam":"“Chemical-free” is misleading because everything, including water, is made of chemicals. Scientists use cautious words like “the results suggest” because one small study can't prove a claim.","trap":"“Natural” doesn't mean safe, and it doesn't mean it works.","big":"Science language is precise and careful; hype language is vague and certain.","kupu":[]},{"k":"test","lab":"Your verdict","cap":"Check the claim like an examiner: the source, the sample, a fair test, then your verdict.","exam":"Overall the claim is weak. It rests on a tiny study of 12 students with no control group, run by the company that sells the drink, and it uses hype words instead of data.","trap":"A judgement needs reasons: “it's weak” on its own won't earn the marks.","big":"Judge a claim on its evidence, not on how confident it sounds.","kupu":[]}],"challenges":[{"q":"Pick a source that passes all four trust checks.","check":"()=>s.src===\"journal\"","ok":"Yes: expert, independent, peer-reviewed, and it shows its data.","no":"()=>`${SRC[s.src].name} passes ${score(s.src)} of 4. Which source is checked by other scientists and shows its data?`","okIsFn":false,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"No real effect here. Run tiny studies (5 or 10 per group) until chance “finds” a gain of +15% or more.","check":"()=>s.swing","ok":"There it is: a big “effect” from pure chance. That's why 12 students isn't enough.","no":"Not yet. Set students per group to 5 or 10 and keep pressing Run a study.","okIsFn":false,"noIsFn":false},{"q":"Now run 10 studies in a row with 200 or more per group. Can chance still fake a big gain?","check":"()=>s.steady","ok":"No: all ten land close to zero. Big samples drown out chance.","no":"()=>`${bigCount()} of 10 so far with 200+ per group. Slide students per group to 200 or more and keep running.`","okIsFn":false,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"Switch on all five fair-test features, then give your verdict on the FocusFizz post.","check":"()=>fairN()===5&&s.verdict===\"weak\"","ok":"Spot on: a fair test would settle it, and the post's evidence is weak.","no":"()=>fairN()<5?`${fairN()} of 5 features on. Which ones are still missing?`:s.verdict?\"Check your verdict: what does the evidence actually show?\":\"All five on. Now choose your verdict.\"","okIsFn":false,"noIsFn":true}],"controls":[{"type":"toggle","id":"hlWho","label":"Who's behind it"},{"type":"toggle","id":"hlHype","label":"Hype words"},{"type":"toggle","id":"hlClaim","label":"The claim"},{"type":"toggle","id":"hlEv","label":"The evidence"},{"type":"button","id":"hlAll","label":"Show all"},{"type":"select","id":"src","label":"Source"},{"type":"play","id":"play","label":""},{"type":"button","id":"run","label":"Run a study"},{"type":"range","id":"n","label":"Students per group"},{"type":"button","id":"clear","label":"Clear"},{"type":"toggle","id":"fControl","label":"Control group"},{"type":"toggle","id":"fRandom","label":"Random groups"},{"type":"toggle","id":"fSame","label":"Same test"},{"type":"toggle","id":"fBlind","label":"Blind"},{"type":"toggle","id":"fBig","label":"Big sample"},{"type":"toggle","id":"reveal","label":"Reveal the hidden factor"},{"type":"select","id":"word","label":"Hype word"},{"type":"select","id":"verdict","label":"Your verdict"}],"shell":"kit"},"vend":{"id":"vend","title":"The vending machine","href":"m/vend.html","desc":"A see-through 3D vending machine you can turn and take apart: a coin's free fall, the torque on a coil motor, a 350 g can dropping 0.80 m, the impulse when it lands, why \"twice as fast\" is a worse design, and what a day's refrigeration costs per can.","blurb":"A see-through 3D vending machine: free fall, torque, impulse and the cost of a cold can.","tags":["NCEA L2 Physics 91171","Year 12"],"built":true,"eyebrow":"Physics · Mechanics","crumb":"NCEA Level 2 Physics · AS91171 Demonstrate understanding of mechanics","badge":{"label":"NCEA L2 Physics 91171","exam":"2026-11-18T14:00:00+13:00","examText":"Wed 18 Nov, 2pm"},"papers":[{"label":"NZQA: Physics past exams and exemplars","url":"https://www2.nzqa.govt.nz/ncea/subjects/past-exams-and-exemplars/physics/"},{"label":"NZQA: AS91171 standard and specifications","url":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/ncea/assessment/view-detailed.do?standardNumber=91171"}],"truth":"A stylised model of a spiral vending machine: cabinet 1830 × 990 × 850 mm, 6 trays × 5 coils, a 350 g can dropping 0.80 m from tray 3 and stopping in 0.050 s on a rubber pad (figures from the practice pack). Coil radius, tray friction (μ), motor speed, the coin's 0.15 m drop and the fridge powers (120 W compressor, 20 W lights, 10 W standby, 30 c/kWh) are estimates. g = 9.8 m s⁻², no air resistance, and the falls are slowed about three times so you can see them. Parts list: vend.parts.md.","footer":"Covers AS91171 (L2 Mechanics): kinematic equations and free fall, Ep and Ek, momentum and impulse (F = Δp/Δt), torque (τ = Fr) and rotational power (P = τω), and energy, power and cost (E = Pt). Add ?lite to the address for a still image instead of the 3D model.","mainLabel":"A see-through 3D vending machine","kupu":[{"mi":"tōpana","en":"force","ta":40375,"note":"","chip":["force","F"],"evidence":"Te Reo Pūtaiao (MoE 2009) cited in entry","stepsNote":"apart, coil, impact"},{"mi":"papatipu","en":"mass","ta":5179,"note":"Papatipu also means ancestral land. In science it means mass.","chip":["mass"],"evidence":"Te Reo Pūtaiao (MoE 2009) cited in entry","stepsNote":"look"},{"mi":"tere","en":"speed","ta":7953,"note":"Also means quick or fast.","chip":["v","coinv"],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"coin, fall, impact, faster"},{"mi":"whakahohoro","en":"acceleration, speeding up","ta":12447,"note":"A general word for speeding up. Science classes may use another term, so check with your teacher.","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"coin"},{"mi":"tō ā-papa","en":"gravity","ta":12845,"note":"Te Aka also lists kume ā-papa.","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Reo Pūtaiao (MoE 2009) cited in entry","stepsNote":"coin, fall"},{"mi":"pūngao neke","en":"kinetic energy","ta":47721,"note":"Te Aka also lists pūngao konikoni.","chip":["ek"],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"fall, faster"},{"mi":"pūngao moe","en":"potential energy","ta":47720,"note":"","chip":["ep"],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"look"},{"mi":"teitei","en":"height","ta":7895,"note":"","chip":["h","coinh"],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"look, faster"}],"steps":[{"k":"look","lab":"Look inside","cap":"A real spiral vending machine, drawn see-through so you can watch it work. Drag to turn it. Six trays of coils, a coin mechanism in the right-hand column, a delivery bin at the bottom and a fridge deck behind.","exam":"Gravitational potential energy Ep = mgh, measured from the level where the object stops. A 0.350 kg can on tray 3, 0.80 m above the landing pad, has Ep = 0.350 × 9.8 × 0.80 = 2.74 J relative to the pad.","trap":"Measuring h from the floor instead of from where the can stops. The h in Ep = mgh is the drop, so choose your reference level and say what it is.","big":"The big idea: every question in this machine is about one object at a time: a coin, a can, a coil. Pick the object, draw its forces, choose the equation.","kupu":["teitei","papatipu","pūngao moe"]},{"k":"apart","lab":"Take it apart","cap":"Slide the parts out to see how it fits together: the glass door and the service panel come forward, the trays spread out, the coils and cans slide out of their trays, the fridge deck drops below.","exam":"The motor exerts a torque on the coil, τ = Fr. Each turn of the coil moves the row of cans forward one pitch (70 mm), doing work W = Fd against friction. Once the front can leaves the tray its only significant force is gravity, so it accelerates downwards at 9.8 m s⁻².","trap":"Treating the whole machine as one object. Pick the object the question is about (the can, the coin, the coil) and draw the forces on it alone.","big":"The big idea: a vending machine is a chain of simple mechanisms. The motor applies a torque to the coil (τ = Fr), the coil does work pushing a can forward (W = Fd), gravity does the rest once the can leaves the tray.","kupu":["tōpana"]},{"k":"coin","lab":"The coin drop","cap":"Press play to post a $2 coin (10 g). It falls about 0.15 m past the validator's sensors, which time it to check it's real. How long does that take, and how fast is it going at the bottom?","exam":"Taking down as positive with u = 0: s = ut + ½at², so t = √(2s ÷ g) = √(2 × 0.15 ÷ 9.8) = 0.175 s, and v = u + at = 9.8 × 0.175 = 1.71 m s⁻¹ (or v² = u² + 2as). The mass of the coin does not appear, because all objects fall with the same acceleration.","trap":"Mixing units (15 cm must become 0.15 m) and choosing an equation that needs a quantity you don't have. If you have no time, use v² = u² + 2as.","big":"The big idea: the kinematic equations. With u = 0 and a = g you can find time from distance (s = ½gt²) and speed from either (v = gt, or v² = 2gs). The coin's mass never appears.","kupu":["tō ā-papa","whakahohoro","tere"]},{"k":"coil","lab":"The coil turns","cap":"The motor turns the coil one full turn and the whole row of cans moves forward 70 mm. The more cans in the coil and the rougher the tray, the more friction the motor must overcome, and the more torque it needs.","exam":"To push 7 cans with μ = 0.3, the friction to overcome is F = μmg × 7 = 0.3 × 0.350 × 9.8 × 7 = 7.2 N, acting at the coil radius r = 0.072 m, so the motor's torque is τ = Fr = 0.52 N m. At 30 rpm, ω = 2π × 30 ÷ 60 = 3.14 rad s⁻¹, so the power is P = τω ≈ 1.6 W.","trap":"Using rpm as ω. Convert: ω = 2π × (rpm ÷ 60) in rad s⁻¹. One full turn is 2π radians.","big":"The big idea: torque is force times radius, τ = Fr. Rotating things also have power: P = τω, with ω in radians per second.","kupu":["tōpana"]},{"k":"fall","lab":"The can falls","cap":"The front can tips off the tray and free-falls to the rubber pad. Change the drop height (which tray) and watch the speed it lands at. Tray 3 is the pack's 0.80 m drop.","exam":"v² = u² + 2as = 0 + 2 × 9.8 × 0.80 = 15.68, so v = 3.96 ≈ 4.0 m s⁻¹. The fall takes t = √(2h ÷ g) = √(1.6 ÷ 9.8) = 0.40 s. The can's kinetic energy on landing equals the potential energy it lost, ½mv² = mgh = 2.7 J.","trap":"Forgetting the square root (giving 15.7 m s⁻¹), or rounding too early. Keep 3.96 for the next step and round the final answer.","big":"The big idea: v² = u² + 2as. Dropped from rest, landing speed depends only on height and g, not on mass.","kupu":["tere","tō ā-papa","pūngao neke"]},{"k":"impact","lab":"Landing: impulse","cap":"The can is brought to rest by the pad. Momentum has to go to zero either way; the time it takes decides the force. Compare the rubber pad (0.050 s) with a bare steel tray.","exam":"Δp = mΔv = 0.350 × 3.96 = 1.39 kg m s⁻¹. F = Δp ÷ Δt = 1.39 ÷ 0.050 ≈ 27.7 N. The rubber pad lengthens Δt, so for the same change in momentum the average force is smaller and the can (and the machine) survives. On a steel tray the stop might take 0.010 s, five times the force.","trap":"Confusing impulse with force. The impulse (Δp) is fixed by the landing speed; the stopping time decides the force. If you ignore the can's weight (3.4 N) in the net force, say so.","big":"The big idea: impulse. Δp = FΔt, so F = Δp ÷ Δt. Longer stopping time, smaller force, for the same change in momentum.","kupu":["tōpana","tere"]},{"k":"faster","lab":"\"Twice as fast\"","cap":"A student claims the machine could dispense \"twice as fast\" by doubling the drop height. Set the drop to 1.60 m and compare with the 0.80 m baseline: speed, momentum, energy and time saved.","exam":"Doubling h to 1.6 m gives v = √(2 × 9.8 × 1.6) = 5.6 m s⁻¹, only √2 ≈ 1.41 times faster, because v ∝ √h, not h. The impact momentum rises by 41 % and the kinetic energy doubles (Ek = mgh). The can arrives only 0.17 s sooner but hits harder: louder, dented cans and broken bottles. \"Faster\" is a worse design outcome.","trap":"\"Double the height, double the speed.\" Speed grows with the square root of height; energy grows in proportion to it.","big":"The big idea: v ∝ √h. Doubling the height multiplies the landing speed by √2 ≈ 1.41, the momentum by 1.41 and the energy by 2. Excellence answers quantify the trade-off.","kupu":["tere","pūngao neke","teitei"]},{"k":"cold","lab":"Keeping it cold","cap":"Behind the bin, a compressor pumps refrigerant to the evaporator at the back of the trays; the fans move the air. It cycles on and off. What does a day cost, and what is that per can sold? (Powers are estimates.)","exam":"Energy = power × time. A 120 W compressor running 50 % of the day uses 0.120 kW × 12 h = 1.44 kWh; with 20 W of lights and 10 W standby all day the machine uses about 2.2 kWh a day, costing about 65 cents at 30 c/kWh. Spread over 40 vends that is about 1.6 cents a can.","trap":"kW and kWh. kW is a rate (power); kWh is an amount of energy. A 120 W compressor does not \"use 120 W per hour\".","big":"The big idea: energy = power × time. Power is a rate (W or kW); energy is an amount (J or kWh). Electricity is sold by the kilowatt-hour.","kupu":[]},{"k":"test","lab":"Test yourself","cap":"Five challenges. Use the controls from any step, then press Check. The machine marks you with the same physics that moves the figure.","exam":"","trap":"","big":"Exam tip: state the equation, substitute with units, then answer the question that was asked: a number with a unit, or a comparison with a reason.","kupu":[]}],"challenges":[{"q":"Choose the drop height that makes the can land at about 4.0 m s⁻¹.","check":"()=>Math.abs(s.h-0.8)<1e-6","ok":"()=>`Yes: tray 3, h = 0.80 m. v = √(2gh) = √(2 × 9.8 × 0.80) = ${fmt(vFall(0.8),2)} m s⁻¹.`","no":"()=>`Not yet: from ${fmt(s.h,2)} m the can lands at ${fmt(vFall(s.h),2)} m s⁻¹. Rearrange v² = 2gh for h.`","okIsFn":true,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"Keep the 0.80 m drop, and set a stopping time that keeps the average force on the can under 15 N.","check":"()=>Math.abs(s.h-0.8)<1e-6&&Fimp()<15","ok":"()=>`Yes: Δt = ${fmt(s.dt,3)} s gives F = ${fmt(pFall(0.8),2)} ÷ ${fmt(s.dt,3)} = ${fmt(Fimp(),1)} N. Same impulse, longer time, smaller force.`","no":"()=>Math.abs(s.h-0.8)>1e-6?\"Set the drop to 0.80 m first.\":`Not yet: F = ${fmt(pFall(0.8),2)} ÷ ${fmt(s.dt,3)} = ${fmt(Fimp(),1)} N. You need Δt > 1.39 ÷ 15 = 0.093 s.`","okIsFn":true,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"Load the coil so the motor has to supply more than 0.5 N m of torque.","check":"()=>torque()>0.5","ok":"()=>`Yes: F = μ n m g = ${fmt(s.mu,2)} × ${s.n} × 0.350 × 9.8 = ${fmt(Ffric(),2)} N, so τ = Fr = ${fmt(Ffric(),2)} × 0.072 = ${fmt(torque(),2)} N m.`","no":"()=>`Not yet: τ = ${fmt(torque(),2)} N m. τ = μ n m g r, so add cans or make the tray rougher (μ).`","okIsFn":true,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"Double the drop to 1.60 m. By what factor does the landing speed rise, and the kinetic energy?","check":"()=>Math.abs(s.h-1.6)<1e-6","ok":"()=>`Yes: v = ${fmt(vFall(1.6),2)} m s⁻¹, ${fmt(vFall(1.6)/vFall(0.8),2)}× the 0.80 m speed (√2, not 2), while Ek doubles to ${fmt(ekFall(1.6),2)} J and the impact momentum is up ${fmt((vFall(1.6)/vFall(0.8)-1)*100)}%.`","no":"()=>`Set the drop to 1.60 m (twice 0.80 m), then check. v ∝ √h.`","okIsFn":true,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"Bring the running cost under 1 cent per can sold (keep the compressor duty at 50%).","check":"()=>s.duty===50&&costVend()<0.01","ok":"()=>`Yes: ${fmt(kWhDay(),2)} kWh a day costs $${fmt(costDay(),2)}; over ${s.vends} vends that is ${fmt(costVend()*100,2)} c each. The fridge runs whether or not anyone buys.`","no":"()=>s.duty!==50?\"Set the compressor duty to 50% first.\":`Not yet: $${fmt(costDay(),2)} a day ÷ ${s.vends} vends = ${fmt(costVend()*100,1)} c a can. You need more than ${Math.ceil(costDay()/0.01)} vends a day.`","okIsFn":true,"noIsFn":true}],"controls":[{"type":"play","id":"play","label":""},{"type":"button","id":"restart","label":"Restart"},{"type":"button","id":"vfront","label":"Front"},{"type":"button","id":"vside","label":"Side"},{"type":"button","id":"vinside","label":"Look inside"},{"type":"range","id":"explode","label":"Take apart"},{"type":"range","id":"h","label":"Drop height"},{"type":"range","id":"dt","label":"Stopping time"},{"type":"button","id":"rubber","label":"Rubber pad (0.050 s)"},{"type":"button","id":"steel","label":"Steel tray (0.010 s)"},{"type":"range","id":"n","label":"Cans in the coil"},{"type":"range","id":"mu","label":"Tray friction μ"},{"type":"range","id":"rpm","label":"Coil speed"},{"type":"range","id":"duty","label":"Compressor duty"},{"type":"range","id":"vends","label":"Cans sold a day"}],"shell":"kit"},"phone":{"id":"phone","title":"The phone machine","href":"m/phone.html","desc":"A 3 349 mAh phone battery on a 20 W charger: charge in coulombs, energy and average power, the \"50% in 30 minutes\" claim, why charging slows above 80%, internal resistance, loads in parallel, wireless induction and a charging capacitor.","blurb":"Coulombs, joules and watts in a phone battery, fast charging, internal resistance and capacitors.","tags":["NCEA L2 Physics 91173","NCEA L3 Physics 91526","Year 12","Year 13"],"built":true,"eyebrow":"Physics · Electricity and electrical systems","crumb":"NCEA Level 2 Physics AS91173 and Level 3 AS91526","badge":{"label":"NCEA L2 91173 · L3 91526","exam":"2026-11-18T14:00:00+13:00","examText":"L2 Wed 18 Nov · L3 Thu 12 Nov"},"papers":[{"label":"NZQA: Physics past exams and exemplars","url":"https://www2.nzqa.govt.nz/ncea/subjects/past-exams-and-exemplars/physics/"},{"label":"NZQA: AS91173 (L2 electricity)","url":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/ncea/assessment/view-detailed.do?standardNumber=91173"},{"label":"NZQA: AS91526 (L3 electrical systems)","url":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/ncea/assessment/view-detailed.do?standardNumber=91526"}],"truth":"Battery 3 349 mAh at 3.87 V, a 20 W charger and 5.0 h of screen time are the practice pack's figures. The cell model (emf 3.5–4.2 V, internal resistance 0.08 Ω, charger at 4.2 V), the screen and modem currents, the wireless-charging emf and efficiency, and the phone temperature are estimates that show the shape of the physics, not measurements of any real phone. Charging is sped up and the capacitor is slowed down so you can watch them.","footer":"Covers AS91173 (L2): charge, current, energy and power (Q = It, E = VQ, P = VI), DC circuits in parallel, and electromagnetic induction (Faraday and Lenz). AS91526 (L3): internal resistance (ε = V + Ir), capacitors (Q = CV, E = ½CV², τ = RC) and the charging curve.","mainLabel":"A phone on charge: a USB-C charger, a cable with moving charge, a battery bar on the screen and a readout card","kupu":[{"mi":"hiko","en":"electricity","ta":1171,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"wireless"},{"mi":"iahiko","en":"electric current","ta":1648,"note":"","chip":["Iavg","Inow","Itot","Ichg"],"evidence":"Te Reo Pūtaiao (MoE 2009) cited in entry","stepsNote":"charge, fast, ccv, circuit"},{"mi":"ngaohiko","en":"voltage","ta":40454,"note":"","chip":["Vterm","pd"],"evidence":"Te Reo Pūtaiao (MoE 2009) cited in entry","stepsNote":"energy, ccv, internal, wireless"},{"mi":"ara iahiko","en":"electric circuit","ta":343,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Reo Pūtaiao (MoE 2009) cited in entry","stepsNote":"circuit"},{"mi":"pūhiko","en":"battery, electrical cell","ta":40049,"note":"","chip":["mAh"],"evidence":"Te Reo Pūtaiao (RP 2009:333) cited in entry; Te Aka also lists the loanwords pāteri and pēteri","stepsNote":"charge"},{"mi":"whana hiko","en":"electric charge","ta":40518,"note":"","chip":["Q","Qcap"],"evidence":"Te Reo Pūtaiao (RP 2009:455) cited in entry","stepsNote":"charge, capacitor"},{"mi":"pūngao hiko","en":"electrical energy","ta":47713,"note":"","chip":["E","Ecap"],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"energy, fast, capacitor"},{"mi":"parenga","en":"resistance, resistor","ta":5300,"note":"Te Aka also lists parenga iahiko for a resistor.","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation); parenga iahiko 46887 is listed for a resistor","stepsNote":"ccv, internal, circuit, capacitor"}],"steps":[{"k":"charge","lab":"Charge stored","cap":"A modern phone battery is about 3 349 mAh at 3.87 V. Milliamp-hours are a charge: current × time. Turn it into coulombs.","exam":"Q = It = 3.349 A h × 3600 s = 12 056 C ≈ 1.21 × 10⁴ C. (3 349 mAh is 3.349 A for one hour, and one hour is 3600 s.)","trap":"Leaving mAh as mA (÷1000 to get amps) or hours as hours (×3600 to get seconds). Q must be in coulombs before it meets V.","big":"The big idea: charge is current × time, Q = It. A battery's mAh rating is a charge in disguise: 1 mAh = 3.6 C.","kupu":["whana hiko","pūhiko","iahiko"]},{"k":"energy","lab":"Energy and power","cap":"Energy stored is voltage × charge. If a full battery lasts exactly 5.0 hours of screen-on time, what is the average power draw?","exam":"E = VQ = 3.87 × 12 056 ≈ 4.67 × 10⁴ J. P = E ÷ t = 46 700 ÷ (5.0 × 3600) ≈ 2.6 W average. The average current is I = P ÷ V = 2.6 ÷ 3.87 ≈ 0.67 A.","trap":"Multiplying mAh by V and calling it joules. 3 349 mAh × 3.87 V = 12.96 Wh, which is 12.96 × 3600 = 46 700 J.","big":"The big idea: E = VQ (or E = VIt) and P = E ÷ t. A phone's whole day runs on a few watts.","kupu":["pūngao hiko","ngaohiko"]},{"k":"fast","lab":"\"50% in 30 min\"","cap":"A 20 W USB-C charger claims 50% in 30 minutes. Half the battery's energy ÷ the power actually reaching the cell = the time. Try other chargers and efficiencies.","exam":"Half of E is 23.3 kJ. At 20 W, t = E ÷ P = 23 300 ÷ 20 = 1167 s ≈ 19.4 min, so the claim is plausible only at ~100 % efficiency; at a realistic 85 % it takes about 23 min, still inside the \"about 30 minutes\" margin.","trap":"Using the charger's rated power as if all of it reached the battery. Heat in the cable, the converter and the cell takes 10–30 %.","big":"The big idea: t = E ÷ P, but only the power that reaches the cell counts. State your efficiency assumption; examiners reward it.","kupu":["pūngao hiko","iahiko"]},{"k":"ccv","lab":"Why it slows above 80%","cap":"Watch the current. Up to about 80% the charger pushes its maximum (constant current). Then the cell's emf gets close to the charger's voltage, the driving pd shrinks, and I = pd ÷ r falls (constant voltage).","exam":"Above about 80 % the battery management system switches from constant current to constant voltage. As the cell's emf rises toward the charger's voltage, the driving potential difference (V_charger − ε) falls, so the current I = (V_charger − ε) ÷ r falls, and the last 20 % takes much longer.","trap":"Saying the battery is 'full' at 80 %, or mixing up the cell's emf with the charger's voltage. The current depends on the difference between them.","big":"The big idea: current is driven by a potential difference across a resistance. As the cell's emf rises toward the charger's voltage, the difference falls, so the current falls: the flat tail of every charging curve.","kupu":["ngaohiko","iahiko","parenga"]},{"k":"internal","lab":"Internal resistance","cap":"Level 3. A cell has internal resistance r. When a game pulls 3 A, some volts are lost inside the cell (Ir) and I²r of heat warms the phone. Change the load and r.","exam":"ε = V + Ir, so the terminal voltage is V = ε − Ir = 3.87 − 3.0 × 0.08 = 3.63 V. The power lost inside the cell is I²r = 3.0² × 0.08 = 0.72 W, which heats the phone; the power delivered is VI = 3.63 × 3.0 = 10.9 W.","trap":"Using ε as the voltage across the load. Under load the terminal voltage is lower by Ir; at no load (I = 0) the terminal voltage equals ε.","big":"The big idea: ε = V + Ir. The terminal voltage drops under load; the lost volts become heat inside the cell. A graph of V against I has intercept ε and gradient −r.","kupu":["parenga","ngaohiko"]},{"k":"circuit","lab":"Loads in parallel","cap":"The screen and the modem sit in parallel across the battery: each gets the full 3.87 V and the currents add. Set each load's current and see the total, the power and the time to flat.","exam":"In parallel each load has the full 3.87 V across it, so the currents add: I = 1.2 + 0.5 = 1.7 A. P = VI = 3.87 × 1.7 = 6.6 W. The battery lasts about 3.349 A h ÷ 1.7 A ≈ 2.0 h. The combined resistance is V ÷ I = 2.3 Ω, less than either branch (3.2 Ω and 7.7 Ω).","trap":"Adding resistances in parallel as if they were in series. Add the currents (or the reciprocals of the resistances), not the resistances.","big":"The big idea: in parallel, every branch has the same voltage and the total current is the sum. The combined resistance is smaller than any branch: 1/R = 1/R₁ + 1/R₂.","kupu":["ara iahiko","iahiko","parenga"]},{"k":"wireless","lab":"Wireless charging","cap":"A Qi pad is a transformer with an air gap. Alternating current in the pad's coil makes a changing magnetic field; the phone's coil sits in it and an emf is induced. Turns, frequency and the gap all matter.","exam":"The alternating current in the pad coil produces a changing magnetic field. The flux through the phone's coil changes, so an emf is induced (ε = −N ΔΦ ÷ Δt, Faraday's law). Increasing the number of turns or the frequency increases the rate of change of flux linkage and so the emf; a larger gap reduces the flux and the efficiency.","trap":"Saying a steady field induces a voltage. Only a changing flux induces an emf; that is why the pad uses alternating current.","big":"The big idea: Faraday's law. An emf is induced only while the flux through the coil is changing: ε = −N ΔΦ ÷ Δt. More turns, faster change (higher frequency) or more flux (smaller gap) → bigger emf. Lenz's law gives the minus sign.","kupu":["hiko","ngaohiko"]},{"k":"capacitor","lab":"A capacitor charges","cap":"Level 3. The phone's power rail uses capacitors to hold the voltage steady. Through a resistor, a capacitor charges exponentially with time constant τ = RC. Change C and R, then press play.","exam":"τ = RC = 100 × 1000 × 10⁻⁶ = 0.10 s. The capacitor reaches 63 % of 3.87 V after 0.10 s and is effectively full after 5τ = 0.5 s. Charge stored Q = CV = 1000 × 10⁻⁶ × 3.87 = 3.87 × 10⁻³ C; energy E = ½CV² = 0.5 × 10⁻³ × 3.87² = 7.5 × 10⁻³ J.","trap":"Forgetting the 10⁻⁶ on microfarads, and using ½QV with Q in mAh. Convert first; then E = ½CV² = ½QV.","big":"The big idea: Q = CV, E = ½CV² and τ = RC. After one time constant the capacitor is at 63 % of the supply; after 5τ it is essentially full.","kupu":["whana hiko","pūngao hiko","parenga"]},{"k":"test","lab":"Test yourself","cap":"Five challenges, two of them Level 3. Use the controls from any step, then press Check.","exam":"","trap":"","big":"Exam tip: convert every unit first (mAh → A, h → s, μF → F), write the equation, then substitute. Say what you assumed about efficiency.","kupu":[]}],"challenges":[{"q":"Choose a battery that stores about 1.8 × 10⁴ C.","check":"()=>Math.abs(Q()-18000)<400","ok":"()=>`Yes: ${fmt(s.mAh)} mAh = ${fmt(s.mAh/1000,3)} A h × 3600 = ${fmt(Q())} C.`","no":"()=>`Not yet: ${fmt(s.mAh)} mAh stores ${fmt(Q())} C. Q = It, so you need 18 000 ÷ 3600 = 5 A h.`","okIsFn":true,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"With the 3 349 mAh battery, find a screen-on time that keeps the average power draw under 2 W.","check":"()=>s.mAh===3349&&Pavg()<2","ok":"()=>`Yes: ${fmt(E()/1000,1)} kJ over ${fmt(s.hours,1)} h is ${fmt(Pavg(),2)} W.`","no":"()=>s.mAh!==3349?\"Set the battery back to 3 349 mAh first.\":`Not yet: ${fmt(Pavg(),2)} W. P = E ÷ t, so t must be more than 46 700 ÷ 2 = 23 350 s ≈ 6.5 h.`","okIsFn":true,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"Set a charger and efficiency so that 50% takes between 25 and 30 minutes (3 349 mAh).","check":"()=>s.mAh===3349&&t50()>=1500&&t50()<=1800","ok":"()=>`Yes: ${fmt(0.5*E()/1000,1)} kJ ÷ (${fmt(s.Pchg)} W × ${Math.round(s.eff*100)}%) = ${fmt(t50()/60,1)} min.`","no":"()=>s.mAh!==3349?\"Set the battery back to 3 349 mAh first.\":`Not yet: ${fmt(t50()/60,1)} min. You need ${fmt(0.5*E()/1000,1)} kJ ÷ P_eff between 1500 and 1800 s, so P × efficiency between ${fmt(0.5*E()/1800,1)} and ${fmt(0.5*E()/1500,1)} W.`","okIsFn":true,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"Level 3: with a 3.0 A load, choose an internal resistance that keeps the lost volts under 0.10 V.","check":"()=>s.Iload===3&&s.Iload*s.r<0.1","ok":"()=>`Yes: Ir = 3.0 × ${fmt(s.r,2)} = ${fmt(3*s.r,2)} V, so the terminal voltage stays at ${fmt(Vterm(),2)} V and only ${fmt(Plost(),2)} W heats the cell.`","no":"()=>s.Iload!==3?\"Set the load to 3.0 A first.\":`Not yet: Ir = ${fmt(3*s.r,2)} V. You need r < 0.10 ÷ 3.0 = 0.033 Ω.`","okIsFn":true,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"Level 3: set the capacitor and resistor so the time constant is about 0.10 s.","check":"()=>Math.abs(tau()-0.1)<=0.01","ok":"()=>`Yes: τ = RC = ${fmt(s.R)} × ${sci(s.C*1e-6,2)} = ${fmt(tau(),3)} s. After 0.10 s the capacitor is at 63%.`","no":"()=>`Not yet: τ = ${fmt(tau()*1000,1)} ms. τ = RC with C in farads (${fmt(s.C)} μF = ${sci(s.C*1e-6,2)} F).`","okIsFn":true,"noIsFn":true}],"controls":[{"type":"play","id":"play","label":""},{"type":"button","id":"restart","label":"Restart"},{"type":"range","id":"mAh","label":"Battery"},{"type":"range","id":"hours","label":"Screen-on time"},{"type":"range","id":"Pchg","label":"Charger"},{"type":"range","id":"eff","label":"Efficiency"},{"type":"range","id":"r","label":"Internal resistance"},{"type":"range","id":"Iload","label":"Load current"},{"type":"range","id":"Iscreen","label":"Screen"},{"type":"range","id":"Imodem","label":"Modem"},{"type":"range","id":"N","label":"Coil turns"},{"type":"range","id":"f","label":"Frequency"},{"type":"range","id":"gap","label":"Gap"},{"type":"select","id":"C","label":"Capacitor"},{"type":"select","id":"R","label":"Resistor"}],"shell":"kit"},"queue":{"id":"queue","title":"The queue machine","href":"m/queue.html","desc":"Checkout and ED queues you can run: random arrivals, normally distributed service times, a live waiting-time distribution, relative risk of breaching the 6-hour target with 8 beds against 12, and a probability tree with its two-way table.","blurb":"Random arrivals, normal service times, a queue you can run, and relative risk in the ED.","tags":["NCEA L2 Maths 91267","Year 12"],"built":true,"eyebrow":"Maths and statistics · Probability","crumb":"NCEA Level 2 Mathematics and Statistics · AS91267 Apply probability methods in solving problems","badge":{"label":"NCEA L2 Maths 91267","exam":"2026-11-19T09:30:00+13:00","examText":"Thu 19 Nov, 9:30am"},"papers":[{"label":"NZQA: Mathematics and Statistics past exams","url":"https://www2.nzqa.govt.nz/ncea/subjects/past-exams-and-exemplars/mathematics-and-statistics/"},{"label":"NZQA: AS91267 standard, papers and schedules","url":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/ncea/assessment/view-detailed.do?standardNumber=91267"},{"label":"Lab: the ED twin","url":"https://assembl-lab.vercel.app/ed/"},{"label":"Lab: the supermarket twin","url":"https://assembl-lab.vercel.app/store/"}],"truth":"Arrivals are Poisson (random, steady average rate); checkout service times are normal with μ = 118 s, σ = 19 s (the practice pack's figures), clipped at 20 s; ED treatment times are a mix of simple cases (exponential, mean 30 min) and 12% complex cases (mean 120 min), about 41 minutes overall (an estimate chosen so that 8 beds at 14 an hour is overloaded and 12 copes). Queues are first come, first served; a patient still inside at the end of a run who has already passed 6 hours counts as a breach, and the rest of the unfinished are left out. A seed makes a run repeatable; change it for a new run. The lab's ED and supermarket twins are fuller models with real data behind them.","footer":"Covers AS91267 (L2 probability): normal distribution probabilities, experimental distributions (shape, centre, spread), probability trees, two-way tables and conditional probability, risk and relative risk. Poisson arrivals are shown as the model behind the simulation (formally AS91586 at Level 3).","mainLabel":"Shoppers arriving at checkouts, a normal curve of service times, two ED runs side by side, and a probability tree","kupu":[{"mi":"tūponotanga","en":"probability, chance","ta":46069,"note":"","chip":["p0","prob","phelp"],"evidence":"Te Reo Pāngarau (MoE 2010) cited in entry","stepsNote":"arrivals, service, ed, tree"},{"mi":"pāpono","en":"event (in probability)","ta":45828,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Reo Pāngarau (MoE 2010) cited in entry","stepsNote":"arrivals, tree"},{"mi":"putanga","en":"outcome","ta":6346,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Reo Pāngarau (MoE 2010) cited in entry","stepsNote":"tree"},{"mi":"tūtira","en":"queue, row, file","ta":8821,"note":"Te Aka also lists rārangi for a line or queue.","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (historical citations; no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"arrivals, wait"},{"mi":"toharite","en":"mean, average","ta":38680,"note":"Te Aka also lists toharite for the median; check which your class uses.","chip":["meanw"],"evidence":"Te Reo Pāngarau (TRP 2010:292) cited in entry","stepsNote":"service, wait"},{"mi":"ine mahora","en":"standard deviation","ta":49898,"note":"","chip":["sig"],"evidence":"Te Aka entry, maths sense (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"service"},{"mi":"tauanga","en":"statistics","ta":44587,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Reo Pāngarau (MoE 2010) cited in entry","stepsNote":"ed"},{"mi":"raraunga","en":"data","ta":6555,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"wait, ed"}],"steps":[{"k":"arrivals","lab":"Arrivals","cap":"Shoppers arrive at random, 1.6 a minute on average. Press play and count how many arrive in each 2-minute window. How often does nobody arrive at all?","exam":"λ = 1.6 × 2 = 3.2 for a 2-minute window, so P(0) = e^(−3.2) = 0.0408 ≈ 0.04. (Poisson is formally a Level 3 distribution; at Level 2, compare the observed distribution's shape, centre and spread with the model.)","trap":"Using λ for one minute in a two-minute question. Scale the rate to the window first.","big":"The big idea: random arrivals at a steady average rate follow a Poisson distribution, with λ = rate × window. The no-arrivals probability is e^(−λ).","kupu":["tūponotanga","pāpono","tūtira"]},{"k":"service","lab":"Service times","cap":"Checkout service times are normally distributed with μ = 118 s and σ = 19 s (the 2025 paper's style). Slide the bounds to shade a region and read the probability.","exam":"P(X > 100) = P(Z > (100 − 118) ÷ 19) = P(Z > −0.95) = 0.829. P(100 < X < 120) = P(−0.95 < Z < 0.11) = 0.829 − 0.456 = 0.373.","trap":"Forgetting that the tables give the area to the left. For P(Z > −0.95) you need 1 − Φ(−0.95), which equals Φ(0.95) by symmetry.","big":"The big idea: standardise with z = (x − μ) ÷ σ, then use the tables (or calculator) for the area. Draw the curve and shade it every time.","kupu":["toharite","ine mahora","tūponotanga"]},{"k":"wait","lab":"Waiting times","cap":"Now put them together. Arrivals at random, service times normal, a few checkouts open. Press play for a simulated hour and watch the queue; the close-up builds the distribution of waiting times.","exam":"With six checkouts and three arrivals a minute (one simulated hour, 180 shoppers), waits ran from 0 to 4.4 minutes with a median of 1.8 minutes and a middle half from 0.9 to 2.4 minutes. Opening a seventh checkout shifted the centre down to a median of 0.0 minutes (most shoppers were served at once) and narrowed the spread to a maximum of 2.3 minutes, with the distribution now strongly skewed to the right.","trap":"Comparing only the means. Say shape, centre AND spread, and note that a different random run gives slightly different numbers.","big":"The big idea: an experimental distribution. Describe it the way the standard asks: shape (usually skewed right), centre (median and mean), spread (IQR, range), and any unusual values, then compare two settings.","kupu":["tūtira","toharite","raraunga"]},{"k":"ed","lab":"The ED and relative risk","cap":"The pack's investigation: 14 arrivals an hour with 8 beds, then 12 beds. Run each for a day, save them as A and B, and compute the relative risk of breaching the 6-hour target. Your numbers will vary; that is the point.","exam":"With 8 beds, 6 of 271 patients breached 6 hours: risk 0.022. With 12 beds, 2 of 308 breached: risk 0.006. Relative risk = 0.022 ÷ 0.006 ≈ 3.4, so adding four beds cut the risk of a breach to about a third, which more than halves it. Both runs were 24 simulated hours, but the breach counts (6 and 2) are small, so another run could give a very different ratio; say so, and run longer before trusting it.","trap":"Reporting the difference in percentages as the relative risk, or quoting a ratio without the sample sizes behind it.","big":"The big idea: relative risk = risk in group A ÷ risk in group B. Say what it means in words, and give the sample sizes, because a ratio of small numbers is shaky.","kupu":["tūponotanga","raraunga","tauanga"]},{"k":"tree","lab":"Tree and table","cap":"Two stages at the checkout: which lane a shopper chooses, then whether they need help. Multiply along the branches, add across the leaves, and turn it into a two-way table.","exam":"P(help) = P(self) × P(help | self) + P(staffed) × P(help | staffed) = 0.4 × 0.3 + 0.6 × 0.1 = 0.12 + 0.06 = 0.18. P(self | help) = 0.12 ÷ 0.18 = 0.667: two-thirds of the shoppers who need help are at the self-checkouts.","trap":"Reading P(help | self) as P(self | help). The condition is the branch you are already on.","big":"The big idea: P(A and B) = P(A) × P(B | A). To get P(B) add every route to B. Conditional probability runs backwards: P(A | B) = P(A and B) ÷ P(B).","kupu":["tūponotanga","pāpono","putanga"]},{"k":"test","lab":"Test yourself","cap":"Five challenges. Use the controls from any step; two need a simulation run. Then press Check.","exam":"","trap":"","big":"Exam tip: write the probability statement first (P(X > 100)), then the working, then a sentence in context with the sample size when it comes from data.","kupu":[]}],"challenges":[{"q":"Set an arrival rate and window so the probability of no arrivals is about 0.05.","check":"()=>Math.abs(poisson(s.lam*s.win,0)-0.05)<0.006","ok":"()=>`Yes: λ = ${fmt(s.lam,1)} × ${s.win} = ${fmt(s.lam*s.win,2)}, so P(0) = e^(−${fmt(s.lam*s.win,2)}) = ${fmt(poisson(s.lam*s.win,0),3)}.`","no":"()=>`Not yet: P(0) = ${fmt(poisson(s.lam*s.win,0),3)}. You need λ = −ln 0.05 ≈ 3.0.`","okIsFn":true,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"Service times (μ = 118 s, σ = 19 s): shade the region for P(X > 150) and read it off.","check":"()=>s.mu===118&&s.sig===19&&s.lo===150&&s.hi>=s.mu+4*s.sig","ok":"()=>`Yes: z = (150 − 118) ÷ 19 = ${fmt((150-118)/19,2)}, P = 1 − Φ(${fmt((150-118)/19,2)}) = ${fmt(1-Phi((150-118)/19),3)}.`","no":"()=>s.mu!==118||s.sig!==19?\"Set μ = 118 and σ = 19 first.\":`Set the lower bound to 150 s and slide the upper bound all the way up. Now: ${fmt(s.lo)} to ${s.hi>=s.mu+4*s.sig?\"∞\":fmt(s.hi)}.`","okIsFn":true,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"Three arrivals a minute: open enough checkouts that the mean wait in a simulated hour is under 1 minute (run the hour, then check).","check":"()=>!!(s.lastRun&&Math.abs(s.lastRun.rate-3)<1e-9&&s.lastRun.mean<1)","ok":"()=>`Yes: ${s.lastRun.c} checkouts, mean wait ${fmt(s.lastRun.mean,2)} min over ${s.lastRun.n} shoppers. Each checkout is busy about ${Math.round(3*s.mu/60/s.lastRun.c*100)}% of the time.`","no":"()=>!s.lastRun?\"No finished hour yet. Set 3 arrivals a minute and press Run an hour (or play the Waiting times step to the end).\":Math.abs(s.lastRun.rate-3)>1e-9?\"Set the arrival rate to 3.0 a minute and run again.\":`Your run: ${s.lastRun.c} checkouts, mean wait ${fmt(s.lastRun.mean,2)} min. Each checkout can serve about ${fmt(60/s.mu*60,1)} an hour; 3 a minute is 180 an hour.`","okIsFn":true,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"ED: run 8 beds as A and 12 beds as B (14 an hour, at least 24 hours each) and find the relative risk of breaching 6 hours.","check":"()=>!!(s.runA&&s.runB&&s.runA.c===8&&s.runB.c===12&&s.runA.rate===14&&s.runB.rate===14&&s.runA.minutes>=1440&&s.runB.minutes>=1440)","ok":"()=>{const rr=s.runB.breach>0?s.runA.breach/s.runB.breach:Infinity;return `Yes: risk ${fmt(s.runA.breach,3)} (${s.runA.breaches} of ${s.runA.nT}) with 8 beds against ${fmt(s.runB.breach,3)} (${s.runB.breaches} of ${s.runB.nT}) with 12. RR = ${isFinite(rr)?fmt(rr,2):\"undefined (no breaches in B)\"}${isFinite(rr)?(rr>=2?\": more than halved.\":\": not halved.\"):\".\"}`}","no":"()=>`Not yet. Saved: A = ${s.runA?`${s.runA.c} beds, ${fmt(s.runA.rate)}/h, ${fmt(s.runA.minutes/60)} h`:\"nothing\"}; B = ${s.runB?`${s.runB.c} beds, ${fmt(s.runB.rate)}/h, ${fmt(s.runB.minutes/60)} h`:\"nothing\"}. Set 14 an hour, 24 hours, 8 beds → Run → Save as A; then 12 beds → Run → Save as B.`","okIsFn":true,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"Tree: set the three chances so that P(needs help) is exactly 0.25.","check":"()=>Math.abs(s.pSelf*s.pHelpSelf+(1-s.pSelf)*s.pHelpStaff-0.25)<0.005","ok":"()=>`Yes: ${fmt(s.pSelf,2)} × ${fmt(s.pHelpSelf,2)} + ${fmt(1-s.pSelf,2)} × ${fmt(s.pHelpStaff,2)} = ${fmt(s.pSelf*s.pHelpSelf+(1-s.pSelf)*s.pHelpStaff,3)}.`","no":"()=>`Not yet: P(help) = ${fmt(s.pSelf*s.pHelpSelf+(1-s.pSelf)*s.pHelpStaff,3)}. Multiply along each branch and add the two \"help\" leaves.`","okIsFn":true,"noIsFn":true}],"controls":[{"type":"play","id":"play","label":""},{"type":"button","id":"restart","label":"Restart"},{"type":"range","id":"lam","label":"Arrivals a minute"},{"type":"range","id":"win","label":"Window"},{"type":"range","id":"lo","label":"Lower bound"},{"type":"range","id":"hi","label":"Upper bound"},{"type":"range","id":"mu","label":"μ"},{"type":"range","id":"sig","label":"σ"},{"type":"range","id":"rate","label":"Arrivals a minute"},{"type":"range","id":"c","label":"Checkouts open"},{"type":"button","id":"runHour","label":"Run an hour"},{"type":"range","id":"edRate","label":"Arrivals an hour"},{"type":"range","id":"beds","label":"Beds"},{"type":"range","id":"edHours","label":"Run for"},{"type":"range","id":"seed","label":"Random seed"},{"type":"button","id":"runED","label":"Run the ED"},{"type":"button","id":"saveA","label":"Save as A"},{"type":"button","id":"saveB","label":"Save as B"},{"type":"range","id":"pSelf","label":"P(self-checkout)"},{"type":"range","id":"pHelpSelf","label":"P(help | self)"},{"type":"range","id":"pHelpStaff","label":"P(help | staffed)"}],"shell":"kit"},"plan":{"id":"plan","title":"The floor-plan studio","href":"m/plan.html","desc":"A DVC design brief: rearrange an ED or supermarket floor plan against fixed walls and measurable rules, save three distinct options, evaluate them, and draw the winner to scale with dimensions and a title block.","blurb":"Rearrange an ED floor plan against a brief, save three options, draw the winner to scale.","tags":["NCEA L2 DVC 91337","NCEA L3 DVC 91627","Year 12","Year 13"],"built":true,"eyebrow":"Design and visual communication · Spatial design","crumb":"NCEA DVC · AS91337 (Level 2) and AS91627 (Level 3) · externally assessed portfolios","badge":{"label":"NCEA DVC 91337 · 91627 (portfolio)"},"papers":[{"label":"NZQA: Design and Visual Communication","url":"https://www2.nzqa.govt.nz/ncea/subjects/past-exams-and-exemplars/design-and-visual-communication/"},{"label":"NZQA: AS91337 (L2, generate design ideas)","url":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/ncea/assessment/view-detailed.do?standardNumber=91337"},{"label":"NZQA: AS91627 (L3, initiate design ideas)","url":"https://www.nzqa.govt.nz/ncea/assessment/view-detailed.do?standardNumber=91627"},{"label":"Lab: the ED twin","url":"https://assembl-lab.vercel.app/ed/"},{"label":"Lab: the supermarket twin","url":"https://assembl-lab.vercel.app/store/"}],"truth":"A simplified spatial-design exercise. The sites (30 × 20 m ED, 36 × 24 m supermarket front end), the zone sizes and the six rules are estimates chosen to make the trade-offs visible, not a real hospital or store. DVC externals are portfolio submissions marked against the standards, not exams; the rules here are a stand-in for the measurable outcomes a real brief would set. The lab's ED and supermarket twins give the simulation evidence.","footer":"Covers the thinking the DVC externals assess: a brief with stakeholder, need, constraints and measures; divergent exploration and a range of distinct design ideas (AS91337, AS91627); evaluation against the brief; and visual communication (scale, dimensions, freehand versus instrumental drawing).","mainLabel":"A top-down floor plan with movable zones, fixed doors and a panel of rule lights","kupu":[{"mi":"hoahoa","en":"to design, plan; a plan of a building","ta":1290,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"brief, explore, communicate"},{"mi":"kaihoahoa","en":"designer, architect","ta":1924,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"brief"},{"mi":"mahere","en":"map, plan, chart","ta":3350,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation); already used in the map machine","stepsNote":"explore, communicate"},{"mi":"āwhata","en":"scale (of a map or drawing)","ta":15342,"note":"","chip":["scaleTxt"],"evidence":"Te Aka entry; already used in the map machine","stepsNote":"communicate"},{"mi":"horahanga","en":"area","ta":45667,"note":"","chip":["area"],"evidence":"Te Reo Pāngarau (MoE 2010) cited in entry; already used in the shape workshop","stepsNote":"explore, evaluate"},{"mi":"tauira","en":"example, pattern, model","ta":7706,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"ideate"},{"mi":"kiritaki","en":"customer, client","ta":11034,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation); already used in the market machine","stepsNote":"brief, evaluate"},{"mi":"āhuatanga hoahoa","en":"design element","ta":38908,"note":"","chip":[],"evidence":"Te Aka entry (no curriculum citation)","stepsNote":"ideate"}],"steps":[{"k":"brief","lab":"The brief","cap":"A DVC project starts with a brief: who it's for, what they need, what can't change, and how you'll know it worked. Read it, then choose the ED or the supermarket.","exam":"The brief identifies the stakeholder (Health NZ, judged against the 95%-within-6-hours target), the need (clear the waiting room without adding beds), the constraints (existing external walls and doors; minimum zone sizes; triage within 8 m of the entrance) and the evidence that will show success (before-and-after simulation runs).","trap":"A brief with no measurable outcome. 'Make the ED flow better' can't be evaluated; '95 % within 6 hours in the twin' can.","big":"The big idea: a brief is the contract you design against. Everything you draw later is judged by it, so write the constraints and the measure of success down first.","kupu":["hoahoa","kiritaki","kaihoahoa"]},{"k":"explore","lab":"Explore the layout","cap":"Drag the zones (or pick one and use the Nudge buttons). Dashed red means a zone breaks a rule. Move triage forward, split the fast-track flow, re-site the bays: watch the rule lights.","exam":"Moving triage to within 8 m of the entrance means arriving patients are assessed before they settle into the waiting room, and placing fast-track beside the waiting room lets low-acuity patients be streamed off without crossing the department. Resus stays beside the ambulance door because seconds matter there.","trap":"Fixing one rule and breaking two others. Check all the lights after every move; a layout is a set of relationships, not a list of rooms.","big":"The big idea: exploration is divergent: try many arrangements quickly, and let the constraints tell you which ideas have legs. Adjacency (what touches what) and distance (how far people walk) drive flow.","kupu":["hoahoa","mahere","horahanga"]},{"k":"ideate","lab":"Three options","cap":"Generate design ideas: arrange the zones one way and Save as A, then change the big moves (where triage goes, where fast-track sits) and save B and C. Different ideas, not three versions of one.","exam":"Option A moves triage next to the entrance and keeps the bays central; Option B splits a fast-track corridor along the north wall so minor cases never enter the main bays; Option C re-sites the waiting room beside reception to shorten the walk. Each was tested against the same six rules.","trap":"Three options that differ only in detail. If the thumbnails look alike, the examiner sees refinement, not ideation.","big":"The big idea: AS91337 and AS91627 reward a range of distinct ideas and the thinking between them. Thumbnails of A, B and C side by side are the visual communication the standards ask for.","kupu":["tauira","āhuatanga hoahoa"]},{"k":"evaluate","lab":"Evaluate","cap":"Score the current layout against the brief: rules met, walking distance, area per person. Then take it to the lab's twin, run the before and after, and screenshot the result as evidence.","exam":"Against the brief, Option B meets six of six rules, shortens the entrance-to-bays walk from 31 m to 22 m and gives 2.0 m² per waiting patient. In the ED twin, the same change lifted patients seen within 6 hours from 71 % to 84 % with no extra beds, so it best meets the stakeholder's need.","trap":"Evaluating against what you like instead of what the brief said. Quote the brief's measure and the number you got.","big":"The big idea: evaluation needs evidence against the brief's measure, not opinion. Numbers from the rules here, and simulation runs from the twin, make the judgement defensible.","kupu":["kiritaki","horahanga"]},{"k":"communicate","lab":"Draw it to scale","cap":"Visual communication: the plan at 1:100 with dimensions in millimetres, a title block, and the difference between a freehand ideation sketch and an instrumental drawing. Select a zone to dimension it.","exam":"The instrumental plan is drawn at 1:100, so the 30 m × 20 m site is 300 mm × 200 mm on the sheet. Dimensions are given in millimetres (triage 3000 × 3000) with extension lines clear of the outline, and the title block records the project, the option and the scale.","trap":"Mixing units on one drawing, or drawing 'roughly to scale'. Pick 1:100 or 1:50 and keep every dimension honest to it.","big":"The big idea: scale = drawing size ÷ real size. At 1:100, one metre is 10 mm on paper. Dimensions go in millimetres, outside the outline, with extension lines, and the title block says what, which option, what scale.","kupu":["mahere","āwhata","hoahoa"]},{"k":"test","lab":"Test yourself","cap":"Five challenges on the ED brief. Use the controls from any step, then press Check.","exam":"","trap":"","big":"Portfolio tip: show the thinking, not just the answer: the brief, three distinct ideas, the rules each one meets, the drawing to scale, and the evidence that it works.","kupu":[]}],"challenges":[{"q":"ED brief: move triage so its centre is within 8 m of the walk-in entrance.","check":"()=>s.brief===\"ed\"&&evalZones(s.zones).triage","ok":"()=>`Yes: triage is ${fmt(evalZones(s.zones).triageD,1)} m from the entrance. Patients are assessed before they sit down.`","no":"()=>s.brief!==\"ed\"?\"Choose the ED brief first.\":`Not yet: ${fmt(evalZones(s.zones).triageD,1)} m. Drag triage toward the entrance on the left wall.`","okIsFn":true,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"Keep every zone inside the walls with no overlaps.","check":"()=>{const r=evalZones(s.zones);return r.inside&&r.nooverlap}","ok":"Yes: all inside, nothing overlapping. The dashed red outlines have gone.","no":"()=>{const r=evalZones(s.zones);return `Not yet: ${r.inside?\"\":\"a zone pokes outside the walls. \"}${r.overlaps?r.overlaps+\" overlap\"+(r.overlaps>1?\"s\":\"\")+\" to fix.\":\"\"}`}","okIsFn":false,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"ED brief: put fast-track beside the waiting room (gap ≤ 1 m) while keeping resus within 6 m of the ambulance door.","check":"()=>{const r=evalZones(s.zones);return s.brief===\"ed\"&&r.fastadj&&r.resus}","ok":"()=>`Yes: fast-track gap ${fmt(Math.max(0,evalZones(s.zones).fastGap),1)} m, resus ${fmt(evalZones(s.zones).resusD,1)} m from the ambulance door.`","no":"()=>{const r=evalZones(s.zones);return s.brief!==\"ed\"?\"Choose the ED brief first.\":`Not yet: fast-track gap ${fmt(Math.max(0,r.fastGap),1)} m (need ≤ 1), resus ${fmt(r.resusD,1)} m from the ambulance door (need ≤ 6).`}","okIsFn":true,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"Save three clearly different ED options (A, B and C), each with triage in a different place.","check":"()=>{const o=s.options;if(!(o.A&&o.B&&o.C))return false;if([o.A,o.B,o.C].some(x=>x.brief!==\"ed\"))return false;const t=k=>o[k].zones.triage;return dist([t(\"A\").x,t(\"A\").y],[t(\"B\").x,t(\"B\").y])>=3&&dist([t(\"A\").x,t(\"A\").y],[t(\"C\").x,t(\"C\").y])>=3&&dist([t(\"B\").x,t(\"B\").y],[t(\"C\").x,t(\"C\").y])>=3}","ok":"()=>`Yes: three options with triage at (${[\"A\",\"B\",\"C\"].map(k=>`${fmt(s.options[k].zones.triage.x,1)}, ${fmt(s.options[k].zones.triage.y,1)}`).join(\"), (\")}). Rules met: ${[\"A\",\"B\",\"C\"].map(k=>s.options[k].r.pass).join(\", \")} of ${B().rules.length}.`","no":"()=>{const o=s.options;return !(o.A&&o.B&&o.C)?`Saved so far: ${[\"A\",\"B\",\"C\"].filter(k=>o[k]).join(\", \")||\"none\"}. Arrange, Save as A; rearrange, Save as B; again for C.`:\"All three are saved, but triage sits in nearly the same place in two of them (move it at least 3 m).\"}","okIsFn":true,"noIsFn":true},{"q":"ED brief: give the waiting room at least 1.5 m² per expected waiting patient (20 patients).","check":"()=>s.brief===\"ed\"&&evalZones(s.zones).area","ok":"()=>`Yes: ${fmt(evalZones(s.zones).areaV)} m² ÷ 20 = ${fmt(evalZones(s.zones).areaV/20,2)} m² each.`","no":"()=>s.brief!==\"ed\"?\"Choose the ED brief first.\":`Not yet: ${fmt(evalZones(s.zones).areaV)} m² is ${fmt(evalZones(s.zones).areaV/20,2)} m² each. You need ≥ 30 m²: select the waiting room and make it wider or taller.`","okIsFn":true,"noIsFn":true}],"controls":[{"type":"select","id":"brief","label":"Brief"},{"type":"select","id":"zone","label":"Zone"},{"type":"button","id":"left","label":"← 0.5 m"},{"type":"button","id":"right","label":"→ 0.5 m"},{"type":"button","id":"up","label":"↑ 0.5 m"},{"type":"button","id":"down","label":"↓ 0.5 m"},{"type":"button","id":"wider","label":"Wider"},{"type":"button","id":"narrower","label":"Narrower"},{"type":"button","id":"taller","label":"Taller"},{"type":"button","id":"shorter","label":"Shorter"},{"type":"button","id":"reset","label":"Back to the start"},{"type":"button","id":"saveA","label":"Save as A"},{"type":"button","id":"saveB","label":"Save as B"},{"type":"button","id":"saveC","label":"Save as C"},{"type":"select","id":"scale","label":"Scale"},{"type":"button","id":"copy","label":"Copy the design notes"}],"shell":"kit"}},"notFound":["momentum, torque, angular velocity, centripetal (vending, downhill, vinyl): no curriculum-cited entry; torohaki/ānga are general words for momentum.","capacitor (phone): two differing Te Aka entries (pūnga iahiko, pūranga iahiko), neither curriculum-cited; left for the teacher.","normal distribution, relative risk (queue): no entry found.","floor plan, scale drawing, brief, stakeholder (floor-plan studio): no entry as a compound; hoahoa covers a building plan.","artificial intelligence: Te Aka has no entry (only *atamai*, intelligent; *waea atamai*, smartphone). Phrases like \"atamai horihori\" are in use elsewhere but were not verified, so none was added. Treat this as newer and contested vocabulary.","lever (fish): Te Aka has verbs for prising or levering, but no noun for a lever. *kauwhiti* appears inside an example sentence but its own entry means something else, so it was not used.","range (statistics), amplitude (Te Aka's two entries conflict), wavelength (only \"(radio) wavelength\"), contour line (Te Aka's *rārangi hua* comes from visual arts), mutation, allele, variation, personification, hyperbole, demand, opportunity cost, trapezium, prism, values (as beliefs): no suitable verified entry found, so none was added.","toharite: Te Reo Pāngarau uses it for *mean*, but Te Aka also records it used for *median* (from Te Ara). The fish machine says so and gives *tau waenga* for median.","iramoe: Te Aka lists it as both *neutron* (Te Reo Pūtaiao) and *recessive gene*. It is used only in atoms, with a note, and left out of genes."]};